-- [building-a-predictive-edge] anatomy-of-predictive-edge
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<h2>What "Edge" Actually Means</h2><p>In sports betting, an <strong>edge</strong> is the measurable advantage you have over the market. It exists when your estimate of an outcome''s true probability is consistently more accurate than the probability implied by the odds being offered. An edge is not based on confidence or luck. It is based on evidence that your predictions are more accurate over a large number of events.</p><p>Many people believe they have an edge after a few successful bets. In reality, a short winning streak can happen purely by chance. A genuine edge only becomes visible after hundreds or even thousands of observations, where results consistently outperform what would be expected from random variation.</p><h3>What Edge Is Not</h3><ul><li>A good tip from a friend or social media.</li><li>A gut feeling about a team or player.</li><li>A strategy that happened to work for a few weeks.</li><li>A single correct prediction.</li></ul><p>None of these demonstrate a lasting advantage. True edge must be measurable, repeatable, and supported by long-term evidence.</p><h2>The Three Types of Edge</h2><h3>1. Information Edge</h3><p>An information edge exists when you possess reliable information that the market has not yet incorporated into the odds. This is the rarest form of edge because modern betting markets react quickly to new information.</p><p>For example, if verified news about a key player''s injury becomes available before bookmakers and the wider market adjust their prices, that information may temporarily create an advantage. Once the market updates the odds, the edge usually disappears.</p><p>Because betting markets are highly competitive, lasting information advantages are uncommon and difficult to maintain.</p><h3>2. Processing Edge</h3><p>A processing edge means you have access to the same public information as everyone else but analyze it more effectively. Rather than relying on opinions, you use stronger methods to estimate probabilities.</p><p>This may involve using statistical models, evaluating historical performance, considering contextual factors such as scheduling or fatigue, and testing whether your predictions remain accurate over time.</p><p>For example, two analysts may study the same match. One focuses only on league position, while the other combines expected goals, player availability, tactical matchups, and home advantage into a validated model. The second analyst has a better chance of producing more accurate probability estimates.</p><p>Processing edge is the most realistic type of edge for serious analysts because it depends on improving analytical quality instead of finding secret information.</p><h3>3. Execution Edge</h3><p>Execution edge is gained by consistently obtaining better prices than the average bettor. It does not create an edge by itself, but it increases the value of an existing one.</p><p>Execution improvements include comparing odds across multiple bookmakers, placing bets before favorable prices disappear, and managing accounts efficiently to maintain access to competitive markets.</p><p>Even a small improvement in price can significantly affect long-term results because better odds increase the expected value of correctly identified opportunities.</p><h2>Why Most Bettors Have No Edge</h2><p>Most bettors do not possess any of the three forms of edge. They typically rely on publicly available information, make subjective predictions without testing their accuracy, and use only one bookmaker offering standard market prices.</p><ul><li>No information edge because everyone has access to the same news.</li><li>No processing edge because probabilities are based on opinion rather than validated analysis.</li><li>No execution edge because prices are not compared across multiple sportsbooks.</li></ul><p>When these factors are combined, the bettor is usually competing against a market that is more efficient and better informed. Over time, this often results in losses rather than consistent profitability.</p><h2>Building Edge Is a Long-Term Project</h2><p>Developing an edge is not about discovering one clever idea or making one successful prediction. It is a continuous process of collecting data, improving probability estimates, testing results, and refining methods.</p><p>A serious analyst records predictions, compares estimated probabilities with actual outcomes, measures forecasting accuracy, and evaluates performance on data that was not used to build the model. This process, known as out-of-sample validation, helps determine whether an apparent edge is genuine or simply the result of random chance.</p><p>For example, if a model predicts that certain outcomes have a 60% chance of occurring, those outcomes should occur close to 60% of the time over a large enough sample. If they do not, the model needs improvement.</p><p>Building a genuine edge often takes months or years of disciplined work. It requires patience, continuous learning, and a willingness to challenge your own assumptions with evidence.</p><h2>Real-Data Benchmark: PunterStat v2.0 Model Calibration vs Market</h2>
<p>On PunterStat''s held-out test set of <strong>27,830 matches (2024/25–2026/27)</strong>, comparing pure statistical models against de-vigged Pinnacle closing odds illustrates why finding genuine +EV requires precision:</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Probability Source</th><th>1X2 Brier Score (Lower = Better)</th><th>Log-Loss</th><th>Ranked Probability Score (RPS)</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Pinnacle Closing Odds (De-vigged)</td><td><strong>0.59811</strong></td><td><strong>1.00018</strong></td><td><strong>0.20301</strong></td></tr>
    <tr><td>PunterStat v2.0 LGBM + Market Blend</td><td>0.59858</td><td>1.00130</td><td>0.20384</td></tr>
    <tr><td>PunterStat v2.0 Dixon-Coles + LGBM Hybrid (Pure Stats)</td><td>0.60868</td><td>1.01607</td><td>0.20869</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Worked +EV Example in Naira:</strong> Suppose your de-vigged model prices an Over 2.5 Goals selection at <strong>52.0% true probability</strong> (fair odds <code>1.92</code>), and a bookmaker offers <code>2.10</code>. On a <strong>₦5,000 stake</strong>: <code>EV = (0.52 &times; ₦5,500) &minus; (0.48 &times; ₦5,000) = ₦2,860 &minus; ₦2,400 = +₦460</code> (<strong>+9.2% ROI</strong>). Over 1 bet, you still lose 48% of the time; over 500 similar +EV bets, expected profit compounds to <strong>+₦230,000</strong>.</p>

<h2>Key Takeaway</h2><p>An edge is a measurable and repeatable ability to estimate probabilities more accurately than the betting market. It is not based on intuition, short-term success, or isolated insights. Genuine edge comes from superior information, better analysis, improved execution, or a combination of these factors. Developing such an advantage is a long-term analytical process that requires testing, validation, and consistent refinement.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'anatomy-of-predictive-edge'
  AND course_id = (SELECT id FROM public.courses WHERE slug = 'building-a-predictive-edge');

-- [implied-probability-explained] from-odds-to-probability
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<h2>The most important formula in sports analysis</h2>
<p>Every bookmaker price makes a claim about the world. It says: "we believe this outcome happens with approximately X% probability." The implied probability formula decodes that claim in one step:</p>
<p><strong>Implied Probability = 1 ÷ Decimal Odds</strong></p>
<p>At 2.10: 1 ÷ 2.10 = 0.476 = 47.6%. The bookmaker is pricing this outcome as a 47.6% chance. At 3.40: 1 ÷ 3.40 = 29.4%. At 3.60: 27.8%.</p>
<p>Once you have these percentages, you have something concrete to argue with. Your analysis either agrees with the bookmaker''s implied probability, or it does not. If it does not, you have the foundation of a value bet.</p>

<h2>A full worked example: reading a match market</h2>
<p>Take a typical Premier League fixture with these prices from our match_odds dataset (sourced from Bet365 and Pinnacle):</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Home win:</strong> 2.10 → 1 ÷ 2.10 = 47.6%</li>
<li><strong>Draw:</strong> 3.40 → 1 ÷ 3.40 = 29.4%</li>
<li><strong>Away win:</strong> 3.60 → 1 ÷ 3.60 = 27.8%</li>
<li><strong>Sum: 104.8%</strong></li>
</ul>
<p>The total exceeds 100% by 4.8 percentage points. That excess is the overround — the bookmaker''s margin. The prices do not represent the bookmaker''s true probability estimate; they represent true probability minus margin.</p>
<p>To estimate the market''s <em>true</em> probability for each outcome (after removing the margin), divide each implied probability by the total:</p>
<ul>
<li>True P(Home) = 47.6 ÷ 104.8 = 45.4%</li>
<li>True P(Draw) = 29.4 ÷ 104.8 = 28.1%</li>
<li>True P(Away) = 27.8 ÷ 104.8 = 26.5%</li>
<li>Sum: 100.0% ✓</li>
</ul>
<p>This de-vigging process gives you the market consensus estimate stripped of commercial margin — a starting point for your own assessment.</p>

<h2>What "value" means in this framework</h2>
<p>A value bet exists when your estimated probability for an outcome exceeds the implied probability embedded in the odds — after removing the margin.</p>
<p>If your analysis suggests the away team has a 32% chance of winning (not the market''s 26.5%), and the price is 3.60 (implying 27.8% before margin), you believe the true probability is approximately 5 percentage points higher than the market''s estimate. Whether you are correct will only be determined over hundreds of similar bets — but the identification process begins with this comparison.</p>

<h2>How odds from our dataset illustrate probability</h2>
<p>Our match_odds table contains pre-match odds from multiple bookmakers for every historical match. Comparing the implied probabilities from different bookmakers reveals market uncertainty: when Bet365 prices a home win at 2.10 (47.6%) and Pinnacle prices it at 2.20 (45.5%), the two-percentage-point difference reflects different probability models. Neither is definitively correct — the closing line, after all money has moved, is the best estimate available.</p>
<p>Matches where bookmakers disagree significantly on implied probability are the ones most likely to contain pricing errors — in one direction or the other. This disagreement shows up as wider spreads between bookmaker prices for the same outcome.</p>

<h2>Implied probability across different bet types</h2>
<p>The same formula applies to any betting market, not just match results:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Asian handicap:</strong> Both sides of a −0.5 handicap should each be near 50% after removing margin. Deviations from 50/50 reflect the market''s assessment of how much stronger one team is.</li>
<li><strong>Over/under totals:</strong> Over 2.5 at 2.00 implies exactly 50%. If you believe a match has a higher than 50% chance of producing three or more goals, the over offers value.</li>
<li><strong>Player props:</strong> Anytime scorer at 3.50 implies 28.6%. Your assessment of that player''s minutes, form, and role should produce a probability estimate you can compare directly.</li>
</ul>

<h2>The habit to build</h2>
<p>Every time you look at a price, immediately convert it to a probability. Do not evaluate a price as "short" or "generous" in isolation — evaluate it as a probability claim and ask whether that claim is accurate. This single habit separates analytical bettors from casual ones. Thinking in probabilities replaces gut feel with a framework that can be tested, tracked, and improved over time.</p>

<h2>Real-Data Benchmark: PunterStat v2.0 Model Calibration vs Market</h2>
<p>On PunterStat''s held-out test set of <strong>27,830 matches (2024/25–2026/27)</strong>, comparing pure statistical models against de-vigged Pinnacle closing odds illustrates why finding genuine +EV requires precision:</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Probability Source</th><th>1X2 Brier Score (Lower = Better)</th><th>Log-Loss</th><th>Ranked Probability Score (RPS)</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Pinnacle Closing Odds (De-vigged)</td><td><strong>0.59811</strong></td><td><strong>1.00018</strong></td><td><strong>0.20301</strong></td></tr>
    <tr><td>PunterStat v2.0 LGBM + Market Blend</td><td>0.59858</td><td>1.00130</td><td>0.20384</td></tr>
    <tr><td>PunterStat v2.0 Dixon-Coles + LGBM Hybrid (Pure Stats)</td><td>0.60868</td><td>1.01607</td><td>0.20869</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Worked +EV Example in Naira:</strong> Suppose your de-vigged model prices an Over 2.5 Goals selection at <strong>52.0% true probability</strong> (fair odds <code>1.92</code>), and a bookmaker offers <code>2.10</code>. On a <strong>₦5,000 stake</strong>: <code>EV = (0.52 &times; ₦5,500) &minus; (0.48 &times; ₦5,000) = ₦2,860 &minus; ₦2,400 = +₦460</code> (<strong>+9.2% ROI</strong>). Over 1 bet, you still lose 48% of the time; over 500 similar +EV bets, expected profit compounds to <strong>+₦230,000</strong>.</p>

<h2>Key Takeaway</h2>
<p>The implied probability formula (1 ÷ decimal odds) converts every bookmaker price into a testable claim. Comparing that claim to your own probability estimate is the entire basis of finding value — everything else in betting analytics is built on this foundation.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'from-odds-to-probability'
  AND course_id = (SELECT id FROM public.courses WHERE slug = 'implied-probability-explained');

-- [singles-doubles-accumulators] what-is-a-single-bet
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<h2><h1>A Single Bet: The Foundation of Sports Wagering</h1><h2>Core Definition</h2><p>A single bet (also called a "single") is the simplest and most fundamental type of sports wager. You place money on one selection for one outcome of one event. If that outcome occurs, the bet wins; if not, it loses.</p><ul><li>One event (e.g., a football match, horse race, tennis match, or basketball game).</li><li>One market/outcome (e.g., match winner, over/under goals, player to score, or horse to win).</li><li>One stake amount.</li><li>Payout depends only on whether that single selection is correct.</li></ul><p>There are no combined odds or multiple legs. The bet stands or falls on that one result alone. This makes singles the easiest entry point for beginners and a preferred option for many experienced bettors who want lower risk and clearer decision-making.</p><hr><h2>How a Single Bet Works</h2><ol><li>Choose an event and market.</li><li>Select one outcome and note the odds offered by the bookmaker.</li><li>Enter your stake (the amount you are risking).</li><li>Wait for the event to finish and settle.</li></ol><p><strong>Payout calculation (decimal odds, the most common international format):</strong></p><p>Total return = Stake × Odds</p><p>Profit = Total return − Stake</p><p><strong>Fractional odds example (common in the UK):</strong> Odds of 5/1 mean you win 5 units profit for every 1 unit staked, plus your stake returned.</p><hr><h2>Clear Examples</h2><p><strong>Football – Match Winner (decimal odds)</strong></p><p>Stake: ₦10 on Team A to win at odds of 2.50.</p><p>If Team A wins: Return = ₦10 × 2.50 = ₦25 (profit ₦15).</p><p>If Team A draws or loses: You lose the ₦10 stake.</p><p><strong>Football – Player Prop</strong></p><p>Stake: ₦20 on a player to be booked at odds of 5.00.</p><p>If the player is booked: Return = ₦100 (profit ₦80).</p><p>Otherwise: Stake is lost.</p><p><strong>Horse Racing – Win Single</strong></p><p>Stake: ₦10 on a horse at 2/1.</p><p>If the horse wins: Return = ₦30 (₦20 profit + ₦10 stake).</p><p>If not: Stake lost.</p><p><strong>Each-way single (common in racing):</strong> You effectively place two bets of equal stake—one to win and one to place (finish in the top positions according to the bookmaker''s terms). The place portion pays at a fraction of the win odds (often 1/4 or 1/5). Total stake is therefore double a straight win single.</p><hr><h2>Singles Across Markets and Sports</h2><p>Singles are not limited to "who wins." Common markets include:</p><ul><li>Match/race winner (moneyline)</li><li>Handicap / spread</li><li>Totals (over/under points or goals)</li><li>Correct score</li><li>Player props (goals, shots, cards, yards, etc.)</li><li>Outrights (tournament or season winners)</li></ul><p>They work in football, horse racing, tennis, basketball, cricket, American football, and virtually any sport or event with fixed odds.</p><hr><h2>Singles vs Accumulators (Parlays / Multiples)</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Single Bet</th><th>Accumulator (Acca)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Selections</td><td>1</td><td>2 or more</td></tr><tr><td>Requirement to win</td><td>Only that one selection correct</td><td>All selections must be correct</td></tr><tr><td>Odds</td><td>The single price offered</td><td>Odds of each leg multiplied together</td></tr><tr><td>Risk level</td><td>Lower</td><td>Higher (one loss kills the whole bet)</td></tr><tr><td>Potential return</td><td>Modest</td><td>Can be much larger</td></tr><tr><td>Ease of tracking</td><td>Simple</td><td>More complex</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Singles give you independent results—you can win some and lose others. An accumulator is all-or-nothing.</p><hr><h2>Advantages of Single Bets</h2><ul><li>Easiest to understand and place.</li><li>Lower risk than multiples.</li><li>Easier to manage bankroll and track results.</li><li>Allows focused analysis on one probability/value opportunity.</li><li>Ideal for beginners and for building disciplined, long-term strategies.</li><li>Settles quickly (as soon as that one event ends).</li></ul><hr><h2>Potential Drawbacks</h2><ul><li>Lower potential payouts compared with successful accumulators.</li><li>Requires consistent value-finding over many bets to profit long-term (bookmakers build in a margin).</li><li>Less "excitement" for some recreational bettors who prefer big multi-leg payouts.</li></ul><hr><h2>Practical Tips for Using Single Bets</h2><ul><li>Focus on finding value: compare the bookmaker''s odds with your estimated true probability.</li><li>Start with markets you understand well.</li><li>Use a staking plan (e.g., fixed percentage of your bankroll) rather than chasing losses.</li><li>Keep records of selections, odds, stakes, and results.</li><li>Remember that even strong favourites lose—treat every single as an independent decision.</li><li>Be aware of voids (event cancelled, player does not participate in certain props) and rare dead-heats (stake usually returned or paid at reduced odds).</li></ul><hr><h2>Key Takeaway</h2><p>A single bet is pure simplicity: one selection, one outcome, stake × odds if correct. It forms the foundation of almost all other bet types and remains one of the smartest ways to approach sports betting for clarity, control, and sustainable decision-making.</p><p>Always gamble responsibly. Only bet what you can afford to lose, and treat betting as entertainment rather than a guaranteed way to make money.</p></h2>'
WHERE slug = 'what-is-a-single-bet'
  AND course_id = (SELECT id FROM public.courses WHERE slug = 'singles-doubles-accumulators');

-- [regression-to-the-mean] why-hot-and-cold-streaks-end
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<h2>What Regression to the Mean Is</h2>
<p>Regression to the mean is the statistical tendency for extreme measurements to be followed by more moderate ones on subsequent measurement. It was first described by Francis Galton in the 19th century: the children of very tall parents tend to be tall, but not as tall as their parents.</p>
<p>In sport, it appears everywhere. The team that concedes five goals in one match is unlikely to concede five in the next. The striker who scores in seven consecutive matches is unlikely to maintain that rate indefinitely. Extreme performances contain an element of luck, and luck does not persist.</p>
<h2>The Betting Opportunity</h2>
<p>Markets often fail to discount regression correctly. After a team scores eight goals in one game, the over market in their next game is overpriced — the crowd overweights what just happened. After a goalkeeper makes a sequence of error-strewn performances, their team''s odds shorten more than the true probability warrants.</p>
<p>The value lies in fading (opposing) the extreme. Not always — sometimes the extreme reflects a genuine step change in quality. But systematically, markets overreact to the exceptional.</p>
<h2>How to Apply It</h2>
<p>Ask: is this extreme result more likely to represent a genuine permanent shift, or a temporary spike? Indicators that it was temporary:</p>
<ul>
<li>The extreme result was against unusual opposition quality</li>
<li>The underlying performance metrics (expected goals, possession) were more moderate</li>
<li>The key performer who drove the extreme is known to have high variance output</li>
</ul>

<h2>Real-Data Benchmark: Sample Size, Variance &amp; Walk-Forward Stability</h2>
<p>Short-term win rates fool almost every bettor. Even on a fair 50/50 proposition (<code>2.00</code> odds), the 95% confidence interval around your observed win rate shrinks only with the <em>square root</em> of sample size (<code>&radic;n</code>):</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Settled Bets (n)</th><th>95% Win-Rate Confidence Band (at True 50%)</th><th>95% ROI Noise Band at 2.00 Odds</th><th>Statistical Verdict</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>25 bets</td><td>30.4% – 69.6%</td><td>&minus;39.2% to +39.2%</td><td>Pure noise — impossible to judge skill</td></tr>
    <tr><td>100 bets</td><td>40.2% – 59.8%</td><td>&minus;19.6% to +19.6%</td><td>Early directional signal only</td></tr>
    <tr><td>500 bets</td><td>45.6% – 54.4%</td><td>&minus;8.8% to +8.8%</td><td>Meaningful performance evaluation</td></tr>
    <tr><td>1,000 bets</td><td>46.9% – 53.1%</td><td>&minus;6.2% to +6.2%</td><td>Reliable statistical proof of edge</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Out-of-Sample Proof (PunterStat v2.0.0):</strong> Across 3 strict walk-forward seasons (never peeking into future matches), PunterStat''s model held steady Brier scores of <code>0.60460</code> (2022/23), <code>0.60983</code> (2023/24), and <code>0.60887</code> (2024/25+) — proving that true statistical validation requires out-of-sample testing over thousands of events, never cherry-picked hot streaks.</p>

<h2>Key Takeaway</h2>
<p><strong>Why Hot Streaks and Cold Streaks End:</strong> Demand large out-of-sample evidence, respect regression to the mean, and calibrate probabilities against objective scoring rules like Brier score and CLV.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'why-hot-and-cold-streaks-end'
  AND course_id = (SELECT id FROM public.courses WHERE slug = 'regression-to-the-mean');

-- [portfolio-bankroll-allocation] what-is-a-betting-portfolio
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<h2>Thinking in Portfolios</h2>
<p>A single bettor who bets on football match winners only has a single-strategy, single-market portfolio. A sophisticated operation may run multiple strategies (match winner, totals, player props), across multiple sports (football, basketball, tennis), in multiple markets (1X2, AH, exchange).</p>
<p>Managing this as a portfolio — rather than as independent unrelated bets — unlocks significant risk management advantages.</p>
<h2>The Portfolio Framework</h2>
<p>Define the portfolio as a set of independent strategies. Each strategy has:</p>
<ul>
<li>A defined market scope (which bets it covers)</li>
<li>A validated edge (CLV history)</li>
<li>A bankroll allocation (% of total)</li>
<li>Performance metrics tracked separately</li>
</ul>
<h2>Why Separate Strategies Matter</h2>
<p>If all bets are tracked together, you cannot identify which parts of your operation are profitable and which are not. A 3% total ROI could come from:</p>
<ul>
<li>Football 1X2: +7% ROI (strong)</li>
<li>Basketball props: −4% ROI (destroying value)</li>
<li>Football AH: +1.5% ROI (neutral)</li>
</ul>
<p>The aggregate masks the problem. Separate tracking reveals it.</p>
<h2>Portfolio vs Single-Strategy Approaches</h2>
<p>Single strategy: simpler to run, harder to diversify, higher variance per bet volume.</p>
<p>Portfolio approach: more complex, better diversification, smoother equity curve, clearer performance attribution.</p>
<h2>The Entry Criteria for a Strategy in the Portfolio</h2>
<p>A strategy enters the portfolio only when:</p>
<ol>
<li>It has produced positive CLV over 200+ bets (validated edge)</li>
<li>It has defined market scope and selection criteria</li>
<li>It has appropriate bankroll allocation within the total portfolio risk limits</li>
</ol>

<h2>Real-Data Simulation: Comparing Staking Systems on a ₦100,000 Bankroll</h2>
<p>Assume a skilled bettor has a genuine <strong>+5% ROI edge</strong> betting at <code>2.00</code> average odds (52.5% true win rate) over 500 bets. How does staking strategy affect growth and Risk of Ruin?</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Staking Method</th><th>Stake Size on ₦100,000</th><th>Expected Bankroll (500 Bets)</th><th>Max Drawdown Risk</th><th>Risk of Ruin (&gt;80% Loss)</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Flat Staking (1% unit)</td><td>₦1,000 / bet</td><td>~₦125,000</td><td>Low (~12–18%)</td><td>&lt; 0.1%</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Quarter-Kelly (0.25&times; Kelly)</td><td>₦1,250 (scales dynamically)</td><td>~₦133,000</td><td>Moderate (~15–22%)</td><td>&lt; 0.1%</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Full Kelly (1.0&times; Kelly)</td><td>₦5,000 (5% on 5% edge)</td><td>~₦185,000 (median)</td><td>Severe (~45–60%)</td><td>High if edge overestimated</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Martingale (Double after loss)</td><td>₦1k &rarr; ₦2k &rarr; ₦4k &rarr; ₦128k</td><td>Eventual Blowup</td><td><strong>100% wipeout on 7-loss run</strong></td><td><strong>&gt; 75% within 500 bets</strong></td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Practical Rule:</strong> Even with a 52.5% win rate, a <strong>7-bet losing streak</strong> has a ~65% probability of occurring at least once in 500 bets. Flat 1–2% units or Quarter-Kelly survive those inevitable downswings cleanly; Martingale and stake-chasing guarantee ruin.</p>

<h2>Key Takeaway</h2>
<p><strong>What Is a Betting Portfolio?:</strong> Protect your capital first: consistent unit sizing (1–2% flat or fractional Kelly) ensures inevitable losing streaks never end your betting operation.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'what-is-a-betting-portfolio'
  AND course_id = (SELECT id FROM public.courses WHERE slug = 'portfolio-bankroll-allocation');

-- [how-bookmaker-margins-work] what-is-the-overround
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<h2><h1>What Is the Overround?</h1><h2>Definition</h2><p>The overround (also called vigorish, juice, margin, or vigorish percentage) is the built-in mathematical advantage a bookmaker holds on a market. It is the amount by which the implied probabilities of all outcomes in a market exceed 100%.</p><p>In a fair market with no bookmaker margin, the implied probabilities of all possible outcomes would add up to exactly 100%. In reality they add up to more than 100%—that excess is the overround.</p><h2>Simple Example</h2><p>Consider a two-way moneyline:</p><ul><li>Team A: 1.91</li><li>Team B: 1.91</li></ul><p>Implied probability for each side = 1 / 1.91 ≈ 52.36%<br>Total implied probability = 52.36% + 52.36% = <strong>104.72%</strong></p><p>The overround is <strong>4.72%</strong>.<br>This means the bookmaker has priced the market so that, if equal money is bet on both sides, they expect to keep roughly 4.72% of the total stakes as profit (before other costs).</p><h2>How to Calculate Overround</h2><p>For any market:</p><ol><li>Convert each set of odds into implied probability:<br>Implied probability = 1 / decimal odds<br>(or use the equivalent formula for American or fractional odds)</li><li>Add all the implied probabilities together.</li><li>Overround = (Sum of implied probabilities – 1) × 100<br>or simply the percentage by which the sum exceeds 100%.</li></ol><p><strong>Multi-way markets</strong> (e.g., three-way soccer match odds or outrights) work the same way—just sum every outcome.</p><h2>Why Bookmakers Use an Overround</h2><p>The overround is the primary way bookmakers make money. It allows them to:</p><ul><li>Profit even when the money is relatively balanced across outcomes</li><li>Cover operational costs, risk, and the possibility of sharp money</li><li>Maintain a consistent edge without needing to predict results perfectly</li></ul><p>A lower overround is more competitive (better for the bettor). A higher overround is less competitive.</p><h2>Typical Overround Ranges</h2><ul><li>Sharp/Asian books or betting exchanges: often 102–104% (2–4% overround)</li><li>Mainstream retail books: commonly 105–110%+ on major markets</li><li>Niche or higher-margin markets (props, minor leagues, etc.): frequently 110–120% or higher</li></ul><p>The tighter the overround, the closer the odds are to the "true" market probability (all else equal).</p><h2>Why the Overround Matters to Bettors</h2><ul><li>It is the hurdle your edge must overcome. If the book''s overround is 5%, you need to be more than 5% better than the market just to break even in the long run.</li><li>Line shopping across books with different overrounds can meaningfully improve your average price and lower the effective margin you face.</li><li>Closing Line Value (CLV) is measured against the market''s closing odds; books with consistently lower overrounds tend to have sharper closing lines.</li><li>Understanding overround helps you evaluate whether a given price is genuinely soft or simply part of a high-margin market.</li></ul><h2>Overround vs. True Probability</h2><p>Bookmaker odds do not represent pure true probabilities. They represent true probabilities adjusted upward by the overround (and sometimes further skewed by expected betting patterns or liability management).</p><p>When you estimate your own probability for an outcome, you compare it to the book''s implied probability. Your edge exists only when your assessed probability is higher than the book''s implied probability by enough to clear the overround and still leave positive expected value.</p><h2>Practical Takeaway</h2><p>The overround is the bookmaker''s built-in commission expressed as the excess of implied probabilities over 100%. Lower overround = better prices for the bettor. Always calculate or be aware of it when evaluating odds quality, and prioritize books and markets that offer tighter margins whenever possible. Your long-term profitability depends on consistently beating this hurdle.</p></h2>

<h2>Real-Data Benchmark: Bookmaker Overround &amp; Closing Line Efficiency</h2>
<p>In PunterStat''s historical odds dataset (<strong>157,752 Pinnacle closing lines</strong> across 39 football leagues), market margin varies sharply between sharp exchanges/bookmakers and soft retail sportsbooks:</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Bookmaker / Market Tier</th><th>Typical 1X2 Overround</th><th>Fair 50/50 Price Offered</th><th>Long-Run Drag on ₦100,000 Staked</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Pinnacle / Betting Exchange (Sharp)</td><td>2.0% – 2.8%</td><td>1.95 – 1.96</td><td>&minus;₦2,200 to &minus;₦2,600</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Tier-1 European / Global Retail</td><td>4.5% – 6.0%</td><td>1.88 – 1.91</td><td>&minus;₦4,800 to &minus;₦5,800</td></tr>
    <tr><td>High-Margin Retail / Multi-Market Props</td><td>7.0% – 11.5%</td><td>1.80 – 1.85</td><td>&minus;₦7,000 to &minus;₦10,500</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Worked Example:</strong> On a Premier League 1X2 market priced at Home <code>2.10</code> (47.62%), Draw <code>3.40</code> (29.41%), Away <code>3.60</code> (27.78%), the summed implied probability is <strong>104.81%</strong> — a <strong>4.81% overround</strong>. Dividing each implied probability by <code>1.0481</code> reveals the fair no-vig probabilities: Home <strong>45.43%</strong> (fair odds <code>2.20</code>), Draw <strong>28.06%</strong> (<code>3.56</code>), Away <strong>26.51%</strong> (<code>3.77</code>). Every time you accept <code>2.10</code> on a true <code>2.20</code> outcome, you surrender 4.5% expected value before kick-off.</p>

<h2>Key Takeaway</h2>
<p><strong>What Is the Overround?:</strong> Always convert offered odds into de-vigged fair probability, compare across bookmakers, and measure your decisions against the sharp closing line.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'what-is-the-overround'
  AND course_id = (SELECT id FROM public.courses WHERE slug = 'how-bookmaker-margins-work');

-- [understanding-odds-formats] decimal-odds
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<img src="/betting-analytics-desk.jpg" alt="Decimal odds and implied probability calculation desk" />
      <h2>How Decimal Odds Translate Directly to Naira (₦) Returns</h2>
      <p>Every major licensed Nigerian bookmaker — including <strong>SportyBet, Bet9ja, BetKing, and 1xBet</strong> — uses <strong>Decimal Odds</strong> (e.g., <code>1.75</code>, <code>2.10</code>, <code>3.50</code>). Decimal odds tell you your <strong>total Naira payout for every ₦1 staked</strong>, including your original stake back:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Total Return (₦) = Stake (₦) × Decimal Odds</strong></li>
        <li><strong>Net Profit (₦) = Total Return (₦) − Stake (₦)</strong></li>
        <li><strong>Implied Probability (%) = (1 ÷ Decimal Odds) × 100</strong></li>
      </ul>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Fixture &amp; Market (EPL / UCL)</th>
            <th>Decimal Odds</th>
            <th>Stake (₦)</th>
            <th>Total Return (₦)</th>
            <th>Net Profit (₦)</th>
            <th>Implied Probability</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Arsenal vs Chelsea — Over 2.5 Goals</strong></td>
            <td>1.80</td>
            <td>₦5,000</td>
            <td>₦9,000</td>
            <td><strong>+₦4,000</strong></td>
            <td>55.6%</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Real Madrid vs Man City — BTTS (GG)</strong></td>
            <td>1.62</td>
            <td>₦10,000</td>
            <td>₦16,200</td>
            <td><strong>+₦6,200</strong></td>
            <td>61.7%</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Atalanta vs AC Milan — Atalanta DNB (0.0)</strong></td>
            <td>2.05</td>
            <td>₦5,000</td>
            <td>₦10,250</td>
            <td><strong>+₦5,250</strong></td>
            <td>48.8%</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <blockquote>
        <strong>The "Sure Odd" Illusion:</strong> Many punters call a <code>1.25</code> odd on a heavy favourite a "banker." Mathematically, <code>1 ÷ 1.25 = 80%</code> — meaning a <code>1.25</code> favourite fails <strong>1 out of every 5 times (20%)</strong>! Combine five <code>1.25</code> "bankers" on one slip and your true chance of winning drops below <strong>32%</strong>.
      </blockquote>

<h2>Key Takeaway</h2>
<p><strong>How Decimal Odds Work on SportyBet, Bet9ja & BetKing (Naira ₦ Examples on EPL & UCL):</strong> Always convert offered odds into de-vigged fair probability, compare across bookmakers, and measure your decisions against the sharp closing line.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'decimal-odds'
  AND course_id = (SELECT id FROM public.courses WHERE slug = 'understanding-odds-formats');

-- [odds-comparison-line-shopping] why-line-shopping-non-negotiable
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<h2><h1>Why Line Shopping Is Non-Negotiable</h1><h2>Introduction</h2><p>Line shopping means comparing the odds for the same bet across multiple sportsbooks and taking the best available price. It is one of the highest-leverage, lowest-effort habits in sports betting. Skipping it is equivalent to voluntarily giving the books extra margin on every wager.</p><p>Over hundreds or thousands of bets, the difference between consistently taking the best price and accepting whatever is in front of you compounds into a large swing in long-term results.</p><h2>The Mathematical Case</h2><p>Every extra cent (or point) of price improvement raises your expected value and lowers the hurdle created by the bookmaker''s overround.</p><p>Simple illustration using decimal odds:</p><ul><li>–110 is equivalent to decimal odds of 1.91</li><li>–105 is equivalent to decimal odds of 1.95</li></ul><p>Break-even win rates on a 50/50 proposition:</p><ul><li>At 1.91 you need to win 52.36% just to break even.</li><li>At 1.95 you need only 51.28%.</li></ul><p>That 1.08 percentage-point reduction in the required win rate is pure edge. Across a large sample of bets it compounds into a meaningful difference in long-term results—even if the quality of your selections stays exactly the same.</p><p>The same principle applies to every market: moneylines, spreads, totals, props, and futures. Small, repeated improvements add up faster than most bettors realize.</p><h2>Why the Differences Exist</h2><p>Books set lines independently. They have different:</p><ul><li>Customer bases and liability</li><li>Risk-management approaches</li><li>Target margins</li><li>Speed of reaction to news and sharp money</li></ul><p>As a result, the same game routinely shows meaningful price gaps. One book may offer 1.91 while another offers 1.95 or even 2.00 on the same side. On spreads and totals the difference is often half a point or more—enough to change the expected value of the bet.</p><h2>Quantifying the Impact</h2><p>Studies and large tracking samples consistently show that disciplined line shoppers improve their average closing-line value and overall ROI by 1–3% or more compared with bettors who use a single book.</p><p>Because most recreational and even many semi-serious bettors operate with thin (or negative) edges, that extra 1–3% is frequently the entire difference between long-term profit and long-term loss.</p><h2>Practical Line-Shopping Process</h2><ol><li>Maintain accounts at several sharp and semi-sharp books (plus any soft books that still offer usable limits).</li><li>Before placing a bet, check the price at a minimum of three to five books.</li><li>Use odds-comparison tools or a simple multi-book interface when available, but verify the number on the actual sportsbook before confirming.</li><li>Factor in any relevant differences in rules (e.g., push rules on spreads/totals, early-payout promotions, or settlement policies).</li><li>Place the bet at the book offering the best combination of price and reliability.</li></ol><p>Speed matters on moving lines, but the habit of checking remains non-negotiable even when you must act quickly.</p><h2>Common Objections and Why They Fail</h2><ul><li><strong>"I only have one main book."</strong><br>Then open more. The administrative cost is trivial compared with the ongoing price disadvantage.</li><li><strong>"The differences are usually tiny."</strong><br>Tiny per bet becomes large over volume. A consistent 2–3 cent improvement is enormous in aggregate.</li><li><strong>"I don''t want to move money around."</strong><br>Keep modest balances at secondary books precisely so you can take the best number without delay.</li><li><strong>"My book is sharp, so the line is already good."</strong><br>Even sharp books are not always the best number on every market. Cross-checking still pays.</li></ul><h2>Additional Benefits Beyond Pure Price</h2><ul><li>Access to better limits or alternative markets at different books.</li><li>Reduced impact when one account becomes limited or restricted.</li><li>Clearer view of true market consensus, which helps with closing-line value assessment.</li><li>Occasional soft prices that appear only at certain books.</li></ul><h2>Real-Data Benchmark: Bookmaker Overround &amp; Closing Line Efficiency</h2>
<p>In PunterStat''s historical odds dataset (<strong>157,752 Pinnacle closing lines</strong> across 39 football leagues), market margin varies sharply between sharp exchanges/bookmakers and soft retail sportsbooks:</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Bookmaker / Market Tier</th><th>Typical 1X2 Overround</th><th>Fair 50/50 Price Offered</th><th>Long-Run Drag on ₦100,000 Staked</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Pinnacle / Betting Exchange (Sharp)</td><td>2.0% – 2.8%</td><td>1.95 – 1.96</td><td>&minus;₦2,200 to &minus;₦2,600</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Tier-1 European / Global Retail</td><td>4.5% – 6.0%</td><td>1.88 – 1.91</td><td>&minus;₦4,800 to &minus;₦5,800</td></tr>
    <tr><td>High-Margin Retail / Multi-Market Props</td><td>7.0% – 11.5%</td><td>1.80 – 1.85</td><td>&minus;₦7,000 to &minus;₦10,500</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Worked Example:</strong> On a Premier League 1X2 market priced at Home <code>2.10</code> (47.62%), Draw <code>3.40</code> (29.41%), Away <code>3.60</code> (27.78%), the summed implied probability is <strong>104.81%</strong> — a <strong>4.81% overround</strong>. Dividing each implied probability by <code>1.0481</code> reveals the fair no-vig probabilities: Home <strong>45.43%</strong> (fair odds <code>2.20</code>), Draw <strong>28.06%</strong> (<code>3.56</code>), Away <strong>26.51%</strong> (<code>3.77</code>). Every time you accept <code>2.10</code> on a true <code>2.20</code> outcome, you surrender 4.5% expected value before kick-off.</p>

<h2>Key Takeaway</h2><p>Line shopping is not an advanced technique—it is basic hygiene. It requires no predictive skill, only the willingness to compare prices and maintain multiple accounts. Over any meaningful sample it systematically improves your average odds, reduces the effective overround you pay, and raises long-term ROI.</p><p>Bettors who treat the first price they see as "good enough" are permanently operating at a self-imposed disadvantage. Those who refuse to take a worse number when a better one is available give themselves one of the most reliable edges available in the entire betting process. Make it non-negotiable.</p></h2>'
WHERE slug = 'why-line-shopping-non-negotiable'
  AND course_id = (SELECT id FROM public.courses WHERE slug = 'odds-comparison-line-shopping');

-- [bankroll-fundamentals] what-is-a-bankroll
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<img src="/betting-analytics-desk.jpg" alt="Bankroll growth curve and unit staking chart" />
      <h2>Why Instant OPay / PalmPay Transfers Destroy Undisciplined Bettors</h2>
      <p>Because funding a Nigerian betting wallet via OPay, PalmPay, Moniepoint, or Kuda takes less than 10 seconds with zero friction, most recreational bettors do not have a <strong>bankroll</strong> — they just deposit <code>₦2,000</code> or <code>₦5,000</code> from their main bank account whenever they see a Champions League lineup. When that bet loses, they immediately transfer another <code>₦10,000</code> from their living/business funds to chase it.</p>
      <h3>The 1%–2% Unit Staking Blueprint</h3>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Ring-Fence Your Betting Capital:</strong> Set a fixed monthly or season bankroll that is 100% separate from rent, food, business inventory, or family expenses.</li>
        <li><strong>Stake in Units (1% to 2% per Single Bet):</strong> Never stake more than 2%–3% of your bankroll on a single match, even on a Champions League final.</li>
      </ol>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Dedicated Bankroll (₦)</th>
            <th>1% Unit Stake (Standard)</th>
            <th>2% Unit Stake (Strong Edge)</th>
            <th>Maximum Weekly Stop-Loss (8 Units)</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>₦25,000</strong></td>
            <td>₦250 / bet</td>
            <td>₦500 / bet</td>
            <td>₦2,000 max weekly loss</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>₦50,000</strong></td>
            <td>₦500 / bet</td>
            <td>₦1,000 / bet</td>
            <td>₦4,000 max weekly loss</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>₦100,000</strong></td>
            <td>₦1,000 / bet</td>
            <td>₦2,000 / bet</td>
            <td>₦8,000 max weekly loss</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>₦250,000</strong></td>
            <td>₦2,500 / bet</td>
            <td>₦5,000 / bet</td>
            <td>₦20,000 max weekly loss</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

<h2>Key Takeaway</h2>
<p><strong>Ring-Fencing Your Naira Bankroll & Sizing 1–2% Units (OPay/PalmPay Discipline):</strong> Protect your capital first: consistent unit sizing (1–2% flat or fractional Kelly) ensures inevitable losing streaks never end your betting operation.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'what-is-a-bankroll'
  AND course_id = (SELECT id FROM public.courses WHERE slug = 'bankroll-fundamentals');

-- [model-evaluation-calibration] train-test-split-testing
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<h2>Why In-Sample Testing Is Misleading</h2>
<p>If you fit a model on a dataset and then evaluate it on the same dataset, you will always get an overly optimistic result. The model has "seen" the answers and has been optimised for them. This is in-sample evaluation — it tells you how well the model memorised the training data, not how well it predicts the future.</p>
<h2>The Train/Test Split</h2>
<p>The solution: reserve a portion of data exclusively for testing. The model never sees this data during training.</p>
<p>Standard split:</p>
<ul>
<li>70–80% of data: training set (model fitted on this)</li>
<li>20–30% of data: test set (model evaluated on this)</li>
</ul>
<p>For time-series data (sports matches are time-ordered), use a temporal split: all matches before date X for training, all matches after date X for testing. Do not randomly shuffle time-series data — this introduces lookahead bias.</p>
<h2>Walk-Forward Testing</h2>
<p>Even a single temporal split can be misleading if the split date is chosen to favour the model. Walk-forward testing (also called rolling window validation) is more robust:</p>
<ol>
<li>Train on months 1–12. Test on month 13.</li>
<li>Train on months 2–13. Test on month 14.</li>
<li>Continue rolling forward.</li>
<li>Aggregate test results across all periods.</li>
</ol>
<p>This simulates how the model would perform in genuine real-time deployment.</p>
<h2>The Minimum Test Set Size</h2>
<p>A test set must be large enough to provide statistically meaningful evaluation. For a betting model:</p>
<ul>
<li>Minimum test set: 300 matches per league</li>
<li>Preferred: 500+ matches per league</li>
<li>Multiple seasons preferred over a single season (captures different competitive environments)</li>
</ul>

<h2>Real-Data Benchmark: Sample Size, Variance &amp; Walk-Forward Stability</h2>
<p>Short-term win rates fool almost every bettor. Even on a fair 50/50 proposition (<code>2.00</code> odds), the 95% confidence interval around your observed win rate shrinks only with the <em>square root</em> of sample size (<code>&radic;n</code>):</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Settled Bets (n)</th><th>95% Win-Rate Confidence Band (at True 50%)</th><th>95% ROI Noise Band at 2.00 Odds</th><th>Statistical Verdict</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>25 bets</td><td>30.4% – 69.6%</td><td>&minus;39.2% to +39.2%</td><td>Pure noise — impossible to judge skill</td></tr>
    <tr><td>100 bets</td><td>40.2% – 59.8%</td><td>&minus;19.6% to +19.6%</td><td>Early directional signal only</td></tr>
    <tr><td>500 bets</td><td>45.6% – 54.4%</td><td>&minus;8.8% to +8.8%</td><td>Meaningful performance evaluation</td></tr>
    <tr><td>1,000 bets</td><td>46.9% – 53.1%</td><td>&minus;6.2% to +6.2%</td><td>Reliable statistical proof of edge</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Out-of-Sample Proof (PunterStat v2.0.0):</strong> Across 3 strict walk-forward seasons (never peeking into future matches), PunterStat''s model held steady Brier scores of <code>0.60460</code> (2022/23), <code>0.60983</code> (2023/24), and <code>0.60887</code> (2024/25+) — proving that true statistical validation requires out-of-sample testing over thousands of events, never cherry-picked hot streaks.</p>

<h2>Key Takeaway</h2>
<p><strong>Train/Test Split and Out-of-Sample Testing:</strong> Demand large out-of-sample evidence, respect regression to the mean, and calibrate probabilities against objective scoring rules like Brier score and CLV.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'train-test-split-testing'
  AND course_id = (SELECT id FROM public.courses WHERE slug = 'model-evaluation-calibration');

-- [building-predictive-models] model-building-pipeline
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<h2>From Data to Probability</h2>
<p>Building a sports prediction model follows a reproducible pipeline. Each step must be completed before the next begins — shortcuts at any stage compromise the entire output.</p>
<h2>The Six-Stage Pipeline</h2>
<p><strong>Stage 1 — Define the question:</strong> What exactly are you predicting? Match winner? Goals scored? Player performance metric? The prediction target must be precisely defined before data collection begins.</p>
<p><strong>Stage 2 — Collect and clean data:</strong> Identify data sources, collect historical data, clean for errors and missing values, structure for analysis. This stage takes the most time.</p>
<p><strong>Stage 3 — Feature engineering:</strong> Create the input variables (features) from raw data. Examples: rolling average xG, home/away performance split, head-to-head record, days since last match.</p>
<p><strong>Stage 4 — Model specification:</strong> Choose the model structure: Poisson regression, logistic regression, Elo rating, or machine learning approach. Simple models should be tried before complex ones.</p>
<p><strong>Stage 5 — Estimation:</strong> Fit the model to historical data. Estimate parameters.</p>
<p><strong>Stage 6 — Evaluation:</strong> Test the model on held-out data. Measure probability accuracy. Compare to the market.</p>
<h2>The Minimum Viable Model</h2>
<p>A first model does not need to be sophisticated. A minimum viable model:</p>
<ul>
<li>Uses 3–5 input features (home xG rate, away xG rate, home advantage, rest days difference, relative position)</li>
<li>Uses Poisson regression with these features as predictors</li>
<li>Produces match-level goal probabilities from which all market probabilities can be derived</li>
</ul>
<p>Build the MVP first. Evaluate it. Improve iteratively.</p>

<h2>Real-Data Benchmark: Sample Size, Variance &amp; Walk-Forward Stability</h2>
<p>Short-term win rates fool almost every bettor. Even on a fair 50/50 proposition (<code>2.00</code> odds), the 95% confidence interval around your observed win rate shrinks only with the <em>square root</em> of sample size (<code>&radic;n</code>):</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Settled Bets (n)</th><th>95% Win-Rate Confidence Band (at True 50%)</th><th>95% ROI Noise Band at 2.00 Odds</th><th>Statistical Verdict</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>25 bets</td><td>30.4% – 69.6%</td><td>&minus;39.2% to +39.2%</td><td>Pure noise — impossible to judge skill</td></tr>
    <tr><td>100 bets</td><td>40.2% – 59.8%</td><td>&minus;19.6% to +19.6%</td><td>Early directional signal only</td></tr>
    <tr><td>500 bets</td><td>45.6% – 54.4%</td><td>&minus;8.8% to +8.8%</td><td>Meaningful performance evaluation</td></tr>
    <tr><td>1,000 bets</td><td>46.9% – 53.1%</td><td>&minus;6.2% to +6.2%</td><td>Reliable statistical proof of edge</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Out-of-Sample Proof (PunterStat v2.0.0):</strong> Across 3 strict walk-forward seasons (never peeking into future matches), PunterStat''s model held steady Brier scores of <code>0.60460</code> (2022/23), <code>0.60983</code> (2023/24), and <code>0.60887</code> (2024/25+) — proving that true statistical validation requires out-of-sample testing over thousands of events, never cherry-picked hot streaks.</p>

<h2>Key Takeaway</h2>
<p><strong>The Model Building Pipeline:</strong> Demand large out-of-sample evidence, respect regression to the mean, and calibrate probabilities against objective scoring rules like Brier score and CLV.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'model-building-pipeline'
  AND course_id = (SELECT id FROM public.courses WHERE slug = 'building-predictive-models');

-- [handicap-and-asian-handicap] draw-no-bet-and-asian-handicap-lines
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<img src="/hero-pitch.jpg" alt="Asian handicap and Draw No Bet tactical guide" />
      <h2>How Asian Handicaps Protect Your Naira Stake From 90th-Minute Equalizers</h2>
      <div data-block="stat" data-factor="btts_rate" data-league="E0"></div>
      <p>Roughly <strong>1 in 4 European football matches ends in a draw</strong>. Instead of losing your entire stake on a 1X2 market when a match finishes <code>1–1</code>, or taking tiny odds on Double Chance (<code>1X</code> at <code>1.18</code>), professional analysts use <strong>Asian Handicap (AH)</strong> lines:</p>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
