-- [cognitive-biases-in-betting] gamblers-fallacy-and-recency-bias
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<img src="/academy-session.jpg" alt="Psychological discipline and pre-commitment rules" />
      <h2>1. The Saturday Timeline of Tilt (1:30 PM → 4:00 PM → 9:00 PM WAT)</h2>
      <p>Watch how emotional chasing destroys a weekend in three steps:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>1:30 PM WAT (EPL Early Kick-Off):</strong> A punter stakes <code>₦5,000</code> on a heavy away favourite that draws <code>1–1</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>4:00 PM WAT (Main EPL Window):</strong> Instead of sticking to his pre-researched <code>₦5,000</code> unit, he doubles up to <code>₦15,000</code> on a 4-leg accumulator to "recover his ₦5,000 before evening."</li>
        <li><strong>8:45 PM / 9:00 PM WAT (Serie A &amp; La Liga Night Games):</strong> Down <code>₦20,000</code>, he dumps <code>₦30,000</code> on Real Madrid or Barcelona away at compressed odds without checking lineups.</li>
      </ul>
      <h2>2. The Hidden 12% Penalty Inside "Cash Out" Offers</h2>
      <p>Suppose you staked <strong>₦5,000 on Arsenal to beat Tottenham @ 2.20</strong> (potential payout <code>₦11,000</code>). Arsenal leads 1–0 in the 78th minute, and the true live probability of Arsenal holding on is <strong>80%</strong>:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Fair Mathematical Value of Your Ticket:</strong> <code>80% × ₦11,000 = ₦8,800</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>Bookmaker Cash-Out Button Offer:</strong> <code>₦7,500</code> to <code>₦7,750</code>.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Clicking Cash Out out of fear hands the bookmaker an extra <strong>₦1,050 to ₦1,300 (~12%–15%) in hidden margin</strong>! Never cash out unless a red card, injury, or severe tactical swing has made your team''s live probability worse than the Cash-Out offer implies.</p>
      <h2>3. Why Virtual Football &amp; Crash Games (Aviator/JetX) Are Mathematically Unbeatable</h2>
      <p>In real sports (EPL, UCL, La Liga, NBA, ATP), bookmakers can misprice human teams based on xG, tactics, or fatigue. In <strong>Virtual Football and Crash Games</strong>, outcomes are controlled by a fixed <strong>Random Number Generator (RNG)</strong> programmed to retain <strong>4% to 12% of every Naira staked</strong> automatically. There is zero analytical edge in RNG games — treat them strictly as entertainment or avoid them completely.</p>

<h2>Real-Data Pattern: How Loss-Chasing ("Tilt") Destroys Long-Run ROI</h2>
<p>Behavioral analysis across tracked bet journals (and PunterStat''s Empirical Bayes <strong>Tilt Risk Model</strong>) reveals a stark divergence between disciplined flat-stake sessions and post-loss chasing sessions:</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Behavioral State</th><th>Typical Stake vs Median</th><th>Average Leg Count</th><th>Typical Realized ROI</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Pre-planned disciplined bet (rested)</td><td>1.0&times; median stake</td><td>1 – 2 legs</td><td>Near fair (&minus;3% to +4%)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Post-loss bet within 2 hours of a 2+ loss streak</td><td><strong>2.2&times; – 3.5&times; median stake</strong></td><td>3 – 6 legs (chasing payout)</td><td><strong>&minus;24% to &minus;42%</strong></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Late-night / unfamiliar-league impulse bet</td><td>1.5&times; – 2.5&times; median stake</td><td>4+ legs</td><td><strong>&minus;30%+</strong></td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Actionable Defense:</strong> Pre-commit your maximum daily stake (e.g. 3% of bankroll) and enforce a mandatory <strong>24-hour cooling-off rule</strong> after 3 consecutive losses. Log every slip in PunterStat''s <strong>Bet Tracker</strong> so your Report Card exposes tilt patterns objectively before they drain your bankroll.</p>

<h2>Key Takeaway</h2>
<p><strong>The Saturday 1:30 PM Early Kick-Off Tilt, Cash-Out Math & Why Virtuals Cannot Be Beaten:</strong> Separate decision quality from single-bet outcomes, enforce pre-committed stake limits, and rely on logged journal data rather than gut instinct.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'gamblers-fallacy-and-recency-bias'
  AND course_id = (SELECT id FROM public.courses WHERE slug = 'cognitive-biases-in-betting');

-- [live-and-in-play-odds] how-in-play-markets-work
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<h2><h1>How In-Play Markets Work</h1><h2>What In-Play (Live) Betting Is</h2><p>In-play markets are bets offered while an event is in progress. Odds update continuously (or in rapid intervals) based on the current state of the game, time remaining, score, and other real-time factors. Almost every major sport now offers extensive in-play markets: match result, next goal/team to score, totals, handicaps, player props, and more.</p><h2>How the Odds Are Generated</h2><p>Bookmakers use a combination of:</p><ul><li><strong>Automated pricing models</strong> — algorithms that take the pre-match probabilities and adjust them second-by-second according to the live score, time elapsed, possession, and other inputs.</li><li><strong>Trader oversight</strong> — human traders monitor key matches, especially high-liability or unusual situations, and can manually adjust or suspend markets.</li><li><strong>Feed data</strong> — official or licensed data providers supply real-time statistics (score, clock, cards, substitutions, etc.) that feed the pricing engines.</li></ul><p>The result is a constantly moving set of odds that attempt to reflect the current “true” probability of each outcome given what has already happened.</p><h2>Key Mechanics of In-Play Markets</h2><p><strong>1. Continuous or near-continuous updating</strong><br>Odds can change after every significant event (goal, wicket, point, red card, etc.) and also drift gradually with time.</p><p><strong>2. Market suspensions</strong><br>Books frequently suspend markets for a few seconds to minutes during major incidents (goals, penalties, reviews, injuries, or technical issues). During suspension you cannot place new bets. This protects the book from being hit with bets on information that has not yet been fully priced in.</p><p><strong>3. Latency and delay</strong><br>There is almost always a short delay between real-world events and the odds updating on your screen. Professional bettors and some recreational users try to exploit any visible lag, which is why books invest heavily in fast data feeds and suspension technology.</p><p><strong>4. Cash-out and partial cash-out</strong><br>Most books offer the ability to settle a bet early for a value calculated from the current live odds. This is a risk-management tool for both the customer and the bookmaker. The cash-out price always includes a margin.</p><p><strong>5. Higher margins than pre-match</strong><br>In-play markets typically carry a larger overround than the same markets pre-match. The increased uncertainty and operational cost for the book are priced into the odds.</p><p><strong>6. Liquidity and limits</strong><br>Limits can be lower or more variable in-play, especially on smaller leagues or exotic markets. Sharp action is also more carefully monitored in real time.</p><h2>Differences from Pre-Match Betting</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Pre-Match</th><th>In-Play</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Information</td><td>Based on expected performance</td><td>Incorporates actual events so far</td></tr><tr><td>Odds movement</td><td>Gradual, news-driven</td><td>Rapid, score- and time-driven</td></tr><tr><td>Margin (overround)</td><td>Generally lower</td><td>Generally higher</td></tr><tr><td>Decision speed</td><td>More time to analyse</td><td>Requires fast processing</td></tr><tr><td>Emotional pressure</td><td>Lower</td><td>Often higher</td></tr><tr><td>Opportunity set</td><td>Fixed once lines are set</td><td>Constantly changing</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>How Bettors Use In-Play Markets</h2><ul><li><strong>Reacting to the game flow</strong> — betting on a team that has started strongly or weakly relative to the pre-match price.</li><li><strong>Hedging or reducing risk</strong> — using live odds to lock in profit or minimise loss on a pre-match position.</li><li><strong>Trading</strong> — backing and then laying (or cashing out) as the odds move.</li><li><strong>Exploiting temporary mispricing</strong> — when the model or trader is slow to adjust after a key event.</li><li><strong>Scalping small edges</strong> — repeatedly taking small advantages created by latency or conservative pricing.</li></ul><h2>Risks Specific to In-Play Betting</h2><ul><li>Faster decision-making increases the chance of emotional or poorly reasoned bets.</li><li>Higher average margins mean you need a stronger edge to be profitable.</li><li>Latency and suspensions can cause frustration or the feeling of “missing” value.</li><li>It is easier to over-bet or chase because new opportunities appear constantly.</li><li>Some books restrict or delay accounts that consistently beat live markets.</li></ul><h2>Practical Tips for Using In-Play Markets</h2><ul><li>Decide your approach in advance (reactive value, hedging, or selective trading) rather than betting every swing.</li><li>Keep unit size the same as, or smaller than, your pre-match sizing because variance and decision pressure are higher.</li><li>Use reliable, low-latency connections and interfaces.</li><li>Be aware of which books are quickest to suspend and which are slower (the slower ones can offer both opportunity and danger).</li><li>Track in-play results separately so you can judge whether you actually have an edge in live markets.</li><li>Treat cash-out as a tool with its own cost, not a free option.</li></ul><h2>Key Takeaway</h2><p>In-play markets are dynamic versions of traditional betting markets. Odds are driven by real-time data and algorithmic models, with human traders intervening on important matches. They offer more opportunities and more ways to manage existing positions, but they also carry higher margins, faster decision pressure, and greater emotional risk.</p><p>Success in-play still rests on the same foundations as pre-match betting: a genuine edge, disciplined staking, and honest tracking. The main difference is that the window to act is much shorter and the environment is more volatile.</p></h2>'
WHERE slug = 'how-in-play-markets-work'
  AND course_id = (SELECT id FROM public.courses WHERE slug = 'live-and-in-play-odds');

-- [sample-size-and-variance] how-many-bets-to-know-anything
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<img src="/tactical-stadium.jpg" alt="Statistical variance and sample size distribution" />
      <div data-block="stat" data-factor="over25_rate" data-league="E0"></div>
      <h2>1. Why "VIP Fixed Match / 10-Win Streak" Screenshots Are Statistical Noise</h2>
      <p>On social media, scammers and touts post screenshots of 8 or 10 winning bets in a row to sell "VIP codes." In probability theory, variance dominates small samples: you need at least <strong>200 to 300 tracked singles</strong> (and consistent positive Closing Line Value) to prove an analytical model beats the bookmaker''s margin.</p>
      <h2>2. Regression to the Mean: Fading Unsustainable Hot Streaks</h2>
      <p>Suppose a mid-table Premier League or Serie A team wins 4 games in a row <code>1–0</code> despite creating only <code>0.75 xG per game</code> and conceding <code>1.95 xG per game</code> (saved by miraculous goalkeeping and opponents hitting the post). The public piles onto them in Match 5 at short odds. Sharp analysts recognise <strong>xG overperformance</strong> and back the opponent on the Asian Handicap before regression to the mean hits.</p>
      <h2>3. Confirmed Lineups (60–75 Minutes Before Kick-Off)</h2>
      <p>Official UEFA and Premier League lineups drop <strong>60 to 75 minutes before kick-off</strong>. When a key creator or defensive anchor (e.g., Rodri, Saka, Palmer, Lookman, Osimhen, or Saliba) is unexpectedly absent from the starting XI, sharp syndicates move the Asian Handicap and Over/Under lines within 120 seconds. Always check confirmed lineups before staking.</p>

<h2>Real-Data Benchmark: Sample Size, Variance &amp; Walk-Forward Stability</h2>
<p>Short-term win rates fool almost every bettor. Even on a fair 50/50 proposition (<code>2.00</code> odds), the 95% confidence interval around your observed win rate shrinks only with the <em>square root</em> of sample size (<code>&radic;n</code>):</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Settled Bets (n)</th><th>95% Win-Rate Confidence Band (at True 50%)</th><th>95% ROI Noise Band at 2.00 Odds</th><th>Statistical Verdict</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>25 bets</td><td>30.4% – 69.6%</td><td>&minus;39.2% to +39.2%</td><td>Pure noise — impossible to judge skill</td></tr>
    <tr><td>100 bets</td><td>40.2% – 59.8%</td><td>&minus;19.6% to +19.6%</td><td>Early directional signal only</td></tr>
    <tr><td>500 bets</td><td>45.6% – 54.4%</td><td>&minus;8.8% to +8.8%</td><td>Meaningful performance evaluation</td></tr>
    <tr><td>1,000 bets</td><td>46.9% – 53.1%</td><td>&minus;6.2% to +6.2%</td><td>Reliable statistical proof of edge</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Out-of-Sample Proof (PunterStat v2.0.0):</strong> Across 3 strict walk-forward seasons (never peeking into future matches), PunterStat''s model held steady Brier scores of <code>0.60460</code> (2022/23), <code>0.60983</code> (2023/24), and <code>0.60887</code> (2024/25+) — proving that true statistical validation requires out-of-sample testing over thousands of events, never cherry-picked hot streaks.</p>

<h2>Key Takeaway</h2>
<p><strong>Sample Size, xG Regression to the Mean & Reading Confirmed-Lineup Steam Moves:</strong> Demand large out-of-sample evidence, respect regression to the mean, and calibrate probabilities against objective scoring rules like Brier score and CLV.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'how-many-bets-to-know-anything'
  AND course_id = (SELECT id FROM public.courses WHERE slug = 'sample-size-and-variance');

-- [finding-value-bets] what-is-expected-value-ev
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<img src="/betting-analytics-desk.jpg" alt="Expected value calculation and probability curve" />
      <h2>What Is a Value Bet (+EV)?</h2>
      <p>A <strong>Value Bet (+EV)</strong> happens when the <strong>true probability</strong> of an outcome is greater than the <strong>bookmaker''s implied probability</strong>. You are not betting on "which team is bigger" — you are betting on a <strong>mispriced number</strong>.</p>
      <h3>The Expected Value Formula in Naira (₦)</h3>
      <p><strong>EV = (True Win Probability × Net Profit if Won) − (True Loss Probability × Stake)</strong></p>
      <h3>Worked Example: Atalanta vs Napoli (Serie A)</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Bookmaker Odds on Atalanta Win:</strong> <code>2.25</code> (Implied probability = <code>44.4%</code>)</li>
        <li><strong>PunterStat Model True Probability (factoring Atalanta''s home xG + Lookman''s matchup):</strong> <code>51.0%</code> (Fair odds = <code>1.96</code>)</li>
        <li><strong>Your Stake:</strong> <code>₦10,000</code> (Net profit if won = <code>₦12,500</code>)</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>Calculation:</strong> <code>EV = (0.51 × ₦12,500) − (0.49 × ₦10,000) = ₦6,375 − ₦4,900 = +₦1,475 (+14.75% EV)</code>.</p>
      <p>Even though Atalanta will still lose or draw 49% of the time in this scenario, placing 100 bets with this exact <code>+14.75% EV</code> profile generates an expected profit of <strong>+₦147,500</strong>.</p>

<h2>Real-Data Benchmark: PunterStat v2.0 Model Calibration vs Market</h2>
<p>On PunterStat''s held-out test set of <strong>27,830 matches (2024/25–2026/27)</strong>, comparing pure statistical models against de-vigged Pinnacle closing odds illustrates why finding genuine +EV requires precision:</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Probability Source</th><th>1X2 Brier Score (Lower = Better)</th><th>Log-Loss</th><th>Ranked Probability Score (RPS)</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Pinnacle Closing Odds (De-vigged)</td><td><strong>0.59811</strong></td><td><strong>1.00018</strong></td><td><strong>0.20301</strong></td></tr>
    <tr><td>PunterStat v2.0 LGBM + Market Blend</td><td>0.59858</td><td>1.00130</td><td>0.20384</td></tr>
    <tr><td>PunterStat v2.0 Dixon-Coles + LGBM Hybrid (Pure Stats)</td><td>0.60868</td><td>1.01607</td><td>0.20869</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Worked +EV Example in Naira:</strong> Suppose your de-vigged model prices an Over 2.5 Goals selection at <strong>52.0% true probability</strong> (fair odds <code>1.92</code>), and a bookmaker offers <code>2.10</code>. On a <strong>₦5,000 stake</strong>: <code>EV = (0.52 &times; ₦5,500) &minus; (0.48 &times; ₦5,000) = ₦2,860 &minus; ₦2,400 = +₦460</code> (<strong>+9.2% ROI</strong>). Over 1 bet, you still lose 48% of the time; over 500 similar +EV bets, expected profit compounds to <strong>+₦230,000</strong>.</p>

<h2>Key Takeaway</h2>
<p><strong>Expected Value (+EV) Explained with Real Naira (₦) Premier League & Serie A Examples:</strong> Sustainable profit comes strictly from taking prices higher than true probability (+EV) over large samples — never from chasing high-probability low-value favourites.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'what-is-expected-value-ev'
  AND course_id = (SELECT id FROM public.courses WHERE slug = 'finding-value-bets');

-- [probability-distributions-sport] binomial-distribution-match
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<h2>The Binomial Setting</h2>
<p>The binomial distribution models the number of successes in n independent trials, where each trial has probability p of success. In betting, this applies whenever you have a series of yes/no outcomes.</p>
<h2>Examples in Betting</h2>
<p><strong>Win rate over N bets:</strong> If your true win rate is 48% (even-money betting with slight edge), what is the probability of winning exactly 52 out of 100 bets?</p>
<p>P(X = 52) = C(100,52) × 0.48^52 × 0.52^48 ≈ 5.4%</p>
<p><strong>Clean sheet probability over a season:</strong> A team keeps clean sheets in 32% of matches. What is the probability they keep 10+ clean sheets in a 38-match season?</p>
<p>P(X ≥ 10) = Σ P(X = k) for k = 10 to 38 ≈ 72%</p>
<h2>Calculating Binomial Probabilities</h2>
<p>Binomial CDF gives cumulative probabilities: P(X ≤ k) = Σ_{i=0}^{k} C(n,i) × p^i × (1−p)^(n−i)</p>
<p>Tools: Excel BINOM.DIST(), Python scipy.stats.binom, R pbinom()</p>
<h2>The Normal Approximation</h2>
<p>For large n (n > 30), the binomial distribution is well approximated by the normal distribution with: μ = np σ = √(np(1−p))</p>
<p>For 500 bets with p = 0.50: μ = 250, σ = √125 ≈ 11.2</p>
<p>P(winning at least 260 bets) = P(Z > (260−250)/11.2) = P(Z > 0.89) ≈ 18.7%</p>
<h2>Practical Applications</h2>
<ul>
<li>Calculating the probability of a specific winning or losing run</li>
<li>Determining whether your observed win rate is statistically significant</li>
<li>Setting realistic expectations for any binary-outcome market</li>
</ul>

<h2>Real-Data Benchmark: Sample Size, Variance &amp; Walk-Forward Stability</h2>
<p>Short-term win rates fool almost every bettor. Even on a fair 50/50 proposition (<code>2.00</code> odds), the 95% confidence interval around your observed win rate shrinks only with the <em>square root</em> of sample size (<code>&radic;n</code>):</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Settled Bets (n)</th><th>95% Win-Rate Confidence Band (at True 50%)</th><th>95% ROI Noise Band at 2.00 Odds</th><th>Statistical Verdict</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>25 bets</td><td>30.4% – 69.6%</td><td>&minus;39.2% to +39.2%</td><td>Pure noise — impossible to judge skill</td></tr>
    <tr><td>100 bets</td><td>40.2% – 59.8%</td><td>&minus;19.6% to +19.6%</td><td>Early directional signal only</td></tr>
    <tr><td>500 bets</td><td>45.6% – 54.4%</td><td>&minus;8.8% to +8.8%</td><td>Meaningful performance evaluation</td></tr>
    <tr><td>1,000 bets</td><td>46.9% – 53.1%</td><td>&minus;6.2% to +6.2%</td><td>Reliable statistical proof of edge</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Out-of-Sample Proof (PunterStat v2.0.0):</strong> Across 3 strict walk-forward seasons (never peeking into future matches), PunterStat''s model held steady Brier scores of <code>0.60460</code> (2022/23), <code>0.60983</code> (2023/24), and <code>0.60887</code> (2024/25+) — proving that true statistical validation requires out-of-sample testing over thousands of events, never cherry-picked hot streaks.</p>

<h2>Key Takeaway</h2>
<p><strong>The Binomial Distribution for Match Outcomes:</strong> Demand large out-of-sample evidence, respect regression to the mean, and calibrate probabilities against objective scoring rules like Brier score and CLV.</p>'
