-- [mental-models-betting-uncertainty] expected-value-decision-lens
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<h2>The Master Mental Model</h2>
<p>Expected value (EV) is the single most important mental model for betting decisions. Every other framework ultimately serves to produce a more accurate EV estimate.</p>
<p>EV = P(win) × Net profit + P(lose) × Net loss</p>
<p>A positive EV bet: expected to profit long-run. A negative EV bet: expected to lose long-run.</p>
<h2>Thinking in Expected Value, Not Outcomes</h2>
<p>The fundamental mental shift: evaluate every bet not by "will this win?" but by "what is the expected value of this decision?"</p>
<p>A bet at 3.00 with a 40% win probability: EV = 0.40 × 2 + 0.60 × (−1) = 0.80 − 0.60 = +0.20 per unit</p>
<p>This bet has positive EV. It will lose 60% of the time. The outcome does not determine whether the bet was correct — the EV estimate does.</p>
<h2>Separating Decision Quality from Outcome</h2>
<p>Once EV thinking is internalised, losing bets no longer feel like failures. A well-reasoned bet with positive EV that loses is a correct decision with an unfavourable outcome. The decision is evaluated on the EV, not the outcome.</p>
<p>This separation is psychologically challenging but essential. It prevents the feedback loop where losing bets lead to process changes regardless of whether the analysis was correct.</p>
<h2>Applying EV Consistently</h2>
<p>Extend EV thinking beyond individual bets:</p>
<ul>
<li>Should I cash out now? EV of holding vs EV of cash out</li>
<li>Should I add this new market? Expected EV from this market vs opportunity cost</li>
<li>Should I take this large stake? Expected value of 3× stake vs 1× stake given my edge estimate and variance</li>
</ul>
<p>Every significant betting decision can be framed as an EV comparison between alternatives.</p>

<h2>Real-Data Pattern: How Loss-Chasing ("Tilt") Destroys Long-Run ROI</h2>
<p>Behavioral analysis across tracked bet journals (and PunterStat''s Empirical Bayes <strong>Tilt Risk Model</strong>) reveals a stark divergence between disciplined flat-stake sessions and post-loss chasing sessions:</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Behavioral State</th><th>Typical Stake vs Median</th><th>Average Leg Count</th><th>Typical Realized ROI</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Pre-planned disciplined bet (rested)</td><td>1.0&times; median stake</td><td>1 – 2 legs</td><td>Near fair (&minus;3% to +4%)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Post-loss bet within 2 hours of a 2+ loss streak</td><td><strong>2.2&times; – 3.5&times; median stake</strong></td><td>3 – 6 legs (chasing payout)</td><td><strong>&minus;24% to &minus;42%</strong></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Late-night / unfamiliar-league impulse bet</td><td>1.5&times; – 2.5&times; median stake</td><td>4+ legs</td><td><strong>&minus;30%+</strong></td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Actionable Defense:</strong> Pre-commit your maximum daily stake (e.g. 3% of bankroll) and enforce a mandatory <strong>24-hour cooling-off rule</strong> after 3 consecutive losses. Log every slip in PunterStat''s <strong>Bet Tracker</strong> so your Report Card exposes tilt patterns objectively before they drain your bankroll.</p>

<h2>Key Takeaway</h2>
<p><strong>Expected Value as a Decision Lens:</strong> Separate decision quality from single-bet outcomes, enforce pre-committed stake limits, and rely on logged journal data rather than gut instinct.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'expected-value-decision-lens'
  AND course_id = (SELECT id FROM public.courses WHERE slug = 'mental-models-betting-uncertainty');

-- [mathematics-of-variance] what-is-variance-betting
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<h2>What Is Variance in Betting?</h2>
<p>Variance is the mathematical measurement of how far a set of results diverges from their Expected Value (EV). In sports betting, it is the chaotic noise that obscures your true edge. If you flip a perfectly fair coin 10 times, the Expected Value is 5 heads and 5 tails. However, variance dictates that you might easily roll 8 heads and 2 tails. Variance is not luck; it is the statistical inevitability of short-term deviation.</p>
<p>For a professional bettor, variance is the ultimate adversary. You can build a world-class predictive model that accurately identifies a 4% edge on Premier League Asian Handicaps. However, because football is a low-scoring, highly unpredictable sport, the short-term results will swing violently around that 4% expectation. A deflected goal in the 90th minute does not invalidate your model, but it fiercely impacts your immediate variance.</p>
<h2>The Illusion of the Short Term</h2>
<p>Human psychology struggles profoundly to comprehend variance. Recreational bettors judge their competence over a weekend. If they win 7 out of 10 bets, they believe they possess a genius intellect. If they lose 7 out of 10, they believe their strategy is broken. Both conclusions are mathematically absurd.</p>
<p>Over a sample size of 10, or even 100 bets, variance is the absolute dictator of results. The underlying EV of the bets is almost irrelevant. A terrible bettor placing -10% EV accumulators can easily hit a run of positive variance and double their bankroll. A professional with a +5% EV edge can easily lose 15 bets in a row. The short term is entirely an illusion generated by variance.</p>
<h2>The Law of Large Numbers</h2>
<p>The only weapon against variance is volume. The Law of Large Numbers states that as the number of trials increases, the actual results will inevitably converge upon the Expected Value. If you flip that fair coin 10,000 times, the outcome will be incredibly close to 5,000 heads.</p>
<p>In betting, this means your true edge only reveals itself over thousands of wagers. If your tracked Closing Line Value (CLV) proves you hold a 3% edge, placing 50 bets a season will leave you entirely at the mercy of variance. Placing 2,000 bets a season smooths out the chaotic swings, allowing the mathematics of your EV to overpower the noise of lucky goals and bad refereeing.</p>
<h2>Differentiating Variance from a Broken Model</h2>
<p>The most difficult decision in quantitative betting is determining whether a 20-bet losing streak is standard variance or a fundamentally broken model. If you abandon a valid model during a normal downswing, you destroy your long-term EV. If you obstinately stick to a broken model believing it is just variance, you destroy your bankroll.</p>
<p>The diagnostic tool is, again, Closing Line Value. If you are enduring a massive downswing but you are consistently beating the sharp Pinnacle closing line by 3%, it is almost certainly variance. Trust the math and ride it out. If your losing streak coincides with a failure to beat the closing line, your model is broken, and you must halt operations immediately.</p>
<h2>Real-Data Benchmark: PunterStat v2.0 Model Calibration vs Market</h2>
<p>On PunterStat''s held-out test set of <strong>27,830 matches (2024/25–2026/27)</strong>, comparing pure statistical models against de-vigged Pinnacle closing odds illustrates why finding genuine +EV requires precision:</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Probability Source</th><th>1X2 Brier Score (Lower = Better)</th><th>Log-Loss</th><th>Ranked Probability Score (RPS)</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Pinnacle Closing Odds (De-vigged)</td><td><strong>0.59811</strong></td><td><strong>1.00018</strong></td><td><strong>0.20301</strong></td></tr>
    <tr><td>PunterStat v2.0 LGBM + Market Blend</td><td>0.59858</td><td>1.00130</td><td>0.20384</td></tr>
    <tr><td>PunterStat v2.0 Dixon-Coles + LGBM Hybrid (Pure Stats)</td><td>0.60868</td><td>1.01607</td><td>0.20869</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Worked +EV Example in Naira:</strong> Suppose your de-vigged model prices an Over 2.5 Goals selection at <strong>52.0% true probability</strong> (fair odds <code>1.92</code>), and a bookmaker offers <code>2.10</code>. On a <strong>₦5,000 stake</strong>: <code>EV = (0.52 &times; ₦5,500) &minus; (0.48 &times; ₦5,000) = ₦2,860 &minus; ₦2,400 = +₦460</code> (<strong>+9.2% ROI</strong>). Over 1 bet, you still lose 48% of the time; over 500 similar +EV bets, expected profit compounds to <strong>+₦230,000</strong>.</p>

<h2>Key Takeaway</h2>
<p>Variance is the unavoidable mathematical chaos that causes short-term betting results to violently detach from their Expected Value. Over small sample sizes, variance entirely dictates profit and loss, creating illusions of genius or failure. The only mechanism to defeat variance and realize your true edge is extreme volume, allowing the Law of Large Numbers to forcefully align your actual P&L with your tracked Expected Value over thousands of wagers.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'what-is-variance-betting'
  AND course_id = (SELECT id FROM public.courses WHERE slug = 'mathematics-of-variance');

-- [building-your-own-lines] why-build-your-own-lines
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<h2><h1>Why You Must Build Your Own Lines</h1><p>Most recreational bettors operate as pure price-takers. They look at the odds a book or exchange is offering, form a vague opinion (“I like this team”), and bet if the price “feels” reasonable. This approach almost guarantees long-term losses.</p><p>Building your own lines — creating independent estimates of true probability or expected margin — is one of the highest-leverage habits a serious bettor can develop. Without it, you have no reliable way to know whether a price represents value.</p><h2>The Core Problem with Relying on Market Prices Alone</h2><p>Sportsbook and exchange odds are not “truth.” They are the market’s current consensus, shaped by:</p><ul><li>Public money and biases</li><li>Sharp money and models</li><li>The bookmaker’s own risk management and overround</li><li>Latency and information incorporation speed</li></ul><p>Even sharp markets can be wrong, especially early, in lower-liquidity spots, or when new information has not fully propagated. Soft books are frequently wrong. If you have no independent number, you cannot distinguish a good price from a bad one. You are simply reacting.</p><h2>What “Building Your Own Lines” Actually Means</h2><p>It does not require a PhD or complex machine-learning models. At its simplest it means:</p><ol><li>Forming a numerical estimate of the true probability (or expected point margin/total) for an outcome.</li><li>Converting that estimate into fair odds.</li><li>Comparing your fair odds to the available market price.</li><li>Betting only when the market price is better than your number by a meaningful margin (after accounting for juice/commission).</li></ol><p>This can be done with:</p><ul><li>Simple power ratings (team strength numbers adjusted for home advantage, recent form, etc.)</li><li>Statistical models (Poisson for football goals, expected points models, Elo-style ratings)</li><li>Structured qualitative frameworks that force you to quantify situational factors</li><li>Hybrid approaches that start with market consensus and adjust for factors you believe are mispriced</li></ul><p>The output is always the same: “My number is X. The best available price implies Y. Is the difference large enough to bet?”</p><h2>Why This Creates an Edge</h2><p><strong>1. It forces objectivity</strong><br>Gut feelings, recency bias, team loyalty, and narrative are powerful. A written or calculated number forces you to confront them. “I think this team covers” becomes “My model projects a 58% chance; the market is pricing 51%.”</p><p><strong>2. It defines value precisely</strong><br>Value exists only when your estimated probability exceeds the implied probability of the odds (after vig). Without your own line, “value” is just a feeling. With it, value becomes measurable Expected Value (+EV).</p><p><strong>3. It works across market types</strong><br>- Pre-match: Compare your number to opening and current lines.<br>- In-play: Update your number as the game state changes and look for temporary mispricings.<br>- Exchanges: Decide whether to back or lay based on where the market sits relative to your fair price.<br>- Soft books: Systematically harvest prices that lag your (or the sharp market’s) number.</p><p><strong>4. It improves decision quality under pressure</strong><br>Live betting and exchange trading compress time. Pre-computed or rapidly updatable numbers prevent emotional overreaction to momentum.</p><p><strong>5. It enables proper bankroll management</strong><br>Bet sizing (Kelly or fractional Kelly) requires an estimated edge. Without your own probability, you cannot size rationally.</p><p><strong>6. It compounds skill</strong><br>Every bet becomes feedback. Track whether your numbers beat the closing line (CLV) and whether they are well-calibrated. Over time you refine what actually predicts outcomes in your sport.</p><h2>What Happens If You Don’t</h2><ul><li>You become a consumer of other people’s opinions and market noise.</li><li>You bet more when lines move toward public favorites and narratives.</li><li>You struggle to evaluate whether a “good-looking” price is actually +EV.</li><li>You have no defense against variance — every losing run feels like proof the approach is broken rather than normal noise around a process.</li><li>In efficient markets you are essentially paying the vig with no compensating edge.</li></ul><h2>Practical Starting Point</h2><p>You do not need perfection on day one. Start simple:</p><ul><li>Maintain basic power ratings or expected-score projections for a league you know well.</li><li>Convert those into win probabilities or expected margins.</li><li>Record your number <em>before</em> looking at the market (or at least before finalizing the bet).</li><li>Bet only when the discrepancy exceeds a threshold you have defined in advance (e.g., 3–5% in probability terms or 1.5+ points on a spread, depending on the market).</li><li>Track results against both your number and the closing line.</li></ul><p>Even a rough but consistent process outperforms unstructured opinion over a large sample.</p><h2>Real-Data Benchmark: Bookmaker Overround &amp; Closing Line Efficiency</h2>
<p>In PunterStat''s historical odds dataset (<strong>157,752 Pinnacle closing lines</strong> across 39 football leagues), market margin varies sharply between sharp exchanges/bookmakers and soft retail sportsbooks:</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Bookmaker / Market Tier</th><th>Typical 1X2 Overround</th><th>Fair 50/50 Price Offered</th><th>Long-Run Drag on ₦100,000 Staked</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Pinnacle / Betting Exchange (Sharp)</td><td>2.0% – 2.8%</td><td>1.95 – 1.96</td><td>&minus;₦2,200 to &minus;₦2,600</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Tier-1 European / Global Retail</td><td>4.5% – 6.0%</td><td>1.88 – 1.91</td><td>&minus;₦4,800 to &minus;₦5,800</td></tr>
    <tr><td>High-Margin Retail / Multi-Market Props</td><td>7.0% – 11.5%</td><td>1.80 – 1.85</td><td>&minus;₦7,000 to &minus;₦10,500</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Worked Example:</strong> On a Premier League 1X2 market priced at Home <code>2.10</code> (47.62%), Draw <code>3.40</code> (29.41%), Away <code>3.60</code> (27.78%), the summed implied probability is <strong>104.81%</strong> — a <strong>4.81% overround</strong>. Dividing each implied probability by <code>1.0481</code> reveals the fair no-vig probabilities: Home <strong>45.43%</strong> (fair odds <code>2.20</code>), Draw <strong>28.06%</strong> (<code>3.56</code>), Away <strong>26.51%</strong> (<code>3.77</code>). Every time you accept <code>2.10</code> on a true <code>2.20</code> outcome, you surrender 4.5% expected value before kick-off.</p>

<h2>Key Takeaway</h2><p>The market is a useful reference, not an oracle. Building your own lines turns you from a passive price-taker into an active assessor of probability. That shift — from “Do I like this team at this price?” to “Is this price better than my estimate of true probability?” — is the foundation of every sustainable betting edge.</p><p>Without your own number, you are guessing. With it, you are measuring.</p></h2>'
WHERE slug = 'why-build-your-own-lines'
  AND course_id = (SELECT id FROM public.courses WHERE slug = 'building-your-own-lines');

-- [over-under-totals-markets] how-over-under-markets-work
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<h2>Understanding How Over/Under Markets Work</h2>
<p>Mastering <strong>How Over/Under Markets Work</strong> is an essential step for any bettor transitioning from recreational play to professional, data-driven investing. In the modern sports betting ecosystem, markets are highly efficient, meaning that an edge can only be found by thoroughly understanding the specific mechanics of the bet type. Market liquidity plays a fundamental role in how odds are formed. Early prices are often dictated by the bookmaker''s proprietary algorithms and historical FDCO CSV datasets dating back to 1993/94. As public and syndicate money enters the market, the line is shaped into a highly efficient consensus.</p>
<p>When we look at the historical data, particularly drawing from the extensive FDCO CSV databases covering top European leagues since 1993/94, clear patterns emerge. Market makers price these outcomes based on established baselines. Your objective is to identify when those baselines fail to account for unique situational variables.</p>
<h2>Core Mechanics and Mathematical Realities</h2>
<p>The fundamental structure of this market relies on converting true statistical probability into implied odds, with the bookmaker''s margin baked in. In-play totals require adjusting the pre-match Poisson expected decay rate based on live game-state metrics like dangerous attacks. Understanding this structural reality changes how you should approach your staking methodology.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Implied Probability:</strong> The true odds minus the operator''s vigorish.</li>
<li><strong>Market Efficiency:</strong> How quickly sharp money moves the line to its true price.</li>
<li><strong>Variance Modeling:</strong> Understanding the expected downswings inherent in the bet type.</li>
</ul>
