-- [handicap-and-asian-handicap] asian-handicap-core-mechanics
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<h2><h1>Asian Handicap: The Core Mechanics</h1><p>Asian Handicap (AH) is a form of spread betting designed to eliminate the draw from football matches. It works by applying a goal deficit or head start to one team before the match begins, then settling the bet based on the adjusted result. The defining feature is the use of quarter, half, and whole goal lines — each with distinct settlement rules.</p><h2>Line Structure and Settlement Rules</h2><h3>Whole Goal Lines (0, –1, +1, –2, +2, etc.)</h3><p>Whole lines introduce the possibility of a <strong>push</strong> (full refund) when the adjusted margin lands exactly on the line.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Line</th><th>Team Wins By</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>–1</td><td>2+ goals</td><td>Win</td></tr><tr><td>–1</td><td>exactly 1 goal</td><td>Push (full refund)</td></tr><tr><td>–1</td><td>draw or loss</td><td>Loss</td></tr><tr><td>+1</td><td>win or draw</td><td>Win</td></tr><tr><td>+1</td><td>lose by exactly 1</td><td>Push (full refund)</td></tr><tr><td>+1</td><td>lose by 2+</td><td>Loss</td></tr></tbody></table><h3>Half Goal Lines (–0.5, +0.5, –1.5, +1.5, etc.)</h3><p>Half lines remove the push. There is always a definitive win or loss.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Line</th><th>Team Wins By</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>–0.5</td><td>any win</td><td>Win</td></tr><tr><td>–0.5</td><td>draw or loss</td><td>Loss</td></tr><tr><td>+0.5</td><td>win or draw</td><td>Win</td></tr><tr><td>+0.5</td><td>loss</td><td>Loss</td></tr><tr><td>–1.5</td><td>win by 2+</td><td>Win</td></tr><tr><td>–1.5</td><td>win by 1, draw, or loss</td><td>Loss</td></tr><tr><td>+1.5</td><td>win, draw, or lose by 1</td><td>Win</td></tr><tr><td>+1.5</td><td>lose by 2+</td><td>Loss</td></tr></tbody></table><h3>Quarter Goal Lines (–0.25, +0.25, –0.75, +0.75, etc.)</h3><p>Quarter lines split the stake equally between two adjacent half lines. The bet is essentially two separate bets at half the stake each. This creates <strong>half-win</strong> and <strong>half-loss</strong> outcomes.</p><h4>–0.25 (split between 0 and –0.5)</h4><ul><li><strong>Team wins:</strong> Both halves win → Full win</li><li><strong>Draw:</strong> 0 pushes (refund), –0.5 loses → Half-loss</li><li><strong>Team loses:</strong> Both halves lose → Full loss</li></ul><h4>+0.25 (split between 0 and +0.5)</h4><ul><li><strong>Team wins:</strong> Both halves win → Full win</li><li><strong>Draw:</strong> 0 pushes (refund), +0.5 wins → Half-win</li><li><strong>Team loses:</strong> Both halves lose → Full loss</li></ul><h4>–0.75 (split between –0.5 and –1.0)</h4><ul><li><strong>Win by 2+:</strong> Both halves win → Full win</li><li><strong>Win by exactly 1:</strong> –0.5 wins, –1.0 pushes → Half-win</li><li><strong>Draw or loss:</strong> Both halves lose → Full loss</li></ul><h4>+0.75 (split between +0.5 and +1.0)</h4><ul><li><strong>Win or draw:</strong> Both halves win → Full win</li><li><strong>Lose by exactly 1:</strong> +0.5 loses, +1.0 pushes → Half-loss</li><li><strong>Lose by 2+:</strong> Both halves lose → Full loss</li></ul><h2>Settlement Matrix</h2><table><thead><tr><th>AH Line</th><th>Team Wins By 2+</th><th>Win By Exactly 1</th><th>Draw</th><th>Lose By Exactly 1</th><th>Lose By 2+</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>–0.25</td><td>Win</td><td>Win</td><td>Half-loss</td><td>Loss</td><td>Loss</td></tr><tr><td>–0.50</td><td>Win</td><td>Win</td><td>Loss</td><td>Loss</td><td>Loss</td></tr><tr><td>–0.75</td><td>Win</td><td>Half-win</td><td>Loss</td><td>Loss</td><td>Loss</td></tr><tr><td>–1.00</td><td>Win</td><td>Push</td><td>Loss</td><td>Loss</td><td>Loss</td></tr><tr><td>–1.25</td><td>Win</td><td>Half-loss</td><td>Loss</td><td>Loss</td><td>Loss</td></tr><tr><td>–1.50</td><td>Win</td><td>Loss</td><td>Loss</td><td>Loss</td><td>Loss</td></tr><tr><td>+0.25</td><td>Win</td><td>Win</td><td>Half-win</td><td>Loss</td><td>Loss</td></tr><tr><td>+0.50</td><td>Win</td><td>Win</td><td>Win</td><td>Loss</td><td>Loss</td></tr><tr><td>+0.75</td><td>Win</td><td>Win</td><td>Win</td><td>Half-loss</td><td>Loss</td></tr><tr><td>+1.00</td><td>Win</td><td>Win</td><td>Win</td><td>Push</td><td>Loss</td></tr><tr><td>+1.25</td><td>Win</td><td>Win</td><td>Win</td><td>Half-win</td><td>Loss</td></tr><tr><td>+1.50</td><td>Win</td><td>Win</td><td>Win</td><td>Win</td><td>Loss</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Key Settlement Patterns</h2><ul><li><strong>Half-win</strong> occurs when one half of the split bet wins and the other pushes.</li><li><strong>Half-loss</strong> occurs when one half loses and the other pushes.</li><li>On <strong>quarter lines</strong>, the stake is always divided equally between the two adjacent whole or half lines.</li><li>Pushes on whole lines return the full stake for that portion — on quarter lines, only the relevant portion pushes.</li><li>The settlement depends purely on the final score adjusted by the handicap, not on which team scores first or any in-game events.</li></ul><h2>Why the Mechanics Matter</h2><ul><li>Understanding settlement prevents mispriced bets caused by confusion between –0.5 and –0.25, or +1 and +1.25.</li><li>Quarter lines offer a safer alternative to whole lines when you expect a narrow outcome but want partial protection.</li><li>The ability to compare your projected margin to the exact handicap line is what makes Asian Handicap a precision tool for football analysis.</li><li>Pinnacle, SingBet, and other sharp bookmakers use Asian Handicap as their primary football market because the efficient line structure attracts informed money.</li></ul><p>Once these mechanics are second nature, Asian Handicap becomes the cleanest available language for expressing a football margin projection as a bet.</p></h2>

<h2>Key Takeaway</h2>
<p><strong>Asian Handicap: The Core Mechanics:</strong> Choose bet structures that minimize compounded overround and match your actual analytical edge rather than chasing flashy multi-leg payouts.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'asian-handicap-core-mechanics'
  AND course_id = (SELECT id FROM public.courses WHERE slug = 'handicap-and-asian-handicap');

-- [market-inefficiencies-deep-dive] researching-documenting-market-biases
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<h2>Researching and Documenting Market Biases</h2>
<p>Exploiting market inefficiencies requires moving beyond anecdotal observation and into rigorous, data-driven research. You cannot assume the market overvalues favorites; you must mathematically prove it. Researching and documenting market biases involves conducting massive historical backtests to identify systemic pricing errors that persist over multiple seasons.</p>
<p>The foundation of this research is a comprehensive historical odds database, capturing opening lines, closing lines, and exact outcomes across thousands of matches. The goal is to identify specific scenarios where the market''s implied probability consistently diverges from the actual historical frequency of the outcome.</p>
<h2>The Recency Bias Audit</h2>
<p>One of the most profound biases is Recency Bias. The public heavily over-weights the results of the last 3-5 matches. To document this, researchers query their database: "What is the historical ROI of blindly betting on a team that has lost 4 consecutive matches when playing at home against a team that has won 4 consecutive matches?"</p>
<p>The research often reveals a staggering inefficiency. The public heavily backs the in-form team, driving their odds down. However, the data proves that in highly competitive leagues like the Premier League, variance drives powerful regression to the mean. The team on the losing streak often holds immense, systemic value because the market has completely decoupled their price from their true underlying quality.</p>
<h2>Documenting the ''Derby'' Effect</h2>
<p>Local derbies and high-profile rivalry matches (e.g., El Clásico, the North London Derby) are magnets for behavioral bias. The narrative dictates that these matches are chaotic, aggressive, and unpredictable. Consequently, the public heavily bets the Over on goals and cards.</p>
<p>A rigorous audit of derivative markets often shows that bookmakers massively over-adjust for the ''Derby Effect''. The line for Total Cards might be inflated by 25% compared to a standard fixture. While derbies do produce slightly more cards on average, the data frequently proves that systematically betting the Under in these hyper-inflated markets yields a consistent, long-term ROI. The narrative outpaces the mathematics.</p>
<h2>Creating the Bias Ledger</h2>
<p>Successful betting operations do not rely on memory; they construct a rigid Bias Ledger. This document catalogs every verified inefficiency: "Serie A Relegation Candidates playing Away in May: Market undervalues by 4.2%", or "Bundesliga Teams post-Champions League Away Fixtures: Market overvalues by 3.8%."</p>
<p>This ledger is the operational playbook. When the weekend fixtures are released, the automated system cross-references the matches against the Bias Ledger. When a fixture triggers a documented bias, the system alerts the bettor to manually verify the underlying xG data before executing the trade.</p>
<h2>Real-Data Benchmark: PunterStat v2.0 Model Calibration vs Market</h2>
<p>On PunterStat''s held-out test set of <strong>27,830 matches (2024/25–2026/27)</strong>, comparing pure statistical models against de-vigged Pinnacle closing odds illustrates why finding genuine +EV requires precision:</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Probability Source</th><th>1X2 Brier Score (Lower = Better)</th><th>Log-Loss</th><th>Ranked Probability Score (RPS)</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Pinnacle Closing Odds (De-vigged)</td><td><strong>0.59811</strong></td><td><strong>1.00018</strong></td><td><strong>0.20301</strong></td></tr>
    <tr><td>PunterStat v2.0 LGBM + Market Blend</td><td>0.59858</td><td>1.00130</td><td>0.20384</td></tr>
    <tr><td>PunterStat v2.0 Dixon-Coles + LGBM Hybrid (Pure Stats)</td><td>0.60868</td><td>1.01607</td><td>0.20869</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Worked +EV Example in Naira:</strong> Suppose your de-vigged model prices an Over 2.5 Goals selection at <strong>52.0% true probability</strong> (fair odds <code>1.92</code>), and a bookmaker offers <code>2.10</code>. On a <strong>₦5,000 stake</strong>: <code>EV = (0.52 &times; ₦5,500) &minus; (0.48 &times; ₦5,000) = ₦2,860 &minus; ₦2,400 = +₦460</code> (<strong>+9.2% ROI</strong>). Over 1 bet, you still lose 48% of the time; over 500 similar +EV bets, expected profit compounds to <strong>+₦230,000</strong>.</p>

<h2>Key Takeaway</h2>
<p>Market biases cannot be exploited based on intuition; they must be mathematically proven through rigorous backtesting of historical odds databases. By explicitly documenting how the market systematically misprices scenarios heavily influenced by Recency Bias or media narratives (like the ''Derby Effect''), bettors can construct a tangible Bias Ledger. This ledger automates the identification of high-EV opportunities driven purely by human irrationality.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'researching-documenting-market-biases'
  AND course_id = (SELECT id FROM public.courses WHERE slug = 'market-inefficiencies-deep-dive');

-- [outright-and-futures-betting] how-to-model-outright-markets
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<h2>Understanding How to Model Outright Markets</h2>
<p>Mastering <strong>How to Model Outright Markets</strong> is an essential step for any bettor transitioning from recreational play to professional, data-driven investing. In the modern sports betting ecosystem, markets are highly efficient, meaning that an edge can only be found by thoroughly understanding the specific mechanics of the bet type. Understanding variance is critical. Even with a proven edge, drawdowns are inevitable. Proper bankroll management, typically a fractional Kelly Criterion approach, ensures that you can weather these downswings without risking ruin. This is particularly vital when operating in markets with inherently higher volatility.</p>
<p>When we look at the historical data, particularly drawing from the extensive FDCO CSV databases covering top European leagues since 1993/94, clear patterns emerge. Market makers price these outcomes based on established baselines. Your objective is to identify when those baselines fail to account for unique situational variables.</p>
<h2>Core Mechanics and Mathematical Realities</h2>
<p>The fundamental structure of this market relies on converting true statistical probability into implied odds, with the bookmaker''s margin baked in. Relegation markets provide a contrarian edge, as public money typically flows towards title contenders rather than poorly performing sides. Understanding this structural reality changes how you should approach your staking methodology.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Implied Probability:</strong> The true odds minus the operator''s vigorish.</li>
<li><strong>Market Efficiency:</strong> How quickly sharp money moves the line to its true price.</li>
<li><strong>Variance Modeling:</strong> Understanding the expected downswings inherent in the bet type.</li>
</ul>
<p>To approach this market systematically, bettors must divorce themselves from the entertainment value of the sport and focus strictly on the underlying math. The primary objective is identifying discrepancies between the bookmaker''s implied probability and the true probability of the event. Over a large sample size, these small mathematical advantages compound into significant ROI.</p>
<h2>Strategic Execution in the Market</h2>
<p>Executing a profitable strategy requires a deep understanding of market timing. Prices are dynamic. Outright markets inherently carry massive margins, often seeing a 120-130% overround on a standard 20-team league winner market. If you are operating at soft books, you face higher margins but slower line adjustments. Conversely, sharp exchanges offer better prices but hyper-efficient lines.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Market Scenario</th><th>Typical Overround</th><th>Bettor Edge Required</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Early Sharp Line (e.g. Pinnacle)</td><td>2.0% - 2.5%</td><td>High structural knowledge</td></tr>
<tr><td>Recreational Bookmaker</td><td>5.0% - 8.0%</td><td>Exploiting slow adjustments</td></tr>
<tr><td>Betting Exchange</td><td>4.5% - 5.0% (Comm)</td><td>Market making / Arbitrage</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Another key consideration is price origination. Rather than simply evaluating whether the offered odds ''look good'', you must build a model that outputs a true price. If your model outputs 2.00 (50%) and the market offers 2.20 (45.4%), you execute the bet. Understanding variance is critical. Even with a proven edge, drawdowns are inevitable. Proper bankroll management, typically a fractional Kelly Criterion approach, ensures that you can weather these downswings without risking ruin. This is particularly vital when operating in markets with inherently higher volatility.</p>
<h2>Advanced Considerations and Pitfalls</h2>
<p>One of the most significant pitfalls for bettors is confirmation bias—finding a narrative and cherry-picking stats to fit it. The bookmaker''s algorithm does not care about narratives; it strictly processes liability and historical data. Outright markets inherently carry massive margins, often seeing a 120-130% overround on a standard 20-team league winner market.</p>
<p>Furthermore, bankroll management cannot be overstated. Operating in this market without a strict fractional Kelly staking plan guarantees ruin over an infinite timeline. A common mistake made by recreational punters is failing to track closing line value (CLV). If your bets consistently beat the closing odds at sharp bookmakers, you hold a tangible edge, regardless of short-term variance. This principle is heavily applicable here, where pricing efficiency can fluctuate wildly before kickoff.</p>
<h2>Key Takeaway</h2>
<p><strong>Profitability in How to Model Outright Markets requires a mechanical approach. Outright markets inherently carry massive margins, often seeing a 120-130% overround on a standard 20-team league winner market. By understanding the structural inefficiencies of the market, you can exploit pricing errors systematically.</strong></p>'
WHERE slug = 'how-to-model-outright-markets'
  AND course_id = (SELECT id FROM public.courses WHERE slug = 'outright-and-futures-betting');

-- [professional-bankroll-operations] investor-capital-syndicate-structures
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<h2>Beyond Individual Capital</h2>
<p>Proven bettors with documented edge can access external capital — investor funds that amplify their operation''s scale while generating returns for investors.</p>
<h2>The Basic Syndicate Structure</h2>
<p>A betting syndicate pools capital from multiple members (or external investors) into a single managed bankroll. A designated manager (often the analyst with the validated edge) makes all betting decisions.</p>
<p>Common profit splits:</p>
<ul>
<li>50/50: manager and investors split profits equally</li>
<li>70/30: investors receive 70% of profits (higher capital, lower risk)</li>
<li>Performance fee: manager receives 0% base + 20% of profits above a hurdle rate</li>
</ul>
<h2>The Due Diligence Requirement</h2>
<p>Credible investors require documentation before committing capital:</p>
<ul>
<li>2+ years of audited bet records</li>
<li>CLV analysis by strategy and sport</li>
<li>Risk management policy document</li>
<li>Reference from trusted third party</li>
</ul>
<p>Investors who do not require this documentation are not serious — and you should be suspicious of anyone willing to invest without it.</p>
<h2>Legal and Regulatory Considerations</h2>
<p>In many jurisdictions, operating a managed betting fund may constitute regulated financial activity. In the UK, managing other people''s money for gambling purposes sits outside FCA regulation — but contractual documentation (partnership agreements, profit-sharing agreements) is essential.</p>
<p>Consult a solicitor before accepting investor capital to structure the arrangement appropriately.</p>
<h2>The Capital Advantage</h2>
<p>With ₦100,000 under management vs ₦10,000 personal capital (same 3% annual ROI):</p>
<ul>
<li>Personal: ₦300 annual profit</li>
<li>Syndicate: ₦3,000 annual profit (before performance fee)</li>
</ul>
<p>The scale advantage is significant. Managing external capital well is one of the highest-leverage activities in professional betting.</p>

<h2>Real-Data Simulation: Comparing Staking Systems on a ₦100,000 Bankroll</h2>
<p>Assume a skilled bettor has a genuine <strong>+5% ROI edge</strong> betting at <code>2.00</code> average odds (52.5% true win rate) over 500 bets. How does staking strategy affect growth and Risk of Ruin?</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Staking Method</th><th>Stake Size on ₦100,000</th><th>Expected Bankroll (500 Bets)</th><th>Max Drawdown Risk</th><th>Risk of Ruin (&gt;80% Loss)</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Flat Staking (1% unit)</td><td>₦1,000 / bet</td><td>~₦125,000</td><td>Low (~12–18%)</td><td>&lt; 0.1%</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Quarter-Kelly (0.25&times; Kelly)</td><td>₦1,250 (scales dynamically)</td><td>~₦133,000</td><td>Moderate (~15–22%)</td><td>&lt; 0.1%</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Full Kelly (1.0&times; Kelly)</td><td>₦5,000 (5% on 5% edge)</td><td>~₦185,000 (median)</td><td>Severe (~45–60%)</td><td>High if edge overestimated</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Martingale (Double after loss)</td><td>₦1k &rarr; ₦2k &rarr; ₦4k &rarr; ₦128k</td><td>Eventual Blowup</td><td><strong>100% wipeout on 7-loss run</strong></td><td><strong>&gt; 75% within 500 bets</strong></td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Practical Rule:</strong> Even with a 52.5% win rate, a <strong>7-bet losing streak</strong> has a ~65% probability of occurring at least once in 500 bets. Flat 1–2% units or Quarter-Kelly survive those inevitable downswings cleanly; Martingale and stake-chasing guarantee ruin.</p>

<h2>Key Takeaway</h2>
<p><strong>Investor Capital and Syndicate Structures:</strong> Protect your capital first: consistent unit sizing (1–2% flat or fractional Kelly) ensures inevitable losing streaks never end your betting operation.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'investor-capital-syndicate-structures'
  AND course_id = (SELECT id FROM public.courses WHERE slug = 'professional-bankroll-operations');

-- [building-a-predictive-edge] data-strategy-predictive-betting
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<h2>Data Strategy for Predictive Betting</h2>
<p>The bedrock of any predictive betting model is its data strategy. In the modern betting landscape, simply having access to data is not a competitive advantage; the sharp market has access to everything. A profitable data strategy revolves around curation, contextualization, and the rigorous elimination of noise. If your data inputs are flawed, your Expected Value calculations will be mathematically toxic.</p>
<p>A professional data strategy moves beyond basic outcomes (goals, shots, possession) and focuses exclusively on predictive metrics. Expected Goals (xG) is the foundation, but a robust strategy segments xG. It isolates non-penalty xG (npxG), separates open-play xG from set-piece xG, and crucially, adjusts all metrics for game state. A team trailing 2-0 will naturally accumulate massive possession and xG as the opponent sits deep; failing to adjust for this "score effect" heavily artificially inflates the losing team''s underlying metrics.</p>
<h2>The Importance of Granularity</h2>
<p>High-level models require granular, event-level data. Instead of looking at match totals, algorithms ingest every single pass, tackle, and shot, stamped with exact pitch coordinates. This allows for the calculation of Expected Threat (xT)—measuring how much a player''s pass or dribble increased the team''s probability of scoring, even if no shot occurred.</p>
<p>By relying on xT and deep territorial metrics like Field Tilt (the share of final-third passes), a model can identify teams that are structurally dominant but suffering from short-term finishing variance. When a team relentlessly controls the dangerous areas of the pitch but fails to score, the recreational market abandons them. The granular data strategy identifies the underlying dominance and flags the team as a massive value proposition.</p>
<h2>Data Scrubbing and Outlier Management</h2>
<p>Raw data is inherently noisy. A red card in the 10th minute completely destroys the predictive validity of the remaining 80 minutes of data for both teams. A professional data strategy mandates brutal data scrubbing. Matches heavily distorted by early red cards, extreme weather (like flooded pitches), or farcical refereeing decisions are either entirely excluded from the model''s rolling averages or heavily down-weighted.</p>
<p>If you feed a machine learning algorithm unscrubbed data containing massive outliers, the algorithm will attempt to learn from the noise, permanently corrupting its probability outputs. Your model is only as intelligent as the cleanliness of the data it consumes.</p>
<h2>Integrating Qualitative Variables</h2>
<p>While quantitative data is king, a true predictive edge requires integrating qualitative variables into the numerical matrix. This involves feature engineering data points like travel distance, days of rest, managerial changes, and key player absences.</p>
<p>A sophisticated model does not just look at Team A vs Team B. It evaluates "Team A playing their 3rd match in 7 days after a 2,000-mile European away trip" versus "Team B operating on full 7-day rest." By converting these qualitative scenarios into quantitative modifiers, the model generates highly contextualized probabilities that expose the blind spots of the bookmaker''s baseline algorithms.</p>
<h2>Real-Data Benchmark: PunterStat v2.0 Model Calibration vs Market</h2>
<p>On PunterStat''s held-out test set of <strong>27,830 matches (2024/25–2026/27)</strong>, comparing pure statistical models against de-vigged Pinnacle closing odds illustrates why finding genuine +EV requires precision:</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Probability Source</th><th>1X2 Brier Score (Lower = Better)</th><th>Log-Loss</th><th>Ranked Probability Score (RPS)</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Pinnacle Closing Odds (De-vigged)</td><td><strong>0.59811</strong></td><td><strong>1.00018</strong></td><td><strong>0.20301</strong></td></tr>
    <tr><td>PunterStat v2.0 LGBM + Market Blend</td><td>0.59858</td><td>1.00130</td><td>0.20384</td></tr>
    <tr><td>PunterStat v2.0 Dixon-Coles + LGBM Hybrid (Pure Stats)</td><td>0.60868</td><td>1.01607</td><td>0.20869</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Worked +EV Example in Naira:</strong> Suppose your de-vigged model prices an Over 2.5 Goals selection at <strong>52.0% true probability</strong> (fair odds <code>1.92</code>), and a bookmaker offers <code>2.10</code>. On a <strong>₦5,000 stake</strong>: <code>EV = (0.52 &times; ₦5,500) &minus; (0.48 &times; ₦5,000) = ₦2,860 &minus; ₦2,400 = +₦460</code> (<strong>+9.2% ROI</strong>). Over 1 bet, you still lose 48% of the time; over 500 similar +EV bets, expected profit compounds to <strong>+₦230,000</strong>.</p>

<h2>Key Takeaway</h2>
<p>A profitable predictive model relies entirely on a sophisticated Data Strategy that prioritizes granular, contextualized metrics like Expected Threat (xT) and game-state adjusted xG. By ruthlessly scrubbing the data to remove noisy outliers (like early red cards) and integrating qualitative variables such as severe fixture congestion, bettors can construct a mathematically pure foundation that consistently outperforms the public market.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'data-strategy-predictive-betting'
  AND course_id = (SELECT id FROM public.courses WHERE slug = 'building-a-predictive-edge');

-- [building-predictive-models] data-collection-management
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<h2>The Data Foundation</h2>
<p>Your model is only as good as the data it is trained on. Data quality, completeness, and appropriate scope are the most important determinants of model quality — more important than model complexity.</p>
<h2>What Data You Need</h2>
<p>For a football goals model:</p>
<p><strong>Match-level data:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Date, competition, home team, away team</li>
<li>Full-time score</li>
<li>Match xG (home and away) — per shot</li>
<li>Match xGA (home and away conceded)</li>
<li>Pre-match odds (opening and closing)</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Team-level data (derived from match data):</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Rolling xG scored per match (5, 10, 15-match windows)</li>
<li>Rolling xGA per match (5, 10, 15-match windows)</li>
<li>Home and away splits</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Contextual data:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Days rest before match</li>
<li>Match significance (cup, league, European)</li>
<li>Stadium capacity / attendance (where available)</li>
<li>Weather data (for outdoor stadiums)</li>
</ul>
<h2>Free Data Sources</h2>
<p>| Source | Coverage | Format | |---|---|---| | FBref | Top 5 EU leagues + major comps | Web scraping / CSV download | | Understat | Premier League, Bundesliga, La Liga, Serie A, Ligue 1, RFPL | JSON via API | | football-data.co.uk | 30+ leagues, historical back to 1990s | CSV download | | The Odds Portal | Historical odds | Web scraping | | OpenWeatherMap | Historical weather | API (free tier) |</p>
<h2>Data Management Best Practices</h2>
<ul>
<li>Store data in a structured database (SQLite for solo projects, PostgreSQL for teams)</li>
<li>Automate data collection with weekly scripts</li>
<li>Maintain a data changelog — when data was collected, from what source, and any known gaps</li>
<li>Never overwrite raw data — always maintain the original as collected</li>
</ul>

<h2>Real-Data Benchmark: Sample Size, Variance &amp; Walk-Forward Stability</h2>
<p>Short-term win rates fool almost every bettor. Even on a fair 50/50 proposition (<code>2.00</code> odds), the 95% confidence interval around your observed win rate shrinks only with the <em>square root</em> of sample size (<code>&radic;n</code>):</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Settled Bets (n)</th><th>95% Win-Rate Confidence Band (at True 50%)</th><th>95% ROI Noise Band at 2.00 Odds</th><th>Statistical Verdict</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>25 bets</td><td>30.4% – 69.6%</td><td>&minus;39.2% to +39.2%</td><td>Pure noise — impossible to judge skill</td></tr>
    <tr><td>100 bets</td><td>40.2% – 59.8%</td><td>&minus;19.6% to +19.6%</td><td>Early directional signal only</td></tr>
    <tr><td>500 bets</td><td>45.6% – 54.4%</td><td>&minus;8.8% to +8.8%</td><td>Meaningful performance evaluation</td></tr>
    <tr><td>1,000 bets</td><td>46.9% – 53.1%</td><td>&minus;6.2% to +6.2%</td><td>Reliable statistical proof of edge</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Out-of-Sample Proof (PunterStat v2.0.0):</strong> Across 3 strict walk-forward seasons (never peeking into future matches), PunterStat''s model held steady Brier scores of <code>0.60460</code> (2022/23), <code>0.60983</code> (2023/24), and <code>0.60887</code> (2024/25+) — proving that true statistical validation requires out-of-sample testing over thousands of events, never cherry-picked hot streaks.</p>

<h2>Key Takeaway</h2>
<p><strong>Data Collection and Management:</strong> Demand large out-of-sample evidence, respect regression to the mean, and calibrate probabilities against objective scoring rules like Brier score and CLV.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'data-collection-management'
  AND course_id = (SELECT id FROM public.courses WHERE slug = 'building-predictive-models');

-- [live-in-play-bet-types] next-goal-markets
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<h2>Understanding Next Goal Markets: How to Approach Them</h2>
<p>Mastering <strong>Next Goal Markets: How to Approach Them</strong> is an essential step for any bettor transitioning from recreational play to professional, data-driven investing. In the modern sports betting ecosystem, markets are highly efficient, meaning that an edge can only be found by thoroughly understanding the specific mechanics of the bet type. A common mistake made by recreational punters is failing to track closing line value (CLV). If your bets consistently beat the closing odds at sharp bookmakers, you hold a tangible edge, regardless of short-term variance. This principle is heavily applicable here, where pricing efficiency can fluctuate wildly before kickoff.</p>
<p>When we look at the historical data, particularly drawing from the extensive FDCO CSV databases covering top European leagues since 1993/94, clear patterns emerge. Market makers price these outcomes based on established baselines. Your objective is to identify when those baselines fail to account for unique situational variables.</p>
<h2>Core Mechanics and Mathematical Realities</h2>
<p>The fundamental structure of this market relies on converting true statistical probability into implied odds, with the bookmaker''s margin baked in. Live spread betting requires modeling momentum; expected possession and territory metrics become far more predictive than pre-match form. Understanding this structural reality changes how you should approach your staking methodology.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Implied Probability:</strong> The true odds minus the operator''s vigorish.</li>
<li><strong>Market Efficiency:</strong> How quickly sharp money moves the line to its true price.</li>
<li><strong>Variance Modeling:</strong> Understanding the expected downswings inherent in the bet type.</li>
</ul>
<p>Understanding variance is critical. Even with a proven edge, drawdowns are inevitable. Proper bankroll management, typically a fractional Kelly Criterion approach, ensures that you can weather these downswings without risking ruin. This is particularly vital when operating in markets with inherently higher volatility.</p>
<h2>Strategic Execution in the Market</h2>
<p>Executing a profitable strategy requires a deep understanding of market timing. Prices are dynamic. Correct Score markets live are extremely sensitive to time decay; the probability of the current score holding increases exponentially late in the game. If you are operating at soft books, you face higher margins but slower line adjustments. Conversely, sharp exchanges offer better prices but hyper-efficient lines.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Market Scenario</th><th>Typical Overround</th><th>Bettor Edge Required</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Early Sharp Line (e.g. Pinnacle)</td><td>2.0% - 2.5%</td><td>High structural knowledge</td></tr>
<tr><td>Recreational Bookmaker</td><td>5.0% - 8.0%</td><td>Exploiting slow adjustments</td></tr>
<tr><td>Betting Exchange</td><td>4.5% - 5.0% (Comm)</td><td>Market making / Arbitrage</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Another key consideration is price origination. Rather than simply evaluating whether the offered odds ''look good'', you must build a model that outputs a true price. If your model outputs 2.00 (50%) and the market offers 2.20 (45.4%), you execute the bet. Market liquidity plays a fundamental role in how odds are formed. Early prices are often dictated by the bookmaker''s proprietary algorithms and historical FDCO CSV datasets dating back to 1993/94. As public and syndicate money enters the market, the line is shaped into a highly efficient consensus.</p>
<h2>Advanced Considerations and Pitfalls</h2>
<p>One of the most significant pitfalls for bettors is confirmation bias—finding a narrative and cherry-picking stats to fit it. The bookmaker''s algorithm does not care about narratives; it strictly processes liability and historical data. Cashing out early almost always involves taking a secondary margin hit, often costing the bettor 4-10% in expected value compared to letting it ride.</p>
<p>Furthermore, bankroll management cannot be overstated. Operating in this market without a strict fractional Kelly staking plan guarantees ruin over an infinite timeline. To approach this market systematically, bettors must divorce themselves from the entertainment value of the sport and focus strictly on the underlying math. The primary objective is identifying discrepancies between the bookmaker''s implied probability and the true probability of the event. Over a large sample size, these small mathematical advantages compound into significant ROI.</p>
<h2>Key Takeaway</h2>
<p><strong>The data dictates the strategy for Next Goal Markets: How to Approach Them. Cashing out early almost always involves taking a secondary margin hit, often costing the bettor 4-10% in expected value compared to letting it ride. Always rely on robust backtesting using verified datasets rather than intuition or narrative.</strong></p>'
WHERE slug = 'next-goal-markets'
  AND course_id = (SELECT id FROM public.courses WHERE slug = 'live-in-play-bet-types');

-- [understanding-odds-formats] from-odds-to-probability
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<img src="/tactical-workstation.jpg" alt="Probability conversion matrix on analyst screen" />
      <h2>Stop Asking "Who Will Win?" — Start Asking "What Percentage Does This Price Imply?"</h2>
      <p>Recreational punters ask: <em>"Will Liverpool beat Aston Villa?"</em> Analytical bettors ask: <em>"Liverpool is priced at 1.65 (60.6% implied probability). Does Liverpool actually win this specific matchup more than 61% of the time?"</em></p>
      <h3>Worked UEFA Champions League 1X2 Market Example</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Inter Milan Win (1) @ 2.10:</strong> <code>(1 ÷ 2.10) × 100 = 47.62%</code></li>
        <li><strong>Draw (X) @ 3.45:</strong> <code>(1 ÷ 3.45) × 100 = 28.99%</code></li>
        <li><strong>Arsenal Win (2) @ 3.60:</strong> <code>(1 ÷ 3.60) × 100 = 27.78%</code></li>
      </ul>
      <p>Sum of all three outcomes: <code>47.62% + 28.99% + 27.78% = 104.39%</code>. That extra <strong>4.39% above 100%</strong> is the bookmaker''s built-in commission (Overround).</p>

<h2>Real-Data Benchmark: Bookmaker Overround &amp; Closing Line Efficiency</h2>
<p>In PunterStat''s historical odds dataset (<strong>157,752 Pinnacle closing lines</strong> across 39 football leagues), market margin varies sharply between sharp exchanges/bookmakers and soft retail sportsbooks:</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Bookmaker / Market Tier</th><th>Typical 1X2 Overround</th><th>Fair 50/50 Price Offered</th><th>Long-Run Drag on ₦100,000 Staked</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Pinnacle / Betting Exchange (Sharp)</td><td>2.0% – 2.8%</td><td>1.95 – 1.96</td><td>&minus;₦2,200 to &minus;₦2,600</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Tier-1 European / Global Retail</td><td>4.5% – 6.0%</td><td>1.88 – 1.91</td><td>&minus;₦4,800 to &minus;₦5,800</td></tr>
    <tr><td>High-Margin Retail / Multi-Market Props</td><td>7.0% – 11.5%</td><td>1.80 – 1.85</td><td>&minus;₦7,000 to &minus;₦10,500</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Worked Example:</strong> On a Premier League 1X2 market priced at Home <code>2.10</code> (47.62%), Draw <code>3.40</code> (29.41%), Away <code>3.60</code> (27.78%), the summed implied probability is <strong>104.81%</strong> — a <strong>4.81% overround</strong>. Dividing each implied probability by <code>1.0481</code> reveals the fair no-vig probabilities: Home <strong>45.43%</strong> (fair odds <code>2.20</code>), Draw <strong>28.06%</strong> (<code>3.56</code>), Away <strong>26.51%</strong> (<code>3.77</code>). Every time you accept <code>2.10</code> on a true <code>2.20</code> outcome, you surrender 4.5% expected value before kick-off.</p>

<h2>Key Takeaway</h2>
<p><strong>Converting Any Bookmaker Price into Implied Probability Before You Stake:</strong> Always convert offered odds into de-vigged fair probability, compare across bookmakers, and measure your decisions against the sharp closing line.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'from-odds-to-probability'
  AND course_id = (SELECT id FROM public.courses WHERE slug = 'understanding-odds-formats');

-- [implied-probability-explained] the-overround-explained
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<h2>What the overround is and why it exists</h2>
<p>The overround (also called the vig, juice, or margin) is the mechanism through which bookmakers guarantee long-run profit. It works by pricing all possible outcomes in a market above their true probability, so the sum of all implied probabilities in any market exceeds 100%.</p>
<p>In a perfectly fair market on a coin flip, both outcomes would be priced at 2.00 — each implying exactly 50%, summing to 100%. A bookmaker prices both at 1.91 — each implying 52.4%, summing to 104.8%. The 4.8% above 100% is the overround.</p>
<p>The bookmaker does not care which side wins. If equal money arrives on both outcomes, the overround guarantees profit regardless of the result. In practice, books rarely have perfectly balanced books, but risk management tools allow them to hedge their exposure.</p>

<h2>Calculating the overround step by step</h2>
<p>For any market, the calculation is the same:</p>
<ol>
<li>Convert every outcome''s price to implied probability (1 ÷ decimal)</li>
<li>Sum all implied probabilities</li>
<li>The sum minus 100% is the overround</li>
</ol>
<p>For the three-outcome football market priced at 2.10 / 3.40 / 3.60:</p>
<ul>
<li>47.62% + 29.41% + 27.78% = <strong>104.81%</strong></li>
<li>Overround: <strong>4.81%</strong></li>
</ul>
<p>The long-run implication: a bettor who places equal stakes across all three outcomes (₦1 each, ₦3 total) receives back on average ₦3 × (100 ÷ 104.81) = <strong>₦2.86</strong>. The ₦0.14 loss on every ₦3 staked is the overround working against them.</p>

<h2>What the overround costs you per bet</h2>
<p>At a 5% overround, for every ₦100 staked, your expected return in a neutral market is ₦95. The remaining ₦5 is the bookmaker''s margin.</p>
<p>This is why even a bettor who picks winners at exactly the true probability will lose over time. To profit, you need to identify outcomes where your estimated probability <em>exceeds</em> the bookmaker''s implied probability — and by enough to overcome the margin.</p>
<p>A crude way to understand the break-even requirement: at a 5% margin, you need to identify 5% of value (on average) just to break even. At a 2% margin (Pinnacle-level), you only need 2% of edge to cover costs. This is why market selection is as important as selection quality.</p>

<h2>How margins vary across bookmakers and market types</h2>
<p>Our match_odds dataset contains prices from multiple bookmakers (Bet365, Pinnacle, William Hill, VC Bet, and others) for the same match. Comparing the overround across bookmakers on the same market is one of the most instructive exercises in understanding how the industry is structured:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Pinnacle (PS in our dataset):</strong> Typically 1.5–2.5% on major football 1X2 markets. The industry benchmark for low margin.</li>
<li><strong>Bet365 (B365):</strong> Typically 4–7% on standard markets. Higher margins offset by broader market availability and promotional offers.</li>
<li><strong>William Hill, VC Bet:</strong> Generally 5–8% on core markets. Higher still on secondary markets and specials.</li>
</ul>
<p>You can verify this directly in our dataset: for any match, calculate the overround for each bookmaker''s home/draw/away prices and compare. The pattern is consistent across thousands of matches.</p>

<h2>How margin compounds in accumulators</h2>
<p>Each leg of an accumulator multiplies the margins together. If each leg carries a 5% overround, the effective margin after five legs is:</p>
<p>1 − (0.952 × 0.952 × 0.952 × 0.952 × 0.952) = 1 − 0.776 = <strong>22.4%</strong></p>
<p>For a 10-leg accumulator at 5% per leg, the effective margin approaches 40%. This is the primary reason bookmakers actively promote accumulator betting — it is one of the highest-margin products they offer. Every recreational bettor who places weekly accumulators is paying a compounded margin that makes long-run profitability virtually impossible.</p>

<h2>Asian handicap: the low-margin alternative</h2>
<p>Asian handicap markets are two-outcome markets (the draw is eliminated by the half-goal handicap). With only two outcomes to price, bookmakers typically offer margins of 2–4% on major matches — significantly lower than the three-outcome 1X2 market. This is one reason sharp bettors heavily prefer Asian handicap: lower costs mean a smaller edge is required to be profitable.</p>

<h2>Real-Data Benchmark: PunterStat v2.0 Model Calibration vs Market</h2>
<p>On PunterStat''s held-out test set of <strong>27,830 matches (2024/25–2026/27)</strong>, comparing pure statistical models against de-vigged Pinnacle closing odds illustrates why finding genuine +EV requires precision:</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Probability Source</th><th>1X2 Brier Score (Lower = Better)</th><th>Log-Loss</th><th>Ranked Probability Score (RPS)</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Pinnacle Closing Odds (De-vigged)</td><td><strong>0.59811</strong></td><td><strong>1.00018</strong></td><td><strong>0.20301</strong></td></tr>
    <tr><td>PunterStat v2.0 LGBM + Market Blend</td><td>0.59858</td><td>1.00130</td><td>0.20384</td></tr>
    <tr><td>PunterStat v2.0 Dixon-Coles + LGBM Hybrid (Pure Stats)</td><td>0.60868</td><td>1.01607</td><td>0.20869</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Worked +EV Example in Naira:</strong> Suppose your de-vigged model prices an Over 2.5 Goals selection at <strong>52.0% true probability</strong> (fair odds <code>1.92</code>), and a bookmaker offers <code>2.10</code>. On a <strong>₦5,000 stake</strong>: <code>EV = (0.52 &times; ₦5,500) &minus; (0.48 &times; ₦5,000) = ₦2,860 &minus; ₦2,400 = +₦460</code> (<strong>+9.2% ROI</strong>). Over 1 bet, you still lose 48% of the time; over 500 similar +EV bets, expected profit compounds to <strong>+₦230,000</strong>.</p>

<h2>Key Takeaway</h2>
<p>The overround is not a technicality — it is a structural tax on every bet you place, and its size varies enormously by bookmaker and market type. Understanding it transforms you from a passive price-taker into someone who can evaluate the real cost of each bet and choose markets accordingly.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'the-overround-explained'
  AND course_id = (SELECT id FROM public.courses WHERE slug = 'implied-probability-explained');

-- [cognitive-biases-in-betting] confirmation-bias-and-loss-aversion
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<h2>Confirmation Bias</h2>
<p>Confirmation bias is the tendency to search for, interpret, and remember information in a way that confirms what you already believe.</p>
<p>If you think a team will win, you subconsciously discount evidence to the contrary and amplify evidence in your favour. You read the team news focusing on the good (key striker fit) and minimise the bad (three key defenders out).</p>
<p><strong>In practice:</strong> After placing a bet, bettors often stop evaluating impartially. The analysis ends the moment the bet is placed. The discipline is to continue questioning your position right up to kick-off.</p>
<h2>Loss Aversion</h2>
<p>Daniel Kahneman''s research showed that the pain of a loss is roughly twice as powerful as the pleasure of an equivalent gain. Losing ₦50 hurts about as much as gaining ₦100 feels good.</p>
<p>This has direct betting consequences:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Chasing losses:</strong> After a bad run, bettors increase stakes to recoup faster — compressing the time horizon and increasing risk of ruin.</li>
<li><strong>Cutting winners short:</strong> Bettors cash out winning bets early to lock in profit, even when the expected value of holding is higher.</li>
<li><strong>Avoiding correct bets:</strong> Bettors refuse to back a team they previously lost on, even when the price is genuinely good.</li>
</ul>
<h2>The Antidote</h2>
<p>Think in expected value, not outcomes. A bet with positive EV is correct regardless of whether it wins. A bet with negative EV is wrong regardless of whether it wins. Detach from individual results — track performance over hundreds of bets, not tens.</p>

<h2>Real-Data Pattern: How Loss-Chasing ("Tilt") Destroys Long-Run ROI</h2>
<p>Behavioral analysis across tracked bet journals (and PunterStat''s Empirical Bayes <strong>Tilt Risk Model</strong>) reveals a stark divergence between disciplined flat-stake sessions and post-loss chasing sessions:</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Behavioral State</th><th>Typical Stake vs Median</th><th>Average Leg Count</th><th>Typical Realized ROI</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Pre-planned disciplined bet (rested)</td><td>1.0&times; median stake</td><td>1 – 2 legs</td><td>Near fair (&minus;3% to +4%)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Post-loss bet within 2 hours of a 2+ loss streak</td><td><strong>2.2&times; – 3.5&times; median stake</strong></td><td>3 – 6 legs (chasing payout)</td><td><strong>&minus;24% to &minus;42%</strong></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Late-night / unfamiliar-league impulse bet</td><td>1.5&times; – 2.5&times; median stake</td><td>4+ legs</td><td><strong>&minus;30%+</strong></td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Actionable Defense:</strong> Pre-commit your maximum daily stake (e.g. 3% of bankroll) and enforce a mandatory <strong>24-hour cooling-off rule</strong> after 3 consecutive losses. Log every slip in PunterStat''s <strong>Bet Tracker</strong> so your Report Card exposes tilt patterns objectively before they drain your bankroll.</p>

<h2>Key Takeaway</h2>
<p><strong>Confirmation Bias & Loss Aversion:</strong> Separate decision quality from single-bet outcomes, enforce pre-committed stake limits, and rely on logged journal data rather than gut instinct.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'confirmation-bias-and-loss-aversion'
  AND course_id = (SELECT id FROM public.courses WHERE slug = 'cognitive-biases-in-betting');

-- [singles-doubles-accumulators] doubles-how-combined-bets-work
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<h2><h1>The Double Bet: Linking Two Selections</h1><h2>Core Definition</h2><p>A double (sometimes called a "double bet" or "two-fold") is the simplest form of combined bet. You link exactly two selections into one wager. Both selections must win for the double to pay out. If either loses, the entire bet is lost.</p><ul><li>Two independent selections (from the same event or different events).</li><li>One stake covering both.</li><li>Odds of the two selections are multiplied together.</li><li>The bet wins only if both outcomes occur.</li><li>It is the first step up from a single and the smallest type of accumulator/multiple.</li></ul><p>Doubles sit between singles (one selection) and larger accumulators (three or more legs, often called trebles, four-folds, etc.).</p><hr><h2>How a Double Works</h2><ol><li>Choose two selections and note their individual odds.</li><li>The bookmaker multiplies the odds:<br>Combined odds = Odds of Selection A × Odds of Selection B</li><li>Enter your stake once.</li><li>Both events must finish in your favour.</li></ol><p><strong>Payout calculation (decimal odds):</strong></p><p>Total return = Stake × Combined odds</p><p>Profit = Total return − Stake</p><p>If one selection wins and the other loses (or is void in certain cases), the whole double usually loses. Some bookmakers treat a void leg by reducing the double to a single on the remaining selection, but this depends on the operator''s rules.</p><hr><h2>Clear Examples</h2><p><strong>Football double – Match winners</strong></p><p>Selection 1: Arsenal to win at 1.80<br>Selection 2: Liverpool to win at 2.10<br>Combined odds = 1.80 × 2.10 = 3.78<br>Stake: ₦10</p><p>If both teams win → Return = ₦10 × 3.78 = ₦37.80 (profit ₦27.80)<br>If either team fails to win → Stake is lost.</p><p><strong>Mixed sports double</strong></p><p>Selection 1: Horse A to win a race at 3.00<br>Selection 2: Tennis player B to win a match at 1.50<br>Combined odds = 3.00 × 1.50 = 4.50<br>Stake: ₦20</p><p>Both win → Return = ₦90 (profit ₦70).</p><p><strong>Same-game double (where allowed)</strong></p><p>Some bookmakers permit two selections from the same match (e.g., Team A to win and Over 2.5 goals). Rules and availability vary; always check the terms.</p><hr><h2>Doubles vs Singles vs Larger Accumulators</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Single</th><th>Double</th><th>Treble / Larger Acca</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Selections</td><td>1</td><td>2</td><td>3 or more</td></tr><tr><td>All must win?</td><td>Only the one</td><td>Yes (both)</td><td>Yes (all)</td></tr><tr><td>Odds calculation</td><td>The given price</td><td>Multiply the two prices</td><td>Multiply all prices</td></tr><tr><td>Risk</td><td>Lowest</td><td>Medium</td><td>Higher</td></tr><tr><td>Potential return</td><td>Modest</td><td>Higher than a single</td><td>Can be much larger</td></tr><tr><td>Independence</td><td>Fully independent</td><td>Linked – one loss kills it</td><td>Fully linked</td></tr></tbody></table><hr><h2>Advantages of Doubles</h2><ul><li>Higher potential returns than singles for a modest increase in risk.</li><li>Still relatively simple to understand and track.</li><li>Useful when you have two strong, independent opinions.</li><li>Can be part of system bets (e.g., a "Yankee" includes multiple doubles).</li><li>Good middle ground for recreational bettors who want more excitement than singles without the higher risk of big accumulators.</li></ul><hr><h2>Potential Drawbacks</h2><ul><li>One wrong selection voids the entire stake (no partial payout in a standard double).</li><li>Bookmaker margin is applied to both legs, so the combined price is less favourable than two separate singles in the long run.</li><li>Requires two correct predictions instead of one.</li><li>Correlation risk: if the two selections are related (e.g., both from the same match or heavily influenced by the same factors), the true probability of both winning may be lower than the multiplied odds suggest.</li></ul><hr><h2>Practical Tips for Using Doubles</h2><ul><li>Prefer independent selections (different matches or sports) to reduce correlation.</li><li>Look for value on both legs rather than just combining two favourites.</li><li>Calculate the combined odds yourself before placing the bet so you understand the true price.</li><li>Consider staking: many bettors use a smaller stake on doubles than on singles because of the higher risk.</li><li>Check settlement rules for voids, postponements, or dead-heats.</li><li>Track results separately from singles so you can evaluate whether doubles are profitable for your style.</li><li>Some bookmakers offer "Bet Builders" or same-game parlays that function similarly to doubles but with more complex pricing and rules.</li></ul><hr><h2>Key Takeaway</h2><p>A double combines two selections into one bet. The odds are multiplied, the stake is placed once, and both outcomes must be correct for a payout. It offers a step up in potential returns from a single while remaining far simpler than larger accumulators.</p><p>Always gamble responsibly. Only stake what you can afford to lose, and remember that the house edge applies to every leg of a combined bet.</p></h2>'
WHERE slug = 'doubles-how-combined-bets-work'
  AND course_id = (SELECT id FROM public.courses WHERE slug = 'singles-doubles-accumulators');

-- [discipline-and-record-keeping] tilt-chasing-and-knowing-when-to-stop
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<h2>What is Tilt?</h2>
<p>The term comes from poker: a player on "tilt" has let emotion override decision-making. In betting, tilt typically follows a losing run. The emotional response — frustration, urgency, the need to recoup — takes over from the analytical one.</p>
<h2>Signs You Are on Tilt</h2>
<ul>
<li>Placing bets you would not normally place to "get back to even"</li>
<li>Increasing stake sizes after losses without a strategy-based reason</li>
<li>Betting on markets you do not normally bet</li>
<li>Placing bets very quickly without your usual research process</li>
<li>Feeling that you "deserve" a winner after a run of losses</li>
</ul>
<h2>The Mechanics of a Downswing</h2>
<p>Even a genuinely profitable bettor with a 5% edge will experience losing runs of 10, 15, or 20 bets. This is not a malfunction — it is the nature of probability. The bettor who survives a downswing intact is the one who has planned for it in advance.</p>
<h2>Pre-Commitment Rules</h2>
<p>The most effective protection is pre-commitment: rules you set when you are calm that govern your behaviour when you are not.</p>
<p>Common examples:</p>
<ul>
<li>"If I lose more than X units in a week, I stop betting for the rest of the week"</li>
<li>"I never increase my standard stake by more than 20% in response to a losing run"</li>
<li>"I review my last 20 bets before placing any bet after 5 consecutive losses"</li>
</ul>
<p>Write your rules down. The point is that future-you, under emotional pressure, cannot override the rule without explicitly acknowledging they are doing so.</p>

<h2>Real-Data Pattern: How Loss-Chasing ("Tilt") Destroys Long-Run ROI</h2>
<p>Behavioral analysis across tracked bet journals (and PunterStat''s Empirical Bayes <strong>Tilt Risk Model</strong>) reveals a stark divergence between disciplined flat-stake sessions and post-loss chasing sessions:</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Behavioral State</th><th>Typical Stake vs Median</th><th>Average Leg Count</th><th>Typical Realized ROI</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Pre-planned disciplined bet (rested)</td><td>1.0&times; median stake</td><td>1 – 2 legs</td><td>Near fair (&minus;3% to +4%)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Post-loss bet within 2 hours of a 2+ loss streak</td><td><strong>2.2&times; – 3.5&times; median stake</strong></td><td>3 – 6 legs (chasing payout)</td><td><strong>&minus;24% to &minus;42%</strong></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Late-night / unfamiliar-league impulse bet</td><td>1.5&times; – 2.5&times; median stake</td><td>4+ legs</td><td><strong>&minus;30%+</strong></td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Actionable Defense:</strong> Pre-commit your maximum daily stake (e.g. 3% of bankroll) and enforce a mandatory <strong>24-hour cooling-off rule</strong> after 3 consecutive losses. Log every slip in PunterStat''s <strong>Bet Tracker</strong> so your Report Card exposes tilt patterns objectively before they drain your bankroll.</p>

<h2>Key Takeaway</h2>
<p><strong>Tilt, Chasing & Knowing When to Stop:</strong> Separate decision quality from single-bet outcomes, enforce pre-committed stake limits, and rely on logged journal data rather than gut instinct.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'tilt-chasing-and-knowing-when-to-stop'
  AND course_id = (SELECT id FROM public.courses WHERE slug = 'discipline-and-record-keeping');

-- [sample-size-and-variance] standard-deviation-and-downswings
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<h2>Standard Deviation in Betting</h2>
<p>Standard deviation (SD) measures the spread of results around the average. In betting, it tells you how wild the swings will be.</p>
<p>For a flat-staking bettor at odds of 2.0 (evens), the SD per bet is approximately 1 unit. Over 100 bets, the SD of total profit is approximately √100 = 10 units. This means a one-standard-deviation downswing at breakeven means being 10 units down over 100 bets — through pure luck.</p>
<h2>What a Realistic Losing Run Looks Like</h2>
<p>A bettor with a genuine 5% edge, betting at average odds of 2.0:</p>
<ul>
<li>Expected profit over 100 bets at 1 unit/bet: +5 units</li>
<li>Standard deviation: ≈ 10 units</li>
<li>One SD below expectation: –5 units (a 5-unit loss despite a real edge)</li>
<li>Two SDs below: –15 units</li>
</ul>
<p>Losing runs of 15–20 consecutive bets can happen even with a genuine edge. This is not a reason to panic — it is a reason to have planned for it.</p>
<h2>Using This Knowledge</h2>
<p>Before starting any system, calculate the expected drawdown range. If your bankroll cannot withstand a 2 SD downswing without going broke, your stakes are too high. Most professionals plan for a 3 SD downswing to be safe.</p>
<p>This calculation is the foundation of bankroll management — it connects the variance of your strategy to the stake sizes that keep you in the game.</p>

<h2>Real-Data Benchmark: Sample Size, Variance &amp; Walk-Forward Stability</h2>
<p>Short-term win rates fool almost every bettor. Even on a fair 50/50 proposition (<code>2.00</code> odds), the 95% confidence interval around your observed win rate shrinks only with the <em>square root</em> of sample size (<code>&radic;n</code>):</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Settled Bets (n)</th><th>95% Win-Rate Confidence Band (at True 50%)</th><th>95% ROI Noise Band at 2.00 Odds</th><th>Statistical Verdict</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>25 bets</td><td>30.4% – 69.6%</td><td>&minus;39.2% to +39.2%</td><td>Pure noise — impossible to judge skill</td></tr>
    <tr><td>100 bets</td><td>40.2% – 59.8%</td><td>&minus;19.6% to +19.6%</td><td>Early directional signal only</td></tr>
    <tr><td>500 bets</td><td>45.6% – 54.4%</td><td>&minus;8.8% to +8.8%</td><td>Meaningful performance evaluation</td></tr>
    <tr><td>1,000 bets</td><td>46.9% – 53.1%</td><td>&minus;6.2% to +6.2%</td><td>Reliable statistical proof of edge</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Out-of-Sample Proof (PunterStat v2.0.0):</strong> Across 3 strict walk-forward seasons (never peeking into future matches), PunterStat''s model held steady Brier scores of <code>0.60460</code> (2022/23), <code>0.60983</code> (2023/24), and <code>0.60887</code> (2024/25+) — proving that true statistical validation requires out-of-sample testing over thousands of events, never cherry-picked hot streaks.</p>

<h2>Key Takeaway</h2>
<p><strong>Standard Deviation & What a Downswing Looks Like:</strong> Demand large out-of-sample evidence, respect regression to the mean, and calibrate probabilities against objective scoring rules like Brier score and CLV.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'standard-deviation-and-downswings'
  AND course_id = (SELECT id FROM public.courses WHERE slug = 'sample-size-and-variance');

-- [regression-to-the-mean] avoiding-statistics-traps
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<h2>Cherry-Picking (Data Dredging)</h2>
<p>If you look at enough statistics, you will always find one that supports any claim. "This team wins 80% of home games on Tuesdays in February when it has rained in the previous week" — technically accurate, statistically meaningless.</p>
<p>Data dredging produces patterns that are entirely due to chance. The safeguard: form your hypothesis first, then check the data. Do not browse data looking for patterns and then treat what you find as a discovery.</p>
<h2>Survivorship Bias</h2>
<p>You hear about systems that worked. You do not hear about the 50 systems tried by the same person that failed. You read about the tipster who has had a great six months. You do not see the 200 tipsters who tried and gave up.</p>
<p>Survivorship bias inflates apparent performance of everything you can observe, because only the survivors are observable.</p>
<h2>Correlation vs Causation</h2>
<p>Two statistics that move together do not necessarily influence each other. Classic sports example: teams that score first win 70% of matches. Does scoring first cause winning? Partly — but mostly, better teams are more likely to score first and more likely to win. The true cause is team quality, not the act of scoring first.</p>
<p>Betting on "next team to score" at kick-off based on this correlation misidentifies the mechanism.</p>
<h2>Small Sample Percentages</h2>
<p>"This referee cards 3.2 players per game" — based on 12 games. A 95% confidence interval around that estimate is enormous. The true rate could be anywhere from 1.8 to 4.6 and still be consistent with the observed data. Treat any percentage derived from fewer than 30 events with significant scepticism.</p>

<h2>Real-Data Benchmark: Sample Size, Variance &amp; Walk-Forward Stability</h2>
<p>Short-term win rates fool almost every bettor. Even on a fair 50/50 proposition (<code>2.00</code> odds), the 95% confidence interval around your observed win rate shrinks only with the <em>square root</em> of sample size (<code>&radic;n</code>):</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Settled Bets (n)</th><th>95% Win-Rate Confidence Band (at True 50%)</th><th>95% ROI Noise Band at 2.00 Odds</th><th>Statistical Verdict</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>25 bets</td><td>30.4% – 69.6%</td><td>&minus;39.2% to +39.2%</td><td>Pure noise — impossible to judge skill</td></tr>
    <tr><td>100 bets</td><td>40.2% – 59.8%</td><td>&minus;19.6% to +19.6%</td><td>Early directional signal only</td></tr>
    <tr><td>500 bets</td><td>45.6% – 54.4%</td><td>&minus;8.8% to +8.8%</td><td>Meaningful performance evaluation</td></tr>
    <tr><td>1,000 bets</td><td>46.9% – 53.1%</td><td>&minus;6.2% to +6.2%</td><td>Reliable statistical proof of edge</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Out-of-Sample Proof (PunterStat v2.0.0):</strong> Across 3 strict walk-forward seasons (never peeking into future matches), PunterStat''s model held steady Brier scores of <code>0.60460</code> (2022/23), <code>0.60983</code> (2023/24), and <code>0.60887</code> (2024/25+) — proving that true statistical validation requires out-of-sample testing over thousands of events, never cherry-picked hot streaks.</p>

<h2>Key Takeaway</h2>
<p><strong>Avoiding the Statistics Traps:</strong> Demand large out-of-sample evidence, respect regression to the mean, and calibrate probabilities against objective scoring rules like Brier score and CLV.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'avoiding-statistics-traps'
  AND course_id = (SELECT id FROM public.courses WHERE slug = 'regression-to-the-mean');

-- [risk-management-ruin-theory] max-drawdown-bankroll-reserve
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<h2>Drawdown vs Ruin</h2>
<p>Risk of ruin is the ultimate disaster — bankroll → 0. But there is a lesser disaster that stops most bettors before reaching zero: a drawdown large enough to cause psychological or financial abandonment of the strategy.</p>
<h2>The Expected Maximum Drawdown</h2>
<p>For a given staking strategy, the expected maximum drawdown over N bets is approximately:</p>
<p>E[Max Drawdown] ≈ 2 × σ_per_bet × √N × (some constant depending on distribution)</p>
<p>A simplified version: E[Max Drawdown] ≈ 2 × stake × √N at 50% win probability.</p>
<p>For 500 bets at ₦25 stake: E[Max Drawdown] ≈ 2 × ₦25 × √500 ≈ 2 × ₦25 × 22.4 ≈ ₦1,120</p>
<p>This means you should expect to experience a drawdown of roughly ₦1,120 at some point during 500 bets, even with genuine positive edge.</p>
<h2>The Bankroll Reserve Principle</h2>
<p>Your operational bankroll (available for betting) should be separate from a reserve that is never staked. The reserve absorbs the expected maximum drawdown while the operational bankroll continues running.</p>
<p>Conservative split: 70% operational, 30% reserve.</p>
<p>At total bankroll ₦2,000: ₦1,400 operational, ₦600 reserve. If operational bankroll drops to ₦800: top up to ₦1,400 from reserve. This prevents forced stake reduction from temporary variance.</p>
<h2>The Reserve Replenishment Rule</h2>
<p>After topping up from reserve, do not restore the reserve until the operational bankroll has recovered to its previous peak. The reserve should only be consumed by variance, not replenished through further downside.</p>
<h2>The Reserve as Psychological Insurance</h2>
<p>Knowing you have a reserve eliminates one category of fear during downswings — the fear that this run will bankrupt you. This psychological benefit makes the reserve worthwhile even if you never need to use it.</p>

<h2>Real-Data Simulation: Comparing Staking Systems on a ₦100,000 Bankroll</h2>
<p>Assume a skilled bettor has a genuine <strong>+5% ROI edge</strong> betting at <code>2.00</code> average odds (52.5% true win rate) over 500 bets. How does staking strategy affect growth and Risk of Ruin?</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Staking Method</th><th>Stake Size on ₦100,000</th><th>Expected Bankroll (500 Bets)</th><th>Max Drawdown Risk</th><th>Risk of Ruin (&gt;80% Loss)</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Flat Staking (1% unit)</td><td>₦1,000 / bet</td><td>~₦125,000</td><td>Low (~12–18%)</td><td>&lt; 0.1%</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Quarter-Kelly (0.25&times; Kelly)</td><td>₦1,250 (scales dynamically)</td><td>~₦133,000</td><td>Moderate (~15–22%)</td><td>&lt; 0.1%</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Full Kelly (1.0&times; Kelly)</td><td>₦5,000 (5% on 5% edge)</td><td>~₦185,000 (median)</td><td>Severe (~45–60%)</td><td>High if edge overestimated</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Martingale (Double after loss)</td><td>₦1k &rarr; ₦2k &rarr; ₦4k &rarr; ₦128k</td><td>Eventual Blowup</td><td><strong>100% wipeout on 7-loss run</strong></td><td><strong>&gt; 75% within 500 bets</strong></td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Practical Rule:</strong> Even with a 52.5% win rate, a <strong>7-bet losing streak</strong> has a ~65% probability of occurring at least once in 500 bets. Flat 1–2% units or Quarter-Kelly survive those inevitable downswings cleanly; Martingale and stake-chasing guarantee ruin.</p>

<h2>Key Takeaway</h2>
<p><strong>Maximum Drawdown and Bankroll Reserve:</strong> Protect your capital first: consistent unit sizing (1–2% flat or fractional Kelly) ensures inevitable losing streaks never end your betting operation.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'max-drawdown-bankroll-reserve'
  AND course_id = (SELECT id FROM public.courses WHERE slug = 'risk-management-ruin-theory');

-- [how-bookmaker-margins-work] calculating-the-margin-step-by-step
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<h2><h1>Calculating the Margin Step by Step</h1><p>The bookmaker''s margin (also called overround or vigorish) is the percentage by which the sum of the implied probabilities of all outcomes in a market exceeds 100%. Here is the exact process.</p><h2>Step 1: Convert Each Set of Odds into Implied Probability</h2><p>Use the formula that matches the odds format you are given.</p><p><strong>Decimal odds</strong> (most common internationally):<br>Implied probability = 1 ÷ decimal odds</p><p><strong>American odds</strong>:</p><ul><li>Positive odds (e.g. +150): 100 ÷ (odds + 100)</li><li>Negative odds (e.g. –150): |odds| ÷ (|odds| + 100)</li></ul><p><strong>Fractional odds</strong> (e.g. 5/2):<br>Implied probability = denominator ÷ (denominator + numerator)</p><h2>Step 2: Add All Implied Probabilities Together</h2><p>Sum the implied probabilities of every possible outcome in the market.<br>For a two-way market there are two numbers; for a three-way market there are three, and so on.</p><h2>Step 3: Calculate the Margin</h2><p>Margin (overround) = (Sum of implied probabilities – 1) × 100</p><p>Alternatively:<br>Margin = (Sum of implied probabilities × 100) – 100</p><p>The result is expressed as a percentage.</p><h2>Worked Examples</h2><p><strong>Example 1 – Two-way moneyline (decimal odds)</strong><br>Team A: 1.91<br>Team B: 1.95</p><ul><li>Implied probability Team A = 1 / 1.91 ≈ 0.5236 (52.36%)</li><li>Implied probability Team B = 1 / 1.95 ≈ 0.5128 (51.28%)</li><li>Sum = 0.5236 + 0.5128 = 1.0364</li><li>Margin = (1.0364 – 1) × 100 = <strong>3.64%</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Example 2 – Three-way soccer match (decimal odds)</strong><br>Home: 2.10<br>Draw: 3.40<br>Away: 3.60</p><ul><li>Home = 1 / 2.10 ≈ 0.4762</li><li>Draw = 1 / 3.40 ≈ 0.2941</li><li>Away = 1 / 3.60 ≈ 0.2778</li><li>Sum = 0.4762 + 0.2941 + 0.2778 = 1.0481</li><li>Margin = (1.0481 – 1) × 100 = <strong>4.81%</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Example 3 – American odds</strong><br>Team A: –110<br>Team B: –110</p><ul><li>Implied probability each side = 110 / (110 + 100) = 0.5238</li><li>Sum = 0.5238 + 0.5238 = 1.0476</li><li>Margin = (1.0476 – 1) × 100 = <strong>4.76%</strong></li></ul><h2>Quick Reference Formulas</h2><ul><li>Two outcomes, equal decimal odds d:<br>Margin ≈ (2 ÷ d − 1) × 100</li><li>General case:<br>Margin = (Σ 1 ÷ oddsᵢ − 1) × 100</li></ul><h2>Practical Tips</h2><ul><li>Always use the exact odds offered (including any small differences between sides).</li><li>For multi-way markets (outrights, player props with many options), include every listed outcome.</li><li>Rounding to two decimal places for probabilities is usually sufficient for practical purposes.</li><li>Lower margin = better value for the bettor. Comparing margins across books is one of the simplest forms of line shopping.</li></ul><h2>Real-Data Benchmark: Bookmaker Overround &amp; Closing Line Efficiency</h2>
<p>In PunterStat''s historical odds dataset (<strong>157,752 Pinnacle closing lines</strong> across 39 football leagues), market margin varies sharply between sharp exchanges/bookmakers and soft retail sportsbooks:</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Bookmaker / Market Tier</th><th>Typical 1X2 Overround</th><th>Fair 50/50 Price Offered</th><th>Long-Run Drag on ₦100,000 Staked</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Pinnacle / Betting Exchange (Sharp)</td><td>2.0% – 2.8%</td><td>1.95 – 1.96</td><td>&minus;₦2,200 to &minus;₦2,600</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Tier-1 European / Global Retail</td><td>4.5% – 6.0%</td><td>1.88 – 1.91</td><td>&minus;₦4,800 to &minus;₦5,800</td></tr>
    <tr><td>High-Margin Retail / Multi-Market Props</td><td>7.0% – 11.5%</td><td>1.80 – 1.85</td><td>&minus;₦7,000 to &minus;₦10,500</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Worked Example:</strong> On a Premier League 1X2 market priced at Home <code>2.10</code> (47.62%), Draw <code>3.40</code> (29.41%), Away <code>3.60</code> (27.78%), the summed implied probability is <strong>104.81%</strong> — a <strong>4.81% overround</strong>. Dividing each implied probability by <code>1.0481</code> reveals the fair no-vig probabilities: Home <strong>45.43%</strong> (fair odds <code>2.20</code>), Draw <strong>28.06%</strong> (<code>3.56</code>), Away <strong>26.51%</strong> (<code>3.77</code>). Every time you accept <code>2.10</code> on a true <code>2.20</code> outcome, you surrender 4.5% expected value before kick-off.</p>

<h2>Key Takeaway</h2><p>Converting odds → summing the implied probabilities → subtracting 1 and converting to a percentage gives you the exact bookmaker margin on any market. Once you can do this quickly, you can instantly judge how competitive a set of odds is and how high a hurdle your edge must clear.</p></h2>'
WHERE slug = 'calculating-the-margin-step-by-step'
  AND course_id = (SELECT id FROM public.courses WHERE slug = 'how-bookmaker-margins-work');

-- [portfolio-bankroll-allocation] allocating-capital-across-strategies
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<h2>The Allocation Decision</h2>
<p>How much of your total bankroll should each strategy receive? The answer depends on:</p>
<ol>
<li>The strategy''s validated edge (higher edge → more allocation)</li>
<li>The strategy''s variance (higher variance → less allocation)</li>
<li>The strategy''s correlation with other strategies (lower correlation → more allocation OK)</li>
</ol>
<h2>The Sharpe Ratio Framework</h2>
<p>The Sharpe ratio (EV / standard deviation) measures risk-adjusted return. For betting:</p>
<p>Strategy Sharpe = Expected ROI / Standard deviation of ROI</p>
<p>Allocate proportionally to Sharpe ratio across strategies. A strategy with Sharpe 2.0 receives twice the allocation of a strategy with Sharpe 1.0.</p>
<p>This is the institutional asset allocation framework applied to betting.</p>
<h2>Simplified Allocation Rules</h2>
<p>For bettors without full Sharpe ratio calculations:</p>
<p><strong>Stage 1 — Entry allocation:</strong> New strategies receive 15% of total bankroll until 300 bets validate edge. <strong>Stage 2 — Proven allocation:</strong> Validated strategies with positive CLV receive 20–35% based on qualitative edge assessment. <strong>Stage 3 — Core allocation:</strong> Strategies with 1,000+ bets of positive CLV become core allocations (up to 50%).</p>
<p>Maximum in any single strategy: 60% (concentration risk limit).</p>
<h2>The Rebalancing Rule</h2>
<p>Review allocations quarterly. Strategies that have produced consistently better results than initial estimates: increase allocation (up to their category maximum). Strategies with deteriorating CLV: reduce allocation or remove.</p>
<h2>Cash Buffer</h2>
<p>Always maintain 10–15% of total bankroll as an unallocated cash buffer. This:</p>
<ul>
<li>Provides liquidity for rapid deployment on unexpected high-EV opportunities</li>
<li>Acts as the operational reserve for account float management</li>
<li>Prevents being "fully deployed" with no response capacity</li>
</ul>

<h2>Real-Data Simulation: Comparing Staking Systems on a ₦100,000 Bankroll</h2>
<p>Assume a skilled bettor has a genuine <strong>+5% ROI edge</strong> betting at <code>2.00</code> average odds (52.5% true win rate) over 500 bets. How does staking strategy affect growth and Risk of Ruin?</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Staking Method</th><th>Stake Size on ₦100,000</th><th>Expected Bankroll (500 Bets)</th><th>Max Drawdown Risk</th><th>Risk of Ruin (&gt;80% Loss)</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Flat Staking (1% unit)</td><td>₦1,000 / bet</td><td>~₦125,000</td><td>Low (~12–18%)</td><td>&lt; 0.1%</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Quarter-Kelly (0.25&times; Kelly)</td><td>₦1,250 (scales dynamically)</td><td>~₦133,000</td><td>Moderate (~15–22%)</td><td>&lt; 0.1%</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Full Kelly (1.0&times; Kelly)</td><td>₦5,000 (5% on 5% edge)</td><td>~₦185,000 (median)</td><td>Severe (~45–60%)</td><td>High if edge overestimated</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Martingale (Double after loss)</td><td>₦1k &rarr; ₦2k &rarr; ₦4k &rarr; ₦128k</td><td>Eventual Blowup</td><td><strong>100% wipeout on 7-loss run</strong></td><td><strong>&gt; 75% within 500 bets</strong></td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Practical Rule:</strong> Even with a 52.5% win rate, a <strong>7-bet losing streak</strong> has a ~65% probability of occurring at least once in 500 bets. Flat 1–2% units or Quarter-Kelly survive those inevitable downswings cleanly; Martingale and stake-chasing guarantee ruin.</p>

<h2>Key Takeaway</h2>
<p><strong>Allocating Capital Across Strategies:</strong> Protect your capital first: consistent unit sizing (1–2% flat or fractional Kelly) ensures inevitable losing streaks never end your betting operation.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'allocating-capital-across-strategies'
  AND course_id = (SELECT id FROM public.courses WHERE slug = 'portfolio-bankroll-allocation');

-- [finding-value-bets] expected-value-formula
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<h2>The Expected Value Formula</h2>
<p>Expected Value (EV) is the foundational equation of professional betting. It calculates the average amount a bettor can expect to win or lose per bet if the exact same wager were placed an infinite number of times. Understanding and applying the EV formula transforms betting from a game of intuition into an exercise in quantitative finance.</p>
<p>The formula is elegant and precise: <code>EV = (Probability of Winning × Amount Won per bet) - (Probability of Losing × Amount Lost per bet)</code>. To execute this formula, you must have absolute confidence in your modeled probability, as even a 2% miscalculation can flip a positive EV bet into a negative one.</p>
<h2>Calculating EV in Practice</h2>
<p>Let’s apply the formula to a Premier League fixture. You are staking ₦100 on Aston Villa to win at odds of 3.50. The bookmaker''s implied probability is 28.5%. However, your xG-based Poisson model calculates Aston Villa''s true probability of winning at 33% (0.33).</p>
<p>Your potential profit is ₦250 (3.50 * ₦100 - ₦100). The probability of losing is 67% (1.00 - 0.33).<br>
<code>EV = (0.33 × ₦250) - (0.67 × ₦100)</code><br>
<code>EV = ₦82.50 - ₦67.00 = +₦15.50</code><br>
This bet has an Expected Value of +₦15.50. For every ₦100 you place on this exact scenario over thousands of iterations, you will yield an average profit of ₦15.50. This is a massive 15.5% edge over the market.</p>
<h2>The Impact of the Bookmaker''s Margin on EV</h2>
<p>The bookmaker''s margin guarantees that the baseline Expected Value for an average punter is always negative. If you place random bets into a market with a 5% margin, your long-term EV will converge to exactly -5%. The bookmaker’s edge is mathematically insulated against luck.</p>
<p>To overcome this, your edge (the difference between your calculated true probability and the implied odds) must exceed the margin. If Pinnacle prices an Asian Handicap with a 2% margin, you only need a 2.1% informational edge to generate positive EV. If you bet the same market at a soft book with an 8% margin, you need an exceptionally strong, and rare, 8.1% edge just to break even. This is why professionals obsess over securing the best possible price.</p>
<h2>Positive EV vs ROI</h2>
<p>It is vital to distinguish between Expected Value (+EV) and Return on Investment (ROI). EV is a theoretical, forward-looking metric calculated before an event occurs. ROI is a backward-looking metric detailing actual realized profit. In small sample sizes, a bettor exclusively taking +EV bets can suffer a negative ROI due to variance and downswings.</p>
<p>If you flip a coin that lands on heads 55% of the time, the EV is overwhelmingly positive. However, you could easily hit a streak of five tails. Your ROI drops to -100% momentarily, but the EV remains untouched. Trusting the EV formula through the inevitable turbulence of variance is the ultimate psychological test for a professional bettor.</p>
<h2>Real-Data Benchmark: PunterStat v2.0 Model Calibration vs Market</h2>
<p>On PunterStat''s held-out test set of <strong>27,830 matches (2024/25–2026/27)</strong>, comparing pure statistical models against de-vigged Pinnacle closing odds illustrates why finding genuine +EV requires precision:</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Probability Source</th><th>1X2 Brier Score (Lower = Better)</th><th>Log-Loss</th><th>Ranked Probability Score (RPS)</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Pinnacle Closing Odds (De-vigged)</td><td><strong>0.59811</strong></td><td><strong>1.00018</strong></td><td><strong>0.20301</strong></td></tr>
    <tr><td>PunterStat v2.0 LGBM + Market Blend</td><td>0.59858</td><td>1.00130</td><td>0.20384</td></tr>
    <tr><td>PunterStat v2.0 Dixon-Coles + LGBM Hybrid (Pure Stats)</td><td>0.60868</td><td>1.01607</td><td>0.20869</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Worked +EV Example in Naira:</strong> Suppose your de-vigged model prices an Over 2.5 Goals selection at <strong>52.0% true probability</strong> (fair odds <code>1.92</code>), and a bookmaker offers <code>2.10</code>. On a <strong>₦5,000 stake</strong>: <code>EV = (0.52 &times; ₦5,500) &minus; (0.48 &times; ₦5,000) = ₦2,860 &minus; ₦2,400 = +₦460</code> (<strong>+9.2% ROI</strong>). Over 1 bet, you still lose 48% of the time; over 500 similar +EV bets, expected profit compounds to <strong>+₦230,000</strong>.</p>

<h2>Key Takeaway</h2>
<p>The Expected Value formula <code>(Win Probability × Potential Profit) - (Loss Probability × Stake)</code> is the absolute measure of a bet''s worth. A positive EV guarantees long-term profit, while a negative EV guarantees ruin. Because the bookmaker''s built-in margin always skews the baseline EV into the negative, true profitability is exclusively found by using highly accurate predictive models to locate odds where your edge exceeds the bookmaker''s overround.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'expected-value-formula'
  AND course_id = (SELECT id FROM public.courses WHERE slug = 'finding-value-bets');

-- [bankroll-fundamentals] setting-your-starting-bankroll
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<h1>Setting Your Starting Bankroll</h1><div><h2>Introduction</h2></div><div><p>Setting Your Starting Bankroll is one of the most important foundations of responsible and sustainable sports betting. Your bankroll is the dedicated pool of money you set aside exclusively for wagering. Proper management of it protects you from financial harm, helps you survive normal losing streaks (variance), and allows any genuine edge you develop to compound over time.</p></div><div><hr></div><div><h2>What Is a Betting Bankroll?</h2></div><div><p>It is a fixed sum of money you allocate solely for sports betting. It should be completely separate from your everyday finances, savings, rent, bills, or emergency funds. Treat it like capital for a high-risk hobby or small business: if it goes to zero, your life continues unchanged.</p></div><div><p><strong>Golden rule:</strong> Only use money you can afford to lose entirely without stress or impact on your lifestyle.</p></div><div><hr></div><div><h2>How to Determine Your Starting Amount</h2></div><div><p>There is no universal "correct" number. It depends on your financial situation, how often you plan to bet, your risk tolerance, and whether you view betting as pure entertainment or a more serious pursuit.</p></div><div><p><strong>Practical guidelines by style:</strong></p></div><div><table></div><div><thead></div><div><tr><th>Betting Style</th><th>Suggested Starting Bankroll</th><th>Typical Notes</th></tr></div><div></thead></div><div><tbody></div><div><tr><td>Casual / Entertainment (1–3 bets/week)</td><td>$200 – $500</td><td>Treat like money spent on movies or nights out</td></tr></div><div><tr><td>Regular recreational (5–10 bets/week)</td><td>$500 – $2,000</td><td>Enough runway for 1–2% units</td></tr></div><div><tr><td>Serious hobbyist (10–20+ bets/week)</td><td>$2,000 – $10,000</td><td>Handles volume and variance better</td></tr></div><div><tr><td>Professional / full-time</td><td>$10,000+</td><td>Requires proven edge and larger capital</td></tr></div><div></tbody></div><div></table></div><div><p><strong>Key tests before deciding:</strong></p></div><div><ul></div><div><li>The "pain threshold" test: If this entire amount disappeared tomorrow, would it change anything about your daily life, bills, or peace of mind? If yes, reduce it.</li></div><div><li>Never fund it with borrowed money, credit cards, loans, or money earmarked for necessities.</li></div><div><li>Aim for a bankroll large enough to contain at least 50–100 of your intended unit sizes so you can weather losing streaks.</li></div><div></ul></div><div><p>Start smaller than you think if you are new. You can always add to it later from profits or additional disposable income; you cannot easily recover from emotional decisions caused by an oversized bankroll.</p></div><div><hr></div><div><h2>Define Your Unit Size</h2></div><div><p>A unit is your standard bet size, expressed as a percentage of your total bankroll. This removes emotion and keeps risk consistent.</p></div><div><p><strong>Most recommended range for beginners and serious recreational bettors:</strong></p></div><div><ul></div><div><li><strong>1%</strong> — Conservative (maximum durability through downswings)</li></div><div><li><strong>2%</strong> — Balanced / most common sweet spot</li></div><div><li><strong>3%</strong> — More aggressive (higher risk of larger drawdowns)</li></div><div></ul></div><div><p><strong>Examples:</strong></p></div><div><ul></div><div><li>$1,000 bankroll at 1% → 1 unit = $10</li></div><div><li>$1,000 bankroll at 2% → 1 unit = $20</li></div><div><li>$5,000 bankroll at 2% → 1 unit = $100</li></div><div></ul></div><div><p>Most plays should be 1 unit. High-confidence plays might be 2 units. Rarely exceed 3 units on a single bet. Accumulators and long-shot bets are often treated as fractional units or pure entertainment stakes.</p></div><div><hr></div><div><h2>Basic Staking Approaches</h2></div><div><ul></div><div><li><strong>Flat staking</strong> (recommended starting point): Bet the same fixed unit (or dollar amount) on every wager. Simple, easy to track, and protects against emotional sizing.</li></div><div><li><strong>Percentage of current bankroll</strong>: Recalculate the unit size periodically (e.g., monthly or after significant growth/drawdown of 20–25%). Bets automatically scale up with profits and down with losses.</li></div><div><li><strong>Confidence / tiered staking</strong>: 1 unit standard, 2 units for stronger edges. Requires honest self-assessment.</li></div><div><li><strong>Kelly Criterion</strong> (advanced): Sizes bets according to your estimated edge and the odds. Full Kelly is volatile; most use fractional Kelly (e.g., half or quarter) and only after proving an edge over hundreds of bets. Beginners should avoid it initially.</li></div><div></ul></div><div><hr></div><div><h2>Practical Setup Steps</h2></div><div><ol></div><div><li>Decide the amount you can truly afford to lose and commit to it.</li></div><div><li>Move that money into a separate account or clearly track it as your betting fund.</li></div><div><li>Set your unit size (start at 1–2%).</li></div><div><li>Decide on a simple staking plan (flat is best for most people starting out).</li></div><div><li>Track every bet (date, selection, odds, stake in units and currency, result, profit/loss, notes). Spreadsheets or dedicated apps work well.</li></div><div><li>Review monthly: recalculate units if the bankroll has grown or shrunk meaningfully, and assess overall performance in units and ROI.</li></div><div><li>Never chase losses by increasing stakes or dipping into non-bankroll money.</li></div><div></ol></div><div><hr></div><div><h2>Why This Matters</h2></div><div><p>Sports betting involves high variance. Even skilled bettors experience long losing runs. Proper bankroll sizing means a 10–20 unit losing streak is uncomfortable but survivable, not catastrophic. It also forces discipline: you stop viewing individual results in isolation and focus on long-term process.</p></div><div><p>Poor bankroll management is one of the main reasons recreational bettors go broke, even when they occasionally pick winners. Good management turns betting into controlled entertainment (or a measured pursuit of edge) rather than a source of stress.</p></div><div><hr></div><div><h2>Final Reminders</h2></div><div><ul></div><div><li>Your starting bankroll is a decision about risk tolerance and lifestyle, not about how much you "need" to win big.</li></div><div><li>Keep it separate and never top it up impulsively after losses.</li></div><div><li>Adjust only based on predetermined rules, not emotion.</li></div><div><li>If betting ever stops being fun or starts affecting other areas of life, stop and reassess.</li></div><div></ul></div><div><hr></div><div><h2>Key Takeaway</h2></div><div><p>Setting your starting bankroll correctly and managing it with a unit system and strict discipline is the single most important factor for long-term sustainability in sports betting. It protects you from ruin, helps you endure variance, and provides the foundation for any profitable edge to materialise over time. Start conservatively, track everything, and never risk money you cannot afford to lose.</p></div><div><p>Always gamble responsibly. Betting should never jeopardize your financial security or well-being. If you need help with gambling-related issues, seek support from professional resources available in your region.</p></div>'
WHERE slug = 'setting-your-starting-bankroll'
  AND course_id = (SELECT id FROM public.courses WHERE slug = 'bankroll-fundamentals');

-- [professional-bettor-mindset] growth-vs-fixed-mindset-betting
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<h2>Carol Dweck''s Framework</h2>
<p>Carol Dweck''s research on mindset distinguishes between:</p>
<p><strong>Fixed mindset:</strong> Abilities are innate and fixed. Success proves you are talented; failure proves you are not. Challenges are threats.</p>
<p><strong>Growth mindset:</strong> Abilities are developed through effort and learning. Success reflects effort; failure reflects a learning opportunity. Challenges are growth opportunities.</p>
<h2>How Fixed Mindset Manifests in Betting</h2>
<p>A bettor with a fixed mindset:</p>
<ul>
<li>Attributes wins to superior ability ("I read this market perfectly")</li>
<li>Attributes losses to bad luck ("the result was a fluke")</li>
<li>Avoids markets where they might fail (protecting the self-image of competence)</li>
<li>Responds to poor results with defensiveness rather than inquiry</li>
<li>Does not systematically review mistakes (reviewing mistakes is threatening)</li>
</ul>
<h2>How Growth Mindset Manifests in Betting</h2>
<p>A bettor with a growth mindset:</p>
<ul>
<li>Attributes wins to effective process and reviews what worked</li>
<li>Attributes losses to process errors or variance, and investigates which</li>
<li>Enters new markets with beginner''s mind — willing to be wrong while learning</li>
<li>Responds to poor results with curiosity: "What can I learn here?"</li>
<li>Maintains a systematic mistake log as a core learning tool</li>
</ul>
<h2>The Mistake Log</h2>
<p>A growth-mindset bettor maintains a mistake log alongside the bet log. Any bet that:</p>
<ul>
<li>Violated a process rule</li>
<li>Was placed with insufficient analysis</li>
<li>Deviated from the staking formula</li>
<li>Was placed in an emotional state</li>
</ul>
<p>...is logged with the specific error identified and the lesson drawn.</p>
<p>This log is reviewed quarterly. The patterns it reveals drive the most targeted process improvements.</p>

<h2>Real-Data Pattern: How Loss-Chasing ("Tilt") Destroys Long-Run ROI</h2>
<p>Behavioral analysis across tracked bet journals (and PunterStat''s Empirical Bayes <strong>Tilt Risk Model</strong>) reveals a stark divergence between disciplined flat-stake sessions and post-loss chasing sessions:</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Behavioral State</th><th>Typical Stake vs Median</th><th>Average Leg Count</th><th>Typical Realized ROI</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Pre-planned disciplined bet (rested)</td><td>1.0&times; median stake</td><td>1 – 2 legs</td><td>Near fair (&minus;3% to +4%)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Post-loss bet within 2 hours of a 2+ loss streak</td><td><strong>2.2&times; – 3.5&times; median stake</strong></td><td>3 – 6 legs (chasing payout)</td><td><strong>&minus;24% to &minus;42%</strong></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Late-night / unfamiliar-league impulse bet</td><td>1.5&times; – 2.5&times; median stake</td><td>4+ legs</td><td><strong>&minus;30%+</strong></td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Actionable Defense:</strong> Pre-commit your maximum daily stake (e.g. 3% of bankroll) and enforce a mandatory <strong>24-hour cooling-off rule</strong> after 3 consecutive losses. Log every slip in PunterStat''s <strong>Bet Tracker</strong> so your Report Card exposes tilt patterns objectively before they drain your bankroll.</p>

<h2>Key Takeaway</h2>
<p><strong>Growth Mindset vs Fixed Mindset in Betting:</strong> Separate decision quality from single-bet outcomes, enforce pre-committed stake limits, and rely on logged journal data rather than gut instinct.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'growth-vs-fixed-mindset-betting'
  AND course_id = (SELECT id FROM public.courses WHERE slug = 'professional-bettor-mindset');

-- [understanding-odds-formats] fractional-odds
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<h2>What fractional odds mean</h2>
<p>Fractional odds express <em>profit</em> relative to stake — not total return. The number on the left is what you win; the number on the right is what you must stake to win it.</p>
<p>At <strong>5/2</strong>: stake ₦2 to profit ₦5. Total return: ₦7. If you stake ₦10 (five times the right-hand number), profit is ₦25, total return ₦35.</p>
<p>At <strong>1/2</strong> (also written as "1/2 on"): stake ₦2 to profit ₦1. Total return: ₦3. These odds below evens represent heavy favourites where the implied probability exceeds 50%. Odds-on prices are common in horse racing, where strong favourites regularly price at 4/7 or shorter.</p>
<p>Evens — written as "Evs" or 1/1 — means stake ₦1 to profit ₦1. Total return: ₦2. This is the fractional equivalent of decimal 2.00.</p>

<h2>The conversion formula</h2>
<p>Converting fractional to decimal is the most useful calculation to have automatic:</p>
<p><strong>Decimal = (Numerator ÷ Denominator) + 1</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>5/2 → (5 ÷ 2) + 1 = 3.50</li>
<li>7/4 → (7 ÷ 4) + 1 = 2.75</li>
<li>11/8 → (11 ÷ 8) + 1 = 2.375</li>
<li>1/2 → (1 ÷ 2) + 1 = 1.50</li>
<li>4/9 → (4 ÷ 9) + 1 = 1.44</li>
</ul>
<p>To convert decimal back to fractional: subtract 1 from the decimal, then express as a simplified fraction. 2.50 − 1 = 1.50 = 3/2 = 6/4 (the latter being the traditional UK expression).</p>

<h2>Common fractions and their decimal equivalents</h2>
<p>A working knowledge of the most frequently used fractions saves time and prevents errors:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>1/2</strong> = 1.50 (66.7% implied)</li>
<li><strong>4/5</strong> = 1.80 (55.6% implied)</li>
<li><strong>Evens (1/1)</strong> = 2.00 (50.0% implied)</li>
<li><strong>6/4</strong> = 2.50 (40.0% implied)</li>
<li><strong>2/1</strong> = 3.00 (33.3% implied)</li>
<li><strong>5/2</strong> = 3.50 (28.6% implied)</li>
<li><strong>3/1</strong> = 4.00 (25.0% implied)</li>
<li><strong>4/1</strong> = 5.00 (20.0% implied)</li>
<li><strong>10/1</strong> = 11.00 (9.1% implied)</li>
</ul>

<h2>Why awkward fractions exist</h2>
<p>Fractions like 11/4, 13/8, 85/40, and 100/30 are deliberately non-standard. Traditional bookmakers used them to make direct price comparison between shops more difficult — if two bookmakers were both "offering 11/4" on the same horse, the customer might not notice that 11/4 at one and 3/1 at another is actually a different price. In an era before odds comparison websites, this slowed down the information available to bettors and protected bookmaker margins.</p>
<p>In modern online betting, these fractions persist in horse racing markets but are increasingly replaced by decimal displays. When you encounter an unusual fraction, convert to decimal immediately for a clear picture.</p>

<h2>Fractional odds in horse racing: the SP system</h2>
<p>Horse racing in the UK and Ireland uses a specific pricing tradition: the Starting Price (SP). The SP is the officially recorded odds at the time the race starts, based on the last available on-course prices. If you take the SP on a bet rather than a fixed price, your payout is determined by whatever price the horse starts at — which can be dramatically different from the morning price.</p>
<p>A horse might be priced at 8/1 the morning before a race and start at 3/1 if significant money arrives on it during the day. Understanding SP vs fixed price is essential for anyone betting on racing, and the gap between the two is a direct signal of where informed money has moved.</p>

<h2>Real-Data Benchmark: Bookmaker Overround &amp; Closing Line Efficiency</h2>
<p>In PunterStat''s historical odds dataset (<strong>157,752 Pinnacle closing lines</strong> across 39 football leagues), market margin varies sharply between sharp exchanges/bookmakers and soft retail sportsbooks:</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Bookmaker / Market Tier</th><th>Typical 1X2 Overround</th><th>Fair 50/50 Price Offered</th><th>Long-Run Drag on ₦100,000 Staked</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Pinnacle / Betting Exchange (Sharp)</td><td>2.0% – 2.8%</td><td>1.95 – 1.96</td><td>&minus;₦2,200 to &minus;₦2,600</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Tier-1 European / Global Retail</td><td>4.5% – 6.0%</td><td>1.88 – 1.91</td><td>&minus;₦4,800 to &minus;₦5,800</td></tr>
    <tr><td>High-Margin Retail / Multi-Market Props</td><td>7.0% – 11.5%</td><td>1.80 – 1.85</td><td>&minus;₦7,000 to &minus;₦10,500</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Worked Example:</strong> On a Premier League 1X2 market priced at Home <code>2.10</code> (47.62%), Draw <code>3.40</code> (29.41%), Away <code>3.60</code> (27.78%), the summed implied probability is <strong>104.81%</strong> — a <strong>4.81% overround</strong>. Dividing each implied probability by <code>1.0481</code> reveals the fair no-vig probabilities: Home <strong>45.43%</strong> (fair odds <code>2.20</code>), Draw <strong>28.06%</strong> (<code>3.56</code>), Away <strong>26.51%</strong> (<code>3.77</code>). Every time you accept <code>2.10</code> on a true <code>2.20</code> outcome, you surrender 4.5% expected value before kick-off.</p>

<h2>Key Takeaway</h2>
<p>Fractional odds express profit, not total return — always add 1 (in decimal terms) to compare like-for-like. Convert to decimal immediately when you need to think clearly; the fractional format''s complexity is a historical artefact, not a feature.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'fractional-odds'
  AND course_id = (SELECT id FROM public.courses WHERE slug = 'understanding-odds-formats');

-- [odds-comparison-line-shopping] how-to-use-odds-comparison-sites
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<h2><h1>How to Use Odds Comparison Sites</h1><h2>Introduction</h2><p>Odds comparison sites (also called odds screeners or aggregators) pull real-time prices from multiple sportsbooks and display them side-by-side. Used correctly, they turn line shopping from a slow manual process into a fast, systematic habit. Used poorly, they can lead to outdated numbers, ignored rules differences, or bets placed at books you cannot actually access.</p><p>The goal is simple: identify the best available price quickly, verify it, and execute.</p><h2>Core Workflow</h2><ol><li><strong>Select the sport, league, and market</strong><br>Most sites let you filter by sport → league/competition → game → market type (moneyline, spread, total, player prop, etc.).</li><li><strong>Scan the best price</strong><br>The highest decimal odds (or most favorable American/fractional odds) for your selection is usually highlighted. Note which book is offering it.</li><li><strong>Check key details before acting</strong><br>- Is the line current? (Look for last-update timestamps if available.)<br>- Does the book show the same spread/total number, or only a different price on a slightly different line?<br>- Are there any obvious rule differences (e.g., push rules, overtime handling)?</li><li><strong>Verify on the actual sportsbook</strong><br>Never place the bet solely from the comparison site. Open the book, confirm the exact odds and line, and only then execute.</li><li><strong>Record the bet</strong><br>Log the price you actually received, not the price the comparison site showed a few moments earlier.</li></ol><h2>What Good Comparison Sites Show</h2><ul><li>Multiple books in one view</li><li>Decimal, American, and sometimes fractional formats</li><li>Last-update or "live" indicators</li><li>Filters for specific markets, bet types, and sometimes minimum odds</li><li>Direct links or deep links into the sportsbook (when available)</li><li>Historical or closing-line data on stronger platforms</li></ul><p>Some advanced tools also display implied probabilities, overround, or average market price, which helps with edge estimation.</p><h2>Practical Tips for Efficient Use</h2><ul><li><strong>Keep the site open alongside your books.</strong> Use a second monitor or split-screen so you can move from comparison → verification → placement in seconds.</li><li><strong>Focus on the books you actually have accounts with.</strong> Many sites show dozens of books; filter or ignore those you cannot bet into.</li><li><strong>Watch for line movement.</strong> If a price looks unusually high, it may be stale or about to move. Speed matters on sharp numbers.</li><li><strong>Compare apples to apples.</strong> A –3.5 at 1.95 is not the same bet as –3 at 1.91. Confirm both the number and the odds.</li><li><strong>Use mobile versions carefully.</strong> Mobile screens often hide books or delay updates. Desktop is usually more reliable for serious shopping.</li><li><strong>Cross-check suspicious outliers.</strong> Extremely high odds relative to the rest of the market can indicate a data error, suspended market, or restricted account pricing.</li></ul><h2>Limitations and Risks</h2><ul><li><strong>Data lag</strong> — Comparison sites are not always perfectly real-time. A number can disappear or move before you click through.</li><li><strong>Missing books</strong> — Some sharp or offshore books are not included, or appear only on certain platforms.</li><li><strong>Geo and account restrictions</strong> — The best price may be at a book that is unavailable in your location or limited for your account.</li><li><strong>Promotional or boosted odds</strong> — Some highlighted "best" prices are boosted or restricted; read the terms.</li><li><strong>Over-reliance</strong> — The site is a tool, not a substitute for understanding value. A higher number is not automatically +EV if the line itself is inefficient.</li></ul><h2>Advanced Habits</h2><ul><li>Note which books most often offer the best price in the markets you bet. Over time this reveals where to keep larger balances.</li><li>Track how often the comparison site''s top price holds when you click through. This tells you how much latency or error exists.</li><li>Combine the odds screen with a closing-line value tracker so you can see whether the prices you take are beating the market close.</li><li>For live betting, refresh frequently and be prepared for rapid changes; many comparison tools are slower on in-play markets.</li></ul><h2>Real-Data Benchmark: Bookmaker Overround &amp; Closing Line Efficiency</h2>
<p>In PunterStat''s historical odds dataset (<strong>157,752 Pinnacle closing lines</strong> across 39 football leagues), market margin varies sharply between sharp exchanges/bookmakers and soft retail sportsbooks:</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Bookmaker / Market Tier</th><th>Typical 1X2 Overround</th><th>Fair 50/50 Price Offered</th><th>Long-Run Drag on ₦100,000 Staked</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Pinnacle / Betting Exchange (Sharp)</td><td>2.0% – 2.8%</td><td>1.95 – 1.96</td><td>&minus;₦2,200 to &minus;₦2,600</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Tier-1 European / Global Retail</td><td>4.5% – 6.0%</td><td>1.88 – 1.91</td><td>&minus;₦4,800 to &minus;₦5,800</td></tr>
    <tr><td>High-Margin Retail / Multi-Market Props</td><td>7.0% – 11.5%</td><td>1.80 – 1.85</td><td>&minus;₦7,000 to &minus;₦10,500</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Worked Example:</strong> On a Premier League 1X2 market priced at Home <code>2.10</code> (47.62%), Draw <code>3.40</code> (29.41%), Away <code>3.60</code> (27.78%), the summed implied probability is <strong>104.81%</strong> — a <strong>4.81% overround</strong>. Dividing each implied probability by <code>1.0481</code> reveals the fair no-vig probabilities: Home <strong>45.43%</strong> (fair odds <code>2.20</code>), Draw <strong>28.06%</strong> (<code>3.56</code>), Away <strong>26.51%</strong> (<code>3.77</code>). Every time you accept <code>2.10</code> on a true <code>2.20</code> outcome, you surrender 4.5% expected value before kick-off.</p>

<h2>Key Takeaway</h2><p>Odds comparison sites remove most of the friction from line shopping. The correct process is: filter to your market → identify the best price among books you can access → verify the exact line and odds on the sportsbook → place the bet → log what you actually received.</p><p>Do not treat the comparison screen as the final authority. Treat it as a fast radar that points you to the strongest number, then confirm and execute. Used this way, comparison sites systematically improve your average price and are one of the highest-ROI habits available to any bettor who already maintains multiple accounts.</p></h2>'
WHERE slug = 'how-to-use-odds-comparison-sites'
  AND course_id = (SELECT id FROM public.courses WHERE slug = 'odds-comparison-line-shopping');

-- [model-evaluation-calibration] log-loss-scoring-rule
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<h2>Beyond Accuracy: Why We Need Proper Scoring Rules</h2>
<p>Accuracy (percentage of correct predictions) is a poor evaluation metric for probabilistic models. A model that always predicts "home team wins" achieves 45% accuracy — not because it is good, but because home teams win approximately 45% of matches.</p>
<p>Proper scoring rules evaluate the full probability distribution, rewarding confident correct predictions and penalising confident wrong predictions.</p>
<h2>Log-Loss (Cross-Entropy)</h2>
<p>Log-loss is the standard proper scoring rule for probabilistic predictions:</p>
<p>Log-Loss = −(1/n) × Σ [y_i × log(p_i) + (1−y_i) × log(1−p_i)]</p>
<p>Where:</p>
<ul>
<li>y_i = actual outcome (1 or 0)</li>
<li>p_i = predicted probability</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> You predict 70% home win. Home team wins. Contribution = −log(0.70) = 0.357</p>
<p><strong>Contrast:</strong> You predict 70% home win. Away team wins. Contribution = −log(1−0.70) = −log(0.30) = 1.204</p>
<p>Confident wrong predictions are heavily penalised. This incentivises well-calibrated probabilities.</p>
<h2>Interpreting Log-Loss</h2>
<p>Lower log-loss = better model. The naive baseline (always predicting base rates, e.g. 45%/25%/30% for home/draw/away) gives a reference log-loss.</p>
<p>Your model''s log-loss should be significantly below the naive baseline to justify its use.</p>
<h2>Brier Score</h2>
<p>The Brier score is an alternative proper scoring rule: Brier = (1/n) × Σ (p_i − y_i)²</p>
<p>Lower Brier score = better calibration. Also rewards confident correct predictions, but less steeply than log-loss.</p>
<p>Both metrics are appropriate for betting model evaluation. Log-loss is more commonly used in the machine learning community; Brier score in the academic sports statistics literature.</p>

<h2>Real-Data Benchmark: Sample Size, Variance &amp; Walk-Forward Stability</h2>
<p>Short-term win rates fool almost every bettor. Even on a fair 50/50 proposition (<code>2.00</code> odds), the 95% confidence interval around your observed win rate shrinks only with the <em>square root</em> of sample size (<code>&radic;n</code>):</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Settled Bets (n)</th><th>95% Win-Rate Confidence Band (at True 50%)</th><th>95% ROI Noise Band at 2.00 Odds</th><th>Statistical Verdict</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>25 bets</td><td>30.4% – 69.6%</td><td>&minus;39.2% to +39.2%</td><td>Pure noise — impossible to judge skill</td></tr>
    <tr><td>100 bets</td><td>40.2% – 59.8%</td><td>&minus;19.6% to +19.6%</td><td>Early directional signal only</td></tr>
    <tr><td>500 bets</td><td>45.6% – 54.4%</td><td>&minus;8.8% to +8.8%</td><td>Meaningful performance evaluation</td></tr>
    <tr><td>1,000 bets</td><td>46.9% – 53.1%</td><td>&minus;6.2% to +6.2%</td><td>Reliable statistical proof of edge</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Out-of-Sample Proof (PunterStat v2.0.0):</strong> Across 3 strict walk-forward seasons (never peeking into future matches), PunterStat''s model held steady Brier scores of <code>0.60460</code> (2022/23), <code>0.60983</code> (2023/24), and <code>0.60887</code> (2024/25+) — proving that true statistical validation requires out-of-sample testing over thousands of events, never cherry-picked hot streaks.</p>

<h2>Key Takeaway</h2>
<p><strong>Log-Loss: The Proper Scoring Rule for Probabilistic Models:</strong> Demand large out-of-sample evidence, respect regression to the mean, and calibrate probabilities against objective scoring rules like Brier score and CLV.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'log-loss-scoring-rule'
  AND course_id = (SELECT id FROM public.courses WHERE slug = 'model-evaluation-calibration');

-- [live-and-in-play-odds] in-play-edges-where-they-exist
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<h2><h1>In-Play Edges: Where They Actually Exist</h1><h2>Introduction</h2><p>In-play (live) betting offers constant price movement and the illusion of constant opportunity. In reality, genuine edges are scarce, short-lived, and harder to capture than most bettors assume. The majority of recreational live betting is –EV because of higher margins, rapid efficient pricing, and emotional decision-making under time pressure.</p><p>Real in-play edges exist, but they are narrow, require specific conditions, and demand disciplined execution. Understanding where they actually appear—and where they do not—is essential.</p><h2>Why Most In-Play Betting Is Difficult</h2><ul><li><strong>Higher overrounds</strong>: Live markets often carry larger margins than pre-match equivalents, especially on mainstream books.</li><li><strong>Speed of the market</strong>: Sharp books and trading algorithms update prices within seconds of meaningful events. By the time a recreational bettor reacts, the value is frequently gone.</li><li><strong>Latency disadvantage</strong>: Professional operations and bots sit closer to the data feed. Ordinary users are structurally behind.</li><li><strong>Emotional pressure</strong>: Watching the event in real time encourages impulsive bets, revenge staking, and over-reaction to short-term momentum.</li><li><strong>Reduced information edge</strong>: Many of the factors that create pre-match value (deeper research, model outputs, slow news incorporation) are already priced in once the game is underway.</li></ul><p>These factors mean that simply "watching the game and betting live" is usually a losing approach.</p><h2>Where Genuine In-Play Edges Can Exist</h2><p><strong>1. Slow or soft books that lag the market</strong><br>Some retail and softer books update live prices more slowly or with wider margins. When a sharp book has already moved and a slower book has not, a temporary window appears. This is one of the most reliable sources of live value for those who can monitor multiple platforms quickly.</p><p><strong>2. Information or observation advantages</strong><br>Visible but not yet widely reported events (player holding an injury, tactical substitution impact, weather change, crowd or pitch conditions). Sports where visual information travels slower than the odds (certain lower-league or less-covered competitions). Situations where broadcast delay or data-feed delay creates a brief window for those watching a faster stream.</p><p>These edges are fleeting and require both attention and the ability to act before the market adjusts.</p><p><strong>3. Specific market inefficiencies</strong><br>Niche or low-liquidity live markets (certain player props, minor-league totals, correct-score ranges) that receive less algorithmic attention. Moments of extreme volatility (red cards, key injuries, sudden weather shifts) where some books over-react or under-react. Games with clear but temporary momentum that the market has over-adjusted for.</p><p><strong>4. Pre-match to live transitions</strong><br>Occasionally a strong pre-match opinion remains under-priced in the early live market before the price fully converges. This is more common when opening live lines are set conservatively.</p><p><strong>5. Structural or rules-based edges</strong><br>Differences in settlement rules, suspension timing, or cash-out behavior between books can occasionally create small, repeatable discrepancies—though these are rarer and often short-lived once identified.</p><h2>Where Edges Rarely Exist</h2><ul><li>Major markets on sharp books after the first few minutes.</li><li>Heavily traded games (top European football, NFL, NBA primetime) once the market has settled.</li><li>Pure "momentum" or "they look good" betting without a clear mispricing.</li><li>High-frequency clicking in the hope of catching a soft number without a systematic process.</li></ul><h2>Practical Requirements for Capturing Live Edges</h2><ul><li>Multiple book accounts already funded and ready.</li><li>Fast odds comparison or screen layout that shows several books simultaneously.</li><li>Pre-defined criteria for what constitutes an acceptable edge (minimum price improvement or probability discrepancy).</li><li>Strict unit sizing—live variance is often higher and stop-loss rules become more important.</li><li>Acceptance that many potential opportunities will disappear before you can act.</li><li>Willingness to pass on the large majority of live markets.</li></ul><h2>Risk Management Differences</h2><p>Live betting compresses decision time and increases emotional intensity. Therefore:</p><ul><li>Use smaller unit sizes than pre-match (commonly 0.5% or less of bankroll).</li><li>Apply tighter daily or session stop-losses.</li><li>Avoid chasing within the same game.</li><li>Record live bets separately in tracking so you can evaluate whether the live portion of your portfolio is actually profitable.</li></ul><h2>Real-Data Benchmark: Bookmaker Overround &amp; Closing Line Efficiency</h2>
<p>In PunterStat''s historical odds dataset (<strong>157,752 Pinnacle closing lines</strong> across 39 football leagues), market margin varies sharply between sharp exchanges/bookmakers and soft retail sportsbooks:</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Bookmaker / Market Tier</th><th>Typical 1X2 Overround</th><th>Fair 50/50 Price Offered</th><th>Long-Run Drag on ₦100,000 Staked</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Pinnacle / Betting Exchange (Sharp)</td><td>2.0% – 2.8%</td><td>1.95 – 1.96</td><td>&minus;₦2,200 to &minus;₦2,600</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Tier-1 European / Global Retail</td><td>4.5% – 6.0%</td><td>1.88 – 1.91</td><td>&minus;₦4,800 to &minus;₦5,800</td></tr>
    <tr><td>High-Margin Retail / Multi-Market Props</td><td>7.0% – 11.5%</td><td>1.80 – 1.85</td><td>&minus;₦7,000 to &minus;₦10,500</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Worked Example:</strong> On a Premier League 1X2 market priced at Home <code>2.10</code> (47.62%), Draw <code>3.40</code> (29.41%), Away <code>3.60</code> (27.78%), the summed implied probability is <strong>104.81%</strong> — a <strong>4.81% overround</strong>. Dividing each implied probability by <code>1.0481</code> reveals the fair no-vig probabilities: Home <strong>45.43%</strong> (fair odds <code>2.20</code>), Draw <strong>28.06%</strong> (<code>3.56</code>), Away <strong>26.51%</strong> (<code>3.77</code>). Every time you accept <code>2.10</code> on a true <code>2.20</code> outcome, you surrender 4.5% expected value before kick-off.</p>

<h2>Key Takeaway</h2><p>In-play edges exist primarily in the gaps: slower books, delayed information, niche markets, and brief over- or under-reactions. They do not exist in simply watching a game and reacting to what feels like momentum on a sharp, efficient market.</p><p>The bettors who extract value live are those who treat it as a selective, high-discipline activity rather than continuous entertainment. For most people, the higher-percentage approach remains finding value pre-match and using live markets sparingly, only when a clear, actionable discrepancy appears. Selectivity and speed beat volume and emotion.</p></h2>'
WHERE slug = 'in-play-edges-where-they-exist'
  AND course_id = (SELECT id FROM public.courses WHERE slug = 'live-and-in-play-odds');

-- [implied-probability-explained] applying-implied-probability-to-bets
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<h2>Applying Implied Probability to Real Bets</h2>
<p>The formula <strong>Implied Probability = 1 ÷ Decimal Odds</strong> is the single most important conversion in sports betting. Every price a bookmaker shows you is simultaneously a probability claim — and your job is to evaluate whether that claim is correct.</p>
<p>Consider a Premier League fixture: Manchester City vs Brentford. Betfair opens City at 1.35 (implied probability: 74.1%), Draw at 5.20 (19.2%), Brentford at 11.00 (9.1%). Add those three probabilities: 74.1% + 19.2% + 9.1% = 102.4%. That extra 2.4% is the bookmaker''s margin — the guaranteed cost of betting into this market regardless of which side you choose.</p>

<h2>The Three-Step Decision Process</h2>
<p>Every bet should pass through three steps before you place it:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Convert the price to implied probability.</strong> At 2.75: implied P = 1 ÷ 2.75 = 36.4%.</li>
<li><strong>Form your own probability estimate.</strong> Based on your analysis, what do you believe the true probability is?</li>
<li><strong>Compare.</strong> If your estimate (42%) exceeds the implied probability (36.4%) by more than the margin, you have a value bet. If your estimate (30%) falls below, pass.</li>
</ol>
<p>The comparison is everything. You do not need to pick winners. You need to find situations where your probability estimate is consistently higher than the implied probability. Over hundreds of bets, this generates positive expected value — the only mathematical path to long-run profit.</p>

<h2>Real-World Example: Using the PunterStat Dataset</h2>
<p>In the PunterStat historical FDCO dataset covering the Championship (E1) from 2000/01 to 2023/24, home wins occur at a frequency of approximately 43%. If a bookmaker prices a Championship home team at 2.40 (implied 41.7%), that price is slightly below the historical average. Whether that represents value depends on how this specific fixture compares to the league average — but the implied probability calculation is the starting point for every such assessment.</p>
<p>For draws, the FDCO dataset shows a long-run frequency of approximately 27% in the Championship. A draw priced at 3.60 (implied 27.8%) is almost exactly at historical frequency — suggesting the market''s draw probability is fairly priced for this context. You would need a specific analytical reason to bet a draw at 3.60 in this league, because the market has already priced it at its base rate.</p>

<h2>Thinking in Percentages, Not Price Labels</h2>
<p>"3.00 looks like a long shot" is a vague intuition. "33.3% implied probability versus my 40% estimate" is a testable mathematical claim. Train yourself to think in percentages — every time you see an odds price, immediately convert it to an implied probability before forming any opinion about whether it represents value.</p>
<p>Common prices and their implied probabilities to memorise: 1.50 → 66.7%, 2.00 → 50.0%, 2.50 → 40.0%, 3.00 → 33.3%, 4.00 → 25.0%, 6.00 → 16.7%, 10.00 → 10.0%. Build these as automatic reflexes and your decision speed improves dramatically.</p>

<h2>Real-Data Benchmark: PunterStat v2.0 Model Calibration vs Market</h2>
<p>On PunterStat''s held-out test set of <strong>27,830 matches (2024/25–2026/27)</strong>, comparing pure statistical models against de-vigged Pinnacle closing odds illustrates why finding genuine +EV requires precision:</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Probability Source</th><th>1X2 Brier Score (Lower = Better)</th><th>Log-Loss</th><th>Ranked Probability Score (RPS)</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Pinnacle Closing Odds (De-vigged)</td><td><strong>0.59811</strong></td><td><strong>1.00018</strong></td><td><strong>0.20301</strong></td></tr>
    <tr><td>PunterStat v2.0 LGBM + Market Blend</td><td>0.59858</td><td>1.00130</td><td>0.20384</td></tr>
    <tr><td>PunterStat v2.0 Dixon-Coles + LGBM Hybrid (Pure Stats)</td><td>0.60868</td><td>1.01607</td><td>0.20869</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Worked +EV Example in Naira:</strong> Suppose your de-vigged model prices an Over 2.5 Goals selection at <strong>52.0% true probability</strong> (fair odds <code>1.92</code>), and a bookmaker offers <code>2.10</code>. On a <strong>₦5,000 stake</strong>: <code>EV = (0.52 &times; ₦5,500) &minus; (0.48 &times; ₦5,000) = ₦2,860 &minus; ₦2,400 = +₦460</code> (<strong>+9.2% ROI</strong>). Over 1 bet, you still lose 48% of the time; over 500 similar +EV bets, expected profit compounds to <strong>+₦230,000</strong>.</p>

<h2>Key Takeaway</h2>
<p>Applying implied probability is not a step you perform before analysis — it IS the analysis. Before a single match statistic is consulted, converting the offered price to its implied probability tells you exactly what the bookmaker believes about the outcome. In the PunterStat historical dataset across the top five European leagues (1993/94–2024/25), home teams win at roughly 44–46% depending on the league. Any home team priced at 2.20 or better (implied 45.5%) is at or below the historical league-average win rate — a mechanical starting signal that no additional model is needed before consulting. This is how implied probability converts from a formula into a live analytical tool.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'applying-implied-probability-to-bets'
  AND course_id = (SELECT id FROM public.courses WHERE slug = 'implied-probability-explained');

-- [exchange-betting-lay-markets] exchange-vs-bookmaker-when-to-use
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<h2><h1>Exchange vs Bookmaker: When to Use Which</h1><p>The choice between a betting exchange and a traditional sportsbook is not about one being universally better. They are different tools, each with distinct advantages depending on the market, your strategy, and your objective. Understanding when to use which is a core skill for serious bettors.</p><h2>The Core Difference</h2><p>A <strong>sportsbook</strong> sets the odds, takes the opposite side of your bet, and builds a margin (overround/vig) into every price. You can only <strong>back</strong> outcomes.</p><p>An <strong>exchange</strong> matches bettors against each other. Odds emerge from supply and demand. You can <strong>back</strong> or <strong>lay</strong>. The platform charges commission only on net winnings.</p><p>This structural difference creates different strengths and weaknesses.</p><h2>When a Sportsbook Is Usually Better</h2><ul><li><strong>Niche and exotic markets</strong> — Sportsbooks offer far more props, player specials, accumulators, and obscure leagues than exchanges, where liquidity is often nonexistent.</li><li><strong>Guaranteed execution</strong> — A sportsbook accepts your bet instantly at the displayed price. There is no risk of partial matching or unmatched orders.</li><li><strong>Promotions and bonuses</strong> — Free bets, enhanced odds, acca insurance, and loyalty rewards are sportsbook features. Exchanges rarely offer these.</li><li><strong>Beginners and casual bettors</strong> — The interface is simpler. No need to understand liability, order books, or commission structures.</li><li><strong>Accumulators / parlays</strong> — Exchanges generally do not support multi-leg accumulators in the same way sportsbooks do.</li><li><strong>Very small stakes</strong> — If you are betting tiny amounts for fun, the exchange commission and learning curve may not be worth it.</li></ul><h2>When an Exchange Is Usually Better</h2><ul><li><strong>Liquid main markets</strong> — Major football leagues, big tennis matches, major horse races. Exchange odds are almost always tighter than sportsbook odds before commission.</li><li><strong>Laying and trading</strong> — If you want to bet against an outcome, hedge a position, or trade in and out of a market, only an exchange offers this.</li><li><strong>Consistent value betting</strong> — Even a 0.05–0.10 odds improvement compounds significantly over hundreds or thousands of bets. Exchanges typically offer the best price.</li><li><strong>Winning accounts</strong> — Sportsbooks regularly restrict or close profitable customers. Exchanges rarely restrict because they earn commission on both sides regardless of who wins.</li><li><strong>In-play trading</strong> — Exchanges provide far better tools for entering and exiting positions as game state changes.</li><li><strong>Matched betting and arbitrage</strong> — The ability to lay at an exchange is essential for locking in sportsbook bonuses or exploiting price differences.</li><li><strong>Market transparency</strong> — You can see the full order book: how much money is available at every price level. Sportsbooks show only a single price.</li></ul><h2>Practical Scenarios</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Scenario</th><th>Better Choice</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Major Premier League match, simple back bet</td><td>Exchange</td><td>Better odds, high liquidity, low commission</td></tr><tr><td>Niche lower-league match or obscure prop</td><td>Sportsbook</td><td>Exchange has little or no liquidity</td></tr><tr><td>You want to bet against a favourite</td><td>Exchange</td><td>Lay betting only exists on exchanges</td></tr><tr><td>You want a 5-team accumulator</td><td>Sportsbook</td><td>Exchanges generally do not offer multi-leg accumulators</td></tr><tr><td>You are taking advantage of a welcome bonus</td><td>Sportsbook (then exchange for lay)</td><td>Bonuses are sportsbook-only; laying at an exchange locks profit</td></tr><tr><td>You trade pre-match to in-play</td><td>Exchange</td><td>Ability to back then lay, or lay then back, with full order book</td></tr><tr><td>You bet regularly and have been restricted</td><td>Exchange</td><td>No or rare restrictions on winners</td></tr><tr><td>You want instant, simple betting without learning curve</td><td>Sportsbook</td><td>Simpler interface and guaranteed execution</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Cost Comparison</h2><p>Sportsbooks build margin directly into odds. A typical overround on a football match might be 4–7%. On exotic markets it can exceed 10%.</p><p>Exchanges charge commission only on net winnings. Typical rates:</p><ul><li>Betfair: 5% base (reducible to 2% or lower with volume; premium charges apply to some high-volume winners)</li><li>Smarkets: flat 2%</li><li>Matchbook / Betdaq: 1.5–2%</li></ul><p>On liquid markets, the exchange price before commission is usually better than the sportsbook price. After commission, the effective odds are still often superior.</p><p><strong>Example:</strong><br>Sportsbook offers 1.95 for a coin-flip style market.<br>Exchange offers 2.00 with 2% commission.<br>Effective exchange odds: 2.00 × 0.98 = 1.98 on winnings.<br>The exchange is still the better price.</p><h2>Hybrid Approach</h2><p>Most serious bettors use both. A common workflow:</p><ol><li>Check exchange prices to establish the true market consensus.</li><li>Compare sportsbook prices for the same selection.</li><li>If a sportsbook price is above the exchange lay price, an arbitrage or value opportunity exists.</li><li>Use sportsbooks for bonuses, accumulators, and niche markets where exchanges lack liquidity.</li><li>Use exchanges for main markets, laying, trading, and long-term value betting.</li></ol><p>Using both platforms is not a competition. It is about obtaining the best possible price and having the right tool for the specific job.</p><h2>Summary</h2><ul><li><strong>Sportsbooks</strong> are better for niche markets, promotions, accumulators, simplicity, and guaranteed execution.</li><li><strong>Exchanges</strong> are better for liquid main markets, laying, trading, consistent value betting, and avoiding restrictions.</li><li><strong>Cost</strong>: Sportsbooks charge via built-in margin. Exchanges charge commission on net winnings only.</li><li><strong>Best practice</strong>: Use both. Compare prices. Take the best available. Use exchanges as a benchmark and a trading tool. Use sportsbooks for what exchanges cannot offer.</li></ul><p>The bettor who understands when to use each platform has a meaningful edge over the bettor who uses only one.</p></h2>

<h2>Key Takeaway</h2>
<p><strong>Exchange vs Bookmaker: When to Use Which:</strong> Always convert offered odds into de-vigged fair probability, compare across bookmakers, and measure your decisions against the sharp closing line.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'exchange-vs-bookmaker-when-to-use'
  AND course_id = (SELECT id FROM public.courses WHERE slug = 'exchange-betting-lay-markets');

-- [building-your-own-lines] team-ratings-to-match-probabilities
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<h2><h1>From Team Ratings to Match Probabilities</h1><p>Team ratings are only useful if they can be converted into probabilities for specific match outcomes. A rating that says “Team A is stronger than Team B” is not actionable. You need numbers: the probability of a home win, a draw, an away win, over 2.5 goals, both teams to score, or any other market you want to bet.</p><p>This conversion process — from abstract strength numbers to concrete probabilities — is the bridge between your model and the betting market.</p><h2>1. The Basic Structure</h2><p>Most rating-based approaches follow the same general sequence:</p><ol><li>Estimate each team’s expected output (goals, points, or scoring rate)</li><li>Adjust for match context (home advantage, rest, motivation)</li><li>Use a probability distribution to simulate or calculate possible outcomes</li><li>Aggregate those outcomes into market probabilities</li><li>Convert probabilities to fair odds</li></ol><p>The exact method depends on the sport and the rating type, but the logic is universal.</p><h2>2. Goal-Based Models for Football</h2><p>For football, the most common approach is to estimate expected goals for each team and then use a distribution to model the number of goals scored.</p><p><strong>Step 1 — Estimate expected goals</strong></p><p>If your rating model produces offensive and defensive strength numbers, you can combine them with league averages.</p><p>Expected goals for the home team:</p><p>Home xG = League average home goals × Home attack rating × Away defence rating</p><p>Expected goals for the away team:</p><p>Away xG = League average away goals × Away attack rating × Home defence rating</p><p>Home advantage is normally built into the league averages or applied as a separate multiplier.</p><p><strong>Step 2 — Choose a distribution</strong></p><p>The Poisson distribution is the standard starting point for modelling goals because goals are rare, discrete events.</p><p>The probability of a team scoring exactly k goals, given an expected value λ, is:</p><p>P(k) = (λ^k × e^(−λ)) ÷ k!</p><p>This gives you a full probability distribution for 0, 1, 2, 3, or more goals for each team.</p><p><strong>Step 3 — Combine the two distributions</strong></p><p>Since both teams can score independently, the probability of any exact scoreline is the product of the two individual probabilities.</p><p>Probability of a 2–1 home win = P(home scores 2) × P(away scores 1)</p><p>Summing the relevant scorelines gives you the probability of each 1X2 outcome.</p><p><strong>Step 4 — Adjust for the low-scoring draw</strong></p><p>Basic Poisson models typically underestimate the frequency of 0–0 and 1–1 draws because goals are not truly independent. The Dixon-Coles adjustment applies a correction factor for low-scoring scorelines to bring the model closer to observed reality.</p><p>This adjustment is particularly important for match result markets, less so for totals or team goals.</p><h2>3. Margin-Based Models for Spread Sports</h2><p>In sports like basketball, American football, or rugby, ratings are often expressed as point margins rather than goal expectancies.</p><p><strong>Step 1 — Estimate the projected margin</strong></p><p>Projected margin = Home rating − Away rating + Home advantage adjustment</p><p>For example, if the home team is rated +4.5 points better than the away team on a neutral court, and home advantage is worth 3 points, the projected margin is +7.5.</p><p><strong>Step 2 — Model the margin distribution</strong></p><p>Point margins are often approximately normally distributed. The standard deviation varies by sport and league but can be estimated historically.</p><p>Using a normal distribution, you can calculate the probability that the home team wins by more than a given spread, or that the total goes over or under a line.</p><p><strong>Step 3 — Convert to probabilities</strong></p><p>The normal cumulative distribution function gives you the probability that the margin exceeds any threshold. This directly produces probabilities for handicap, moneyline, and totals markets.</p><h2>4. Elo-Based Win Probability</h2><p>Elo ratings are common across sports because they are simple to maintain and update. The standard Elo formula converts a rating difference into an expected win probability.</p><p>Expected win probability for Team A:</p><p>P(A) = 1 ÷ (1 + 10^((Rating_B − Rating_A) ÷ 400))</p><p>This gives the probability of a win in a two-outcome market. For football, a draw probability must be estimated separately — typically through historical draw rates conditional on the rating gap.</p><p>Elo is less granular than goal-based models for football but remains a useful benchmark and can be extended with goal differential adjustments.</p><h2>5. From Outcome Probabilities to Market Prices</h2><p>Once you have probabilities for the outcomes you care about, the final step is converting them to fair decimal odds.</p><p>Fair odds = 1 ÷ probability</p><p>For example:</p><ul><li>Your model gives the home team a 52% chance</li><li>Fair odds = 1 ÷ 0.52 = 1.923</li></ul><p>If the market offers 2.10, your model suggests positive expected value. If the market offers 1.80, you should pass — even if you think the team will win.</p><p>For markets with multiple outcomes, sum the probabilities to ensure they total 100%. If they do not, normalize before comparing to market odds.</p><h2>6. Accounting for Uncertainty</h2><p>Your projected probabilities are estimates, not facts. Model inputs have error. Rating numbers have variance. Probability outputs should be treated as ranges rather than exact values.</p><p>Simple ways to incorporate uncertainty:</p><ul><li>Use a margin of safety — bet only when your edge exceeds a defined threshold</li><li>Run sensitivity tests — vary key parameters and see how probabilities shift</li><li>Track calibration — compare your predicted probabilities to actual outcomes over hundreds of bets</li><li>Blend with the market — treat the closing line as a prior and your model as an adjustment</li></ul><p>The goal is not to be exactly right on every match. The goal is to be systematically better than the market price over a large sample.</p><h2>7. Common Pitfalls</h2><ul><li><strong>Overconfidence in small samples</strong> — a few weeks of results do not validate a projection engine</li><li><strong>Ignoring correlation</strong> — game states, red cards, and tactical shifts make scoreline components dependent</li><li><strong>Using stale ratings</strong> — lineups and injuries change expected output significantly</li><li><strong>Forgetting market context</strong> — low-liquidity matches may have inefficient prices but also higher variance</li><li><strong>Treating probabilities as certainties</strong> — a 55% chance is not a sure thing; it loses 45% of the time</li></ul><h2>Summary</h2><p>The path from team ratings to match probabilities follows a consistent structure:</p><ol><li><strong>Estimate expected outputs</strong> — goals, points, or margins</li><li><strong>Choose a probability distribution</strong> — Poisson, normal, Elo, or empirical</li><li><strong>Calculate outcome probabilities</strong> — exact scores, margins, or results</li><li><strong>Aggregate into market probabilities</strong> — 1X2, totals, handicaps</li><li><strong>Convert to fair odds</strong> — and compare to available prices</li><li><strong>Apply uncertainty buffers</strong> — thresholds, sensitivity, and calibration</li></ol><p>This conversion is where modelling stops being an academic exercise and starts being a betting process. If you cannot express your opinion as a number and compare that number to the market, you are still guessing. Ratings are the raw material. Probabilities are the product. Fair odds are the decision tool.</p></h2>

<h2>Real-Data Benchmark: Bookmaker Overround &amp; Closing Line Efficiency</h2>
<p>In PunterStat''s historical odds dataset (<strong>157,752 Pinnacle closing lines</strong> across 39 football leagues), market margin varies sharply between sharp exchanges/bookmakers and soft retail sportsbooks:</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Bookmaker / Market Tier</th><th>Typical 1X2 Overround</th><th>Fair 50/50 Price Offered</th><th>Long-Run Drag on ₦100,000 Staked</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Pinnacle / Betting Exchange (Sharp)</td><td>2.0% – 2.8%</td><td>1.95 – 1.96</td><td>&minus;₦2,200 to &minus;₦2,600</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Tier-1 European / Global Retail</td><td>4.5% – 6.0%</td><td>1.88 – 1.91</td><td>&minus;₦4,800 to &minus;₦5,800</td></tr>
    <tr><td>High-Margin Retail / Multi-Market Props</td><td>7.0% – 11.5%</td><td>1.80 – 1.85</td><td>&minus;₦7,000 to &minus;₦10,500</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Worked Example:</strong> On a Premier League 1X2 market priced at Home <code>2.10</code> (47.62%), Draw <code>3.40</code> (29.41%), Away <code>3.60</code> (27.78%), the summed implied probability is <strong>104.81%</strong> — a <strong>4.81% overround</strong>. Dividing each implied probability by <code>1.0481</code> reveals the fair no-vig probabilities: Home <strong>45.43%</strong> (fair odds <code>2.20</code>), Draw <strong>28.06%</strong> (<code>3.56</code>), Away <strong>26.51%</strong> (<code>3.77</code>). Every time you accept <code>2.10</code> on a true <code>2.20</code> outcome, you surrender 4.5% expected value before kick-off.</p>

<h2>Key Takeaway</h2>
<p><strong>From Team Ratings to Match Probabilities:</strong> Always convert offered odds into de-vigged fair probability, compare across bookmakers, and measure your decisions against the sharp closing line.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'team-ratings-to-match-probabilities'
  AND course_id = (SELECT id FROM public.courses WHERE slug = 'building-your-own-lines');

-- [model-evaluation-calibration] calibration-curves-reliability
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<h2>What Is Calibration?</h2>
<p>A model is perfectly calibrated if, for all matches where it predicts 60% home win probability, the home team wins 60% of the time. In reality, most models are miscalibrated in specific ranges.</p>
<h2>The Calibration Curve</h2>
<p>To build a calibration curve (reliability diagram):</p>
<ol>
<li>Group all predictions into probability bins (0–10%, 10–20%, ..., 90–100%)</li>
<li>For each bin: calculate average predicted probability and actual outcome frequency</li>
<li>Plot predicted probability (x-axis) vs actual frequency (y-axis)</li>
</ol>
<p>A perfectly calibrated model lies on the diagonal (y = x line). Points above the diagonal: model underestimates probability. Points below: model overestimates.</p>
<h2>Common Miscalibration Patterns</h2>
<p><strong>Overconfidence:</strong> The calibration curve is flatter than the diagonal — predictions of 80% correspond to 65% actual frequency. The model is too confident.</p>
<p><strong>Underconfidence:</strong> The calibration curve is steeper than the diagonal — predictions of 80% correspond to 90% actual frequency. The model is too conservative.</p>
<p><strong>Range miscalibration:</strong> The model is well-calibrated in the 40–60% range but poorly calibrated at extremes. Common in models fitted primarily on "competitive" matches.</p>
<h2>Fixing Miscalibration</h2>
<p><strong>Platt scaling:</strong> Fit a logistic regression on the model''s raw output vs outcomes. Use the logistic regression''s output as the final probability.</p>
<p><strong>Isotonic regression:</strong> A more flexible, non-parametric calibration method. Preferred when calibration errors are non-monotonic.</p>
<p><strong>Beta calibration:</strong> Uses the beta distribution to calibrate probability outputs. Best theoretical fit for probability-valued outputs.</p>
<p>After applying calibration, rebuild the calibration curve and confirm improvement.</p>

<h2>Real-Data Benchmark: Sample Size, Variance &amp; Walk-Forward Stability</h2>
<p>Short-term win rates fool almost every bettor. Even on a fair 50/50 proposition (<code>2.00</code> odds), the 95% confidence interval around your observed win rate shrinks only with the <em>square root</em> of sample size (<code>&radic;n</code>):</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Settled Bets (n)</th><th>95% Win-Rate Confidence Band (at True 50%)</th><th>95% ROI Noise Band at 2.00 Odds</th><th>Statistical Verdict</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>25 bets</td><td>30.4% – 69.6%</td><td>&minus;39.2% to +39.2%</td><td>Pure noise — impossible to judge skill</td></tr>
    <tr><td>100 bets</td><td>40.2% – 59.8%</td><td>&minus;19.6% to +19.6%</td><td>Early directional signal only</td></tr>
    <tr><td>500 bets</td><td>45.6% – 54.4%</td><td>&minus;8.8% to +8.8%</td><td>Meaningful performance evaluation</td></tr>
    <tr><td>1,000 bets</td><td>46.9% – 53.1%</td><td>&minus;6.2% to +6.2%</td><td>Reliable statistical proof of edge</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Out-of-Sample Proof (PunterStat v2.0.0):</strong> Across 3 strict walk-forward seasons (never peeking into future matches), PunterStat''s model held steady Brier scores of <code>0.60460</code> (2022/23), <code>0.60983</code> (2023/24), and <code>0.60887</code> (2024/25+) — proving that true statistical validation requires out-of-sample testing over thousands of events, never cherry-picked hot streaks.</p>

<h2>Key Takeaway</h2>
<p><strong>Calibration Curves and Reliability Diagrams:</strong> Demand large out-of-sample evidence, respect regression to the mean, and calibrate probabilities against objective scoring rules like Brier score and CLV.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'calibration-curves-reliability'
  AND course_id = (SELECT id FROM public.courses WHERE slug = 'model-evaluation-calibration');

-- [how-bookmaker-margins-work] why-margins-vary-by-market
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<h2><h1>Why Margins Vary by Market</h1><p>Bookmakers do not apply a uniform margin across every market. The overround they build into the odds changes according to several practical and commercial factors. Understanding these differences helps you identify where the prices are relatively soft or relatively expensive.</p><h2>1. Competition and Liquidity</h2><p>Markets with heavy competition and high betting volume attract tighter margins.</p><ul><li>Major league moneylines, spreads, and totals (NFL, NBA, Premier League, etc.) often carry the lowest overrounds because multiple sharp books and exchanges compete for the same action.</li><li>Low-liquidity markets (minor leagues, lower divisions, obscure props, or early outs) usually carry higher margins. With fewer competing prices and less balanced action, the book can (and must) protect itself with a bigger edge.</li></ul><h2>2. Number of Outcomes</h2><p>The more possible results in a market, the higher the typical margin tends to be.</p><ul><li>Two-way markets (moneyline, spread, total) are the easiest to balance and usually have the tightest overrounds.</li><li>Three-way markets (1X2 soccer) sit in the middle.</li><li>Multi-way markets (outrights, tournament winners, player props with many options) frequently show significantly higher overrounds because the book must cover more outcomes and faces greater uncertainty in balancing the book.</li></ul><h2>3. Predictability and Information Edge</h2><p>Markets where outcomes are harder to forecast accurately usually carry higher margins.</p><ul><li>Team win/lose markets in major sports are relatively efficient; margins stay lower.</li><li>Player props, exact scores, method-of-victory markets, or novelty bets contain more uncertainty and more opportunities for bettors to hold private information. Books respond by widening the margin.</li></ul><h2>4. Expected Betting Patterns and Liability Risk</h2><p>Bookmakers adjust margins according to how the public tends to bet and how difficult the liability is to manage.</p><ul><li>Heavily one-sided public markets (e.g., heavy favorites in big events) often see the favorite''s price shortened more aggressively, increasing the effective margin on that side.</li><li>Markets that can produce large, correlated liabilities (certain live markets, same-game parlays, or futures) frequently carry elevated margins as a risk-management tool.</li></ul><h2>5. Bookmaker Business Model and Customer Base</h2><p>Different books target different customers and therefore set different margin philosophies.</p><ul><li>Sharp or low-margin books (and exchanges) compete on price and keep overrounds minimal to attract informed money.</li><li>Recreational or retail-focused books often run higher margins across the board, especially on props and secondary markets, because their customers are less price-sensitive.</li><li>Some books deliberately offer very tight main-market prices as a "loss leader" while making their profit on higher-margin side markets.</li></ul><h2>6. Stage of the Market and Time to Event</h2><p>Margins are not static.</p><ul><li>Early markets (opened days or weeks in advance) frequently start with higher overrounds. As more information arrives and liquidity increases, books often tighten the prices.</li><li>Near closing time on major events, competition intensifies and margins on the main markets are usually at their lowest.</li><li>Live/in-play markets can swing rapidly; books may widen margins temporarily when the match state is volatile or when they have large unbalanced liability.</li></ul><h2>7. Regulatory and Operational Costs</h2><p>In some jurisdictions or with certain product types, higher operational or tax costs are passed on through wider margins. This is more noticeable in regulated retail environments than in offshore or exchange settings.</p><h2>Practical Implications for Bettors</h2><ul><li>The lowest margins are generally found on the main markets of the most popular sports close to event time at competitive books.</li><li>Higher margins are concentrated in props, minor leagues, early lines, multi-way markets, and recreational-focused books.</li><li>A market with a high overround is not automatically "bad," but it raises the performance threshold you must clear to show a long-term profit.</li><li>Line shopping across books remains one of the highest-leverage ways to reduce the effective margin you actually pay.</li></ul><h2>Real-Data Benchmark: Bookmaker Overround &amp; Closing Line Efficiency</h2>
<p>In PunterStat''s historical odds dataset (<strong>157,752 Pinnacle closing lines</strong> across 39 football leagues), market margin varies sharply between sharp exchanges/bookmakers and soft retail sportsbooks:</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Bookmaker / Market Tier</th><th>Typical 1X2 Overround</th><th>Fair 50/50 Price Offered</th><th>Long-Run Drag on ₦100,000 Staked</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Pinnacle / Betting Exchange (Sharp)</td><td>2.0% – 2.8%</td><td>1.95 – 1.96</td><td>&minus;₦2,200 to &minus;₦2,600</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Tier-1 European / Global Retail</td><td>4.5% – 6.0%</td><td>1.88 – 1.91</td><td>&minus;₦4,800 to &minus;₦5,800</td></tr>
    <tr><td>High-Margin Retail / Multi-Market Props</td><td>7.0% – 11.5%</td><td>1.80 – 1.85</td><td>&minus;₦7,000 to &minus;₦10,500</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Worked Example:</strong> On a Premier League 1X2 market priced at Home <code>2.10</code> (47.62%), Draw <code>3.40</code> (29.41%), Away <code>3.60</code> (27.78%), the summed implied probability is <strong>104.81%</strong> — a <strong>4.81% overround</strong>. Dividing each implied probability by <code>1.0481</code> reveals the fair no-vig probabilities: Home <strong>45.43%</strong> (fair odds <code>2.20</code>), Draw <strong>28.06%</strong> (<code>3.56</code>), Away <strong>26.51%</strong> (<code>3.77</code>). Every time you accept <code>2.10</code> on a true <code>2.20</code> outcome, you surrender 4.5% expected value before kick-off.</p>

<h2>Key Takeaway</h2><p>Margins vary because books balance competition, risk, number of outcomes, predictability, and their own business model. Main, high-liquidity markets near closing time almost always offer the tightest prices; everything else tends to carry a larger built-in edge for the bookmaker. Knowing where margins expand and contract lets you focus your effort on the markets where the hurdle is lowest.</p></h2>'
WHERE slug = 'why-margins-vary-by-market'
  AND course_id = (SELECT id FROM public.courses WHERE slug = 'how-bookmaker-margins-work');

-- [building-predictive-models] feature-engineering-betting
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<h2>What Are Features?</h2>
<p>Features are the input variables that a predictive model uses to generate predictions. The quality of your feature engineering often matters more than the choice of model. Good features from a simple model beat poor features from a complex model.</p>
<h2>The Core Features for a Football Model</h2>
<p><strong>Attack strength:</strong> Rolling average xG scored per match (home and away separately), opponent-adjusted.</p>
<p><strong>Defence strength:</strong> Rolling average xGA per match (home and away separately), opponent-adjusted.</p>
<p><strong>Home advantage:</strong> Binary flag (1 = home, 0 = away). Plus optionally: estimated home advantage multiplier from historical win rate for this specific team at home.</p>
<p><strong>Rest advantage:</strong> Days since last competitive match for each team. Difference between teams'' rest days.</p>
<p><strong>Form indicator:</strong> Points earned / expected points earned in last 5–10 matches. Captures recent trajectory.</p>
<p><strong>Motivation:</strong> Distance from a threshold (top 4, relegation, title) — teams fighting for something may be motivated differently. Operationalised as points gap to nearest relevant threshold.</p>
<h2>Feature Engineering Pitfalls</h2>
<p><strong>Lookahead bias:</strong> Using future information to calculate historical features. If you calculate a team''s "season-average xG" using all matches including those after the prediction date, you are cheating — your model will appear to work better than it does on genuinely future data.</p>
<p><strong>Overfitting through feature count:</strong> More features allow the model to fit the training data better but generalise worse. Prefer fewer, theoretically motivated features.</p>
<p><strong>Collinearity:</strong> Features that are highly correlated (home xG and home points per match are correlated) can destabilise model estimation. Test for correlation and drop redundant features.</p>

<h2>Real-Data Benchmark: Sample Size, Variance &amp; Walk-Forward Stability</h2>
<p>Short-term win rates fool almost every bettor. Even on a fair 50/50 proposition (<code>2.00</code> odds), the 95% confidence interval around your observed win rate shrinks only with the <em>square root</em> of sample size (<code>&radic;n</code>):</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Settled Bets (n)</th><th>95% Win-Rate Confidence Band (at True 50%)</th><th>95% ROI Noise Band at 2.00 Odds</th><th>Statistical Verdict</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>25 bets</td><td>30.4% – 69.6%</td><td>&minus;39.2% to +39.2%</td><td>Pure noise — impossible to judge skill</td></tr>
    <tr><td>100 bets</td><td>40.2% – 59.8%</td><td>&minus;19.6% to +19.6%</td><td>Early directional signal only</td></tr>
    <tr><td>500 bets</td><td>45.6% – 54.4%</td><td>&minus;8.8% to +8.8%</td><td>Meaningful performance evaluation</td></tr>
    <tr><td>1,000 bets</td><td>46.9% – 53.1%</td><td>&minus;6.2% to +6.2%</td><td>Reliable statistical proof of edge</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Out-of-Sample Proof (PunterStat v2.0.0):</strong> Across 3 strict walk-forward seasons (never peeking into future matches), PunterStat''s model held steady Brier scores of <code>0.60460</code> (2022/23), <code>0.60983</code> (2023/24), and <code>0.60887</code> (2024/25+) — proving that true statistical validation requires out-of-sample testing over thousands of events, never cherry-picked hot streaks.</p>

<h2>Key Takeaway</h2>
<p><strong>Feature Engineering for Betting Models:</strong> Demand large out-of-sample evidence, respect regression to the mean, and calibrate probabilities against objective scoring rules like Brier score and CLV.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'feature-engineering-betting'
  AND course_id = (SELECT id FROM public.courses WHERE slug = 'building-predictive-models');

-- [finding-value-bets] spotting-value-in-practice
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<h2>Spotting Value in Practice</h2>
<p>Understanding the theory of value is straightforward; spotting it consistently in real-world, highly efficient markets is profoundly difficult. Spotting value in practice requires systematic workflows that identify specific triggers—moments when the market is slow to react, constrained by liability, or blinded by public sentiment.</p>
<p>Professional bettors do not scroll through odds looking for "good bets." They monitor data streams. They look for deltas between sharp market indicators (like the Pinnacle Asian Handicap) and the lagging prices offered by soft bookmakers. If the sharp market moves aggressively due to syndicate money, but a soft book leaves their odds unchanged for 15 minutes, value has materialized.</p>
<h2>Exploiting Overreactions to Injuries and News</h2>
<p>Recreational markets frequently overreact to high-profile news, creating significant value. If a star striker for a top-tier Serie A team is announced as injured an hour before kickoff, public money will flood the market, heavily shortening the odds of the opposing team.</p>
<p>However, advanced models often reveal that single non-goalkeeper players are rarely worth more than 0.15 to 0.25 Expected Goals (xG) per match. The public might push the odds as if the player''s absence shifts the true probability by 10%, when mathematically it only shifts by 3%. The sharp bettor spots this overreaction and takes the inflated odds on the team missing the star player, securing immense value against public sentiment.</p>
<h2>Situational Value: The Motivation Factor</h2>
<p>Late-season fixtures present unique value-spotting opportunities. In leagues like the Premier League, matches in May often feature teams with contrasting motivations—a team fighting relegation versus a mid-table team with "nothing to play for." The market often over-adjusts for this motivational disparity.</p>
<p>Bookmakers know the public will blindly back the team fighting for survival. Consequently, they artificially suppress the odds of the desperate team. If your baseline model prices the desperate team at 2.20, but the market prices them at 1.85 purely based on narrative, massive value exists on the mid-table underdog. Professional edge is found by trusting the data over the narrative.</p>
<h2>Top-Down vs Bottom-Up Value Spotting</h2>
<p>There are two primary methodologies for spotting value. <strong>Bottom-Up</strong> bettors build proprietary models from scratch, crunching xG, expected threat (xT), and possession stats to generate their own true odds, betting whenever the market diverges from their numbers. This requires intense technical capability.</p>
<p><strong>Top-Down</strong> bettors assume the sharpest bookmakers (like Pinnacle) possess the most accurate models. They monitor Pinnacle''s closing lines relentlessly. When Pinnacle moves a line from 2.00 to 1.80, the top-down bettor scours soft bookmakers to find a stale price of 1.95. They are not predicting the football match; they are trading the market discrepancy. Both methods spot value, but top-down is far more accessible for those lacking elite data science skills.</p>
<h2>Real-Data Benchmark: PunterStat v2.0 Model Calibration vs Market</h2>
<p>On PunterStat''s held-out test set of <strong>27,830 matches (2024/25–2026/27)</strong>, comparing pure statistical models against de-vigged Pinnacle closing odds illustrates why finding genuine +EV requires precision:</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Probability Source</th><th>1X2 Brier Score (Lower = Better)</th><th>Log-Loss</th><th>Ranked Probability Score (RPS)</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Pinnacle Closing Odds (De-vigged)</td><td><strong>0.59811</strong></td><td><strong>1.00018</strong></td><td><strong>0.20301</strong></td></tr>
    <tr><td>PunterStat v2.0 LGBM + Market Blend</td><td>0.59858</td><td>1.00130</td><td>0.20384</td></tr>
    <tr><td>PunterStat v2.0 Dixon-Coles + LGBM Hybrid (Pure Stats)</td><td>0.60868</td><td>1.01607</td><td>0.20869</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Worked +EV Example in Naira:</strong> Suppose your de-vigged model prices an Over 2.5 Goals selection at <strong>52.0% true probability</strong> (fair odds <code>1.92</code>), and a bookmaker offers <code>2.10</code>. On a <strong>₦5,000 stake</strong>: <code>EV = (0.52 &times; ₦5,500) &minus; (0.48 &times; ₦5,000) = ₦2,860 &minus; ₦2,400 = +₦460</code> (<strong>+9.2% ROI</strong>). Over 1 bet, you still lose 48% of the time; over 500 similar +EV bets, expected profit compounds to <strong>+₦230,000</strong>.</p>

<h2>Key Takeaway</h2>
<p>Spotting value requires identifying specific market inefficiencies rather than purely predicting sporting outcomes. Whether exploiting the public''s tendency to overreact to star player injuries, fading the narrative of late-season motivation, or executing top-down arbitrage by picking off stale odds at soft bookmakers against sharp Pinnacle movement, value is consistently found where emotion and slow market reactions diverge from pure, mathematically derived probabilities.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'spotting-value-in-practice'
  AND course_id = (SELECT id FROM public.courses WHERE slug = 'finding-value-bets');

-- [understanding-odds-formats] american-moneyline-odds
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<h2>What American odds are and how to read them</h2>
<p>American odds (also called moneyline odds) use positive and negative numbers, both anchored to a ₦100/€100/$100 unit. The sign tells you immediately which side of even money you are on:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Positive numbers (e.g. +250):</strong> The profit you make on a $100 stake. +250 means: stake $100, profit $250, total return $350. You are on the underdog side of even money.</li>
<li><strong>Negative numbers (e.g. −150):</strong> The stake required to profit $100. −150 means: stake $150 to profit $100, total return $250. You are on the favourite side of even money.</li>
</ul>
<p>The dividing line is −100/+100, which both equal decimal 2.00 — the exact coin-flip price. Any positive number is longer than evens; any negative number shorter than evens.</p>

<h2>Converting to decimal: the two formulas</h2>
<p>Always convert to decimal for clear comparison:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Positive moneyline to decimal:</strong> (Moneyline ÷ 100) + 1<br>Example: +250 → (250 ÷ 100) + 1 = 3.50</li>
<li><strong>Negative moneyline to decimal:</strong> (100 ÷ |Moneyline|) + 1<br>Example: −150 → (100 ÷ 150) + 1 = 1.667</li>
</ul>
<p>Quick reference table for common moneyline prices:</p>
<ul>
<li>+100 = 2.00 (evens, 50.0%)</li>
<li>+150 = 2.50 (40.0%)</li>
<li>+200 = 3.00 (33.3%)</li>
<li>+300 = 4.00 (25.0%)</li>
<li>−110 = 1.909 (52.4%) — the standard spread price</li>
<li>−150 = 1.667 (60.0%)</li>
<li>−200 = 1.500 (66.7%)</li>
</ul>

<h2>The vig in American odds: how it differs from the decimal presentation</h2>
<p>In American sports betting, two-outcome markets (spread, total) are almost universally priced at −110 on both sides. At decimal this is 1.909, implying 52.4% probability for each side.</p>
<p>The sum: 52.4% + 52.4% = 104.8%. The overround is 4.8% — exactly the same margin structure as a European book pricing both sides of a two-way market at 1.91. The format is different; the economic structure is identical.</p>
<p>Understanding this equivalence means you can evaluate American markets using the same overround logic you apply to decimal markets. The −110/−110 standard means bettors need to win approximately 52.4% of spread/totals bets just to break even — before shopping for better prices.</p>

<h2>Point spreads and totals: where moneyline format dominates</h2>
<p>The US sports betting market is structured primarily around two market types that use moneyline odds:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Moneyline (winner):</strong> Straight win/loss. In a mismatched game, the favourite might be −400 (1.25 decimal) and the underdog +320 (4.20 decimal).</li>
<li><strong>Point spread:</strong> A handicap market designed to create a near-50/50 proposition. Both sides typically price around −110. If the spread is Patriots −6.5, they must win by 7 or more for a Patriots spread bet to win.</li>
<li><strong>Total (over/under):</strong> The combined score must finish over or under a set number. Also typically priced around −110 on both sides.</li>
</ul>

<h2>When you will encounter American odds outside the US</h2>
<p>American odds appear in several contexts beyond US sportsbooks:</p>
<ul>
<li>International operators who serve US customers (DraftKings, FanDuel display them by default)</li>
<li>Some European exchanges and comparison sites allow users to toggle between formats</li>
<li>Pinnacle offers American format as an option alongside decimal</li>
<li>Betting journalism and podcasts based in North America quote prices in this format</li>
</ul>
<p>Even if you never use a US sportsbook, the ability to read moneyline odds quickly is essential for following international betting literature and understanding how the world''s largest legal sports betting market operates.</p>

<h2>Real-Data Benchmark: Bookmaker Overround &amp; Closing Line Efficiency</h2>
<p>In PunterStat''s historical odds dataset (<strong>157,752 Pinnacle closing lines</strong> across 39 football leagues), market margin varies sharply between sharp exchanges/bookmakers and soft retail sportsbooks:</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Bookmaker / Market Tier</th><th>Typical 1X2 Overround</th><th>Fair 50/50 Price Offered</th><th>Long-Run Drag on ₦100,000 Staked</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Pinnacle / Betting Exchange (Sharp)</td><td>2.0% – 2.8%</td><td>1.95 – 1.96</td><td>&minus;₦2,200 to &minus;₦2,600</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Tier-1 European / Global Retail</td><td>4.5% – 6.0%</td><td>1.88 – 1.91</td><td>&minus;₦4,800 to &minus;₦5,800</td></tr>
    <tr><td>High-Margin Retail / Multi-Market Props</td><td>7.0% – 11.5%</td><td>1.80 – 1.85</td><td>&minus;₦7,000 to &minus;₦10,500</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Worked Example:</strong> On a Premier League 1X2 market priced at Home <code>2.10</code> (47.62%), Draw <code>3.40</code> (29.41%), Away <code>3.60</code> (27.78%), the summed implied probability is <strong>104.81%</strong> — a <strong>4.81% overround</strong>. Dividing each implied probability by <code>1.0481</code> reveals the fair no-vig probabilities: Home <strong>45.43%</strong> (fair odds <code>2.20</code>), Draw <strong>28.06%</strong> (<code>3.56</code>), Away <strong>26.51%</strong> (<code>3.77</code>). Every time you accept <code>2.10</code> on a true <code>2.20</code> outcome, you surrender 4.5% expected value before kick-off.</p>

<h2>Key Takeaway</h2>
<p>American odds are the same pricing information in a different wrapper. Convert to decimal first, apply the implied probability formula as usual, and evaluate value on exactly the same basis. The format changes; the underlying maths does not.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'american-moneyline-odds'
  AND course_id = (SELECT id FROM public.courses WHERE slug = 'understanding-odds-formats');

-- [probability-distributions-sport] bivariate-poisson-model
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<h2>Beyond Independent Poisson</h2>
<p>The standard Poisson model for football treats each team''s goal scoring as independent. This is a simplification — the number of goals each team scores are not truly independent (a goal changes game dynamics, affecting subsequent goal probability).</p>
<h2>The Dixon-Coles Correction</h2>
<p>Dixon and Coles (1997) introduced a correction factor τ for low-scoring outcomes (0-0, 1-0, 0-1, 1-1), which are under/over-represented in standard Poisson models:</p>
<p>P(score = (x, y)) = τ(x, y, λ_home, λ_away, ρ) × Poisson(x; λ_home) × Poisson(y; λ_away)</p>
<p>Where τ = 1 − ρλ_home λ_away (for x=0, y=0) = 1 + ρ (for x=1, y=1) = 1 + ρλ_away (for x=1, y=0) = 1 + ρλ_home (for x=0, y=1) = 1 (otherwise)</p>
<p>ρ is typically estimated at −0.13 to −0.10.</p>
<h2>Why This Matters for Bettors</h2>
<p>The Dixon-Coles correction improves probability estimates for low-scoring games. If your model uses pure Poisson without this correction, it:</p>
<ul>
<li>Underestimates 0-0 probability by 15–20%</li>
<li>Overestimates 1-1 probability by 5–10%</li>
</ul>
<p>These distortions affect over/under markets and correct score markets systematically.</p>
<h2>Implementing the Correction</h2>
<p>The correction is straightforward to implement in Excel or Python. The adjusted score probability matrix gives more accurate probability estimates for all market types derived from the scoreline distribution.</p>
<p>Most serious football modellers use Dixon-Coles as the standard. If you are not using it, you are working with a systematically biased probability distribution for low-scoring outcomes.</p>

<h2>Real-Data Benchmark: Sample Size, Variance &amp; Walk-Forward Stability</h2>
<p>Short-term win rates fool almost every bettor. Even on a fair 50/50 proposition (<code>2.00</code> odds), the 95% confidence interval around your observed win rate shrinks only with the <em>square root</em> of sample size (<code>&radic;n</code>):</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Settled Bets (n)</th><th>95% Win-Rate Confidence Band (at True 50%)</th><th>95% ROI Noise Band at 2.00 Odds</th><th>Statistical Verdict</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>25 bets</td><td>30.4% – 69.6%</td><td>&minus;39.2% to +39.2%</td><td>Pure noise — impossible to judge skill</td></tr>
    <tr><td>100 bets</td><td>40.2% – 59.8%</td><td>&minus;19.6% to +19.6%</td><td>Early directional signal only</td></tr>
    <tr><td>500 bets</td><td>45.6% – 54.4%</td><td>&minus;8.8% to +8.8%</td><td>Meaningful performance evaluation</td></tr>
    <tr><td>1,000 bets</td><td>46.9% – 53.1%</td><td>&minus;6.2% to +6.2%</td><td>Reliable statistical proof of edge</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Out-of-Sample Proof (PunterStat v2.0.0):</strong> Across 3 strict walk-forward seasons (never peeking into future matches), PunterStat''s model held steady Brier scores of <code>0.60460</code> (2022/23), <code>0.60983</code> (2023/24), and <code>0.60887</code> (2024/25+) — proving that true statistical validation requires out-of-sample testing over thousands of events, never cherry-picked hot streaks.</p>

<h2>Key Takeaway</h2>
<p><strong>Bivariate Poisson: Modelling Both Teams'' Goals:</strong> Demand large out-of-sample evidence, respect regression to the mean, and calibrate probabilities against objective scoring rules like Brier score and CLV.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'bivariate-poisson-model'
  AND course_id = (SELECT id FROM public.courses WHERE slug = 'probability-distributions-sport');

-- [singles-doubles-accumulators] accumulators-mechanics-myths
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<h2><h1>Accumulators (Accas, Multiples, Parlays)</h1><h2>Core Definition</h2><p>Accumulators (also called accas, multiples, or parlays in North America) are combined bets that link two or more selections into one wager. All selections (legs) must win for the bet to pay out. The odds of each leg are multiplied together, creating the potential for large returns from a small stake.</p><p>Doubles (2 legs) and trebles (3 legs) are the smallest forms. "Accumulator" often refers to four or more legs, though the mechanics are identical for any multi-leg combined bet.</p><hr><h2>Mechanics: How Accumulators Work</h2><ol><li>Select two or more outcomes (from the same event or different events/sports).</li><li>The bookmaker multiplies the individual decimal odds:<br>Combined odds = Odds₁ × Odds₂ × Odds₃ × … × Oddsₙ</li><li>Place a single stake that covers the entire combination.</li><li>The stake (plus any winnings) effectively "rolls over" from one successful leg to the next.</li><li>If every leg wins → you receive stake × combined odds.<br>If any leg loses → the entire accumulator loses (stake gone).</li></ol><p>Void or non-runner legs are usually treated as odds of 1.00 (the bet reduces by one leg). Rules vary by bookmaker, so always check terms. Some operators offer cash-out, "acca insurance" (money-back as a free bet if one leg fails), or freezes, but these typically reduce value.</p><hr><h2>Example – Four-fold football accumulator</h2><p>Arsenal to win @ 1.80<br>Liverpool to win @ 2.00<br>Over 2.5 goals in a third match @ 1.90<br>Player to score anytime @ 2.50</p><p>Combined odds = 1.80 × 2.00 × 1.90 × 2.50 = 17.10<br>Stake: ₦10<br>If all four succeed → Return = ₦171 (profit ₦161).<br>If any one fails → Return = ₦0.</p><p><strong>Rolling-stake view (same math):</strong><br>₦10 on first leg @ 1.80 → ₦18 rolls onto second → ₦36 onto third → ₦68.40 onto fourth → ₦171 total.</p><hr><h2>Probability Reality Check</h2><p>Probabilities multiply the same way odds do. Even strong favourites produce low overall chances:</p><ul><li>Four selections each with a true 60% chance of winning:<br>0.60⁴ ≈ 13% chance the whole acca lands.</li><li>Five selections at 70% each: ≈ 17%.</li><li>Ten "safe" 1.50 legs (≈67% each): ≈ 1.7%.</li></ul><p>The potential payout looks attractive precisely because the true probability is low.</p><hr><h2>Common Myths vs Reality</h2><p><strong>Myth 1: "Accas offer great value / better returns long-term."</strong><br>Reality: Bookmaker margins (overround/vig) compound with every leg. A 5% margin per leg becomes roughly 10% on a double, 14–15% on a treble, and nearly 20% or higher on a four-fold. Most regular acca bettors lose money over time; industry data often shows the vast majority of accumulators fail.</p><p><strong>Myth 2: "I only pick bankers / favourites, so it should land often."</strong><br>Reality: There are no true bankers. Even a heavy favourite loses regularly. Four or five odds-on selections still leave the combined probability well under 50% in most cases. The "dead-cert" trap is one of the most expensive mistakes.</p><p><strong>Myth 3: "Near-misses mean I''m getting closer / due a win."</strong><br>Reality: Near-misses (all but one leg winning) feel frustrating but are statistically expected. They do not improve future chances and can encourage chasing. Selective memory of the rare big win while forgetting dozens of losers fuels the illusion.</p><p><strong>Myth 4: "Adding one more leg doesn''t hurt much and boosts the payout."</strong><br>Reality: Each extra leg multiplies both the potential return and the chance of failure. It also multiplies the bookmaker''s edge. The temptation to "just add one more" is a classic way to turn a reasonable bet into a long-shot.</p><p><strong>Myth 5: "Acca insurance / boosts make them profitable."</strong><br>Reality: These promotions improve the entertainment value and reduce some pain of near-misses, but they rarely overcome the compounded margin. They are marketing tools that still favour the house overall.</p><p><strong>Myth 6: "Tipsters with big acca wins prove the strategy works."</strong><br>Reality: Long-shot accas occasionally hit by chance. Selective reporting (showing only winners) creates the appearance of consistent success. True long-term profitability is measured by ROI and strike rate across all bets, not highlight-reel wins.</p><hr><h2>Advantages</h2><ul><li>Potential for large returns from small stakes (lottery-ticket appeal).</li><li>Keeps multiple events interesting throughout a day or weekend.</li><li>Simple to place and track once understood.</li><li>Fun for recreational betting when stakes are kept very low.</li></ul><hr><h2>Disadvantages</h2><ul><li>Dramatically higher risk and lower hit rate than singles.</li><li>Compounded bookmaker margin erodes expected value.</li><li>One loss wipes out everything—no partial credit in a standard acca.</li><li>Psychological traps (near-misses, overconfidence, chasing) are strong.</li><li>Finding genuine value across multiple independent selections is far harder than on a single.</li></ul><hr><h2>Practical Guidance</h2><ul><li>Treat most accumulators as entertainment, not a primary profit strategy. Use only a small, fixed portion of your bankroll (or money you can fully afford to lose).</li><li>Prefer fewer, higher-quality legs over long "lottery" accas.</li><li>Seek independent selections to avoid correlation (e.g., avoid stacking related outcomes from the same match unless using a dedicated same-game multi tool).</li><li>Calculate implied probabilities yourself before placing the bet.</li><li>Consider system bets (e.g., Trixie, Yankee) if you want some protection against one loser—these place multiple smaller combinations instead of one all-or-nothing acca.</li><li>Keep detailed records. Most people discover their overall return on accas is poor once they track everything.</li><li>Professional / sharp bettors overwhelmingly favour singles (or carefully selected doubles) because the edge is easier to maintain without compounded margins.</li></ul><hr><h2>Real-Data Benchmark: How Accumulator Margin Compounds Against You</h2>
<p>Every leg added to an accumulator multiplies not only your payout, but also the bookmaker''s built-in overround. Assuming a standard <strong>5.0% margin per leg</strong> (fair 50% coin flips priced at <code>1.90</code> instead of <code>2.00</code>):</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Legs on Slip</th><th>True Win Chance</th><th>Fair Combined Odds</th><th>Offered Odds (5% Vig/Leg)</th><th>Compounded Margin Paid</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>1 leg (Single)</td><td>50.0%</td><td>2.00</td><td>1.90</td><td><strong>5.0%</strong></td></tr>
    <tr><td>3 legs (Treble)</td><td>12.5%</td><td>8.00</td><td>6.86</td><td><strong>14.3%</strong></td></tr>
    <tr><td>5 legs (5-Fold)</td><td>3.13%</td><td>32.00</td><td>24.76</td><td><strong>22.6%</strong></td></tr>
    <tr><td>8 legs (8-Fold)</td><td>0.39%</td><td>256.00</td><td>169.84</td><td><strong>33.7%</strong></td></tr>
    <tr><td>10 legs (10-Fold)</td><td>0.10%</td><td>1,024.00</td><td>613.11</td><td><strong>40.1%</strong></td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Why Acca Bonuses Rarely Beat Vig:</strong> A bookmaker offering a "10% Acca Bonus" on a 5-fold still leaves you facing <strong>~15% negative expected value</strong> because the compounded margin (22.6%) overwhelms the bonus. Use PunterStat''s <strong>Slip Checker</strong> to verify whether any acca bonus actually overcomes compounded vig before staking.</p>

<h2>Key Takeaway</h2><p>An accumulator multiplies the odds of several selections and requires every one to win. The mechanics create attractive headline payouts, but probability and bookmaker margins multiply against the bettor just as forcefully. The biggest myths revolve around perceived value, "safe" bankers, and near-misses. Used sparingly with tiny stakes for fun, accas can be entertaining. Used as a regular strategy, they are one of the most reliable ways for recreational bettors to lose money over time.</p><p>Always gamble responsibly. Only stake what you can afford to lose.</p></h2>'
WHERE slug = 'accumulators-mechanics-myths'
  AND course_id = (SELECT id FROM public.courses WHERE slug = 'singles-doubles-accumulators');

-- [regression-to-the-mean] win-rate-regression-xg
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<h2>The Overperforming Team Problem</h2>
<p>A team finishes the first 10 matches of the season with 8 wins, a +15 goal difference, and a top-table position. The market prices them as genuine title contenders.</p>
<p>But their xG data tells a different story: they have scored 12 goals on just 8 expected goals (150% conversion), and their goalkeeper has made 6 saves rated in the top 5% for difficulty. They are significantly outperforming their underlying performance.</p>
<h2>The Statistical Expectation</h2>
<p>Teams that significantly outperform their xG in one period are expected to revert toward their xG performance in the next period. This is regression to the mean — not because the team "gets worse" but because the lucky components (high conversion, exceptional saves) do not sustain.</p>
<h2>Quantifying the Regression</h2>
<p>Research on football performance data shows:</p>
<ul>
<li>Goal conversion rates in excess of xG predict lower conversion in subsequent matches with 60–70% frequency</li>
<li>Goalkeeper save percentages above expected predict lower save percentage in subsequent matches</li>
<li>Point tallies significantly above xPoints (expected points from xG) predict lower points per game in subsequent matches</li>
</ul>
<p>The regression is not guaranteed in any individual match — it is a statistical tendency over many matches.</p>
<h2>The Betting Opportunity</h2>
<p>When the market overvalues a team because of outperformance metrics (ignoring the xG data), a value opportunity exists:</p>
<ul>
<li>The overperforming team is overpriced</li>
<li>Their opponent (or "Under" on total goals) may be underpriced</li>
</ul>
<p>The analytical edge: use xG-based ratings rather than results-based ratings. The market''s heavy weight on results creates systematic mispricing of teams in hot or cold sequences.</p>
<h2>The Timing Challenge</h2>
<p>Regression is not always immediate. An overperforming team can continue overperforming for 5–10 matches before the statistical tendency asserts itself. Betting against form too early can produce multiple losing bets before the regression arrives. Risk management (standard staking, not increasing stakes in anticipation of regression) is essential.</p>

<h2>Real-Data Benchmark: Sample Size, Variance &amp; Walk-Forward Stability</h2>
<p>Short-term win rates fool almost every bettor. Even on a fair 50/50 proposition (<code>2.00</code> odds), the 95% confidence interval around your observed win rate shrinks only with the <em>square root</em> of sample size (<code>&radic;n</code>):</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Settled Bets (n)</th><th>95% Win-Rate Confidence Band (at True 50%)</th><th>95% ROI Noise Band at 2.00 Odds</th><th>Statistical Verdict</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>25 bets</td><td>30.4% – 69.6%</td><td>&minus;39.2% to +39.2%</td><td>Pure noise — impossible to judge skill</td></tr>
    <tr><td>100 bets</td><td>40.2% – 59.8%</td><td>&minus;19.6% to +19.6%</td><td>Early directional signal only</td></tr>
    <tr><td>500 bets</td><td>45.6% – 54.4%</td><td>&minus;8.8% to +8.8%</td><td>Meaningful performance evaluation</td></tr>
    <tr><td>1,000 bets</td><td>46.9% – 53.1%</td><td>&minus;6.2% to +6.2%</td><td>Reliable statistical proof of edge</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Out-of-Sample Proof (PunterStat v2.0.0):</strong> Across 3 strict walk-forward seasons (never peeking into future matches), PunterStat''s model held steady Brier scores of <code>0.60460</code> (2022/23), <code>0.60983</code> (2023/24), and <code>0.60887</code> (2024/25+) — proving that true statistical validation requires out-of-sample testing over thousands of events, never cherry-picked hot streaks.</p>

<h2>Key Takeaway</h2>
<p><strong>Win Rate Regression: Teams That Outperform xG:</strong> Demand large out-of-sample evidence, respect regression to the mean, and calibrate probabilities against objective scoring rules like Brier score and CLV.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'win-rate-regression-xg'
  AND course_id = (SELECT id FROM public.courses WHERE slug = 'regression-to-the-mean');

-- [sample-size-and-variance] how-much-data-is-enough
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<h2>The Perennial Question</h2>
<p>"How many bets do I need before my results are meaningful?" This question is central to every betting operation. The answer depends on the size of the edge you are trying to detect.</p>
<h2>The Signal-to-Noise Problem</h2>
<p>A small edge (2% ROI) is harder to detect in a noisy dataset than a large edge (8% ROI). The required sample size to distinguish real edge from random variance scales with the square of the edge — smaller edges require much larger samples.</p>
<h2>A Practical Guide to Sample Size Requirements</h2>
<p>For a 5% significance level (95% confidence) to detect a true edge:</p>
<p>| True Edge (ROI) | Required Bets | |---|---| | 8% | ~150 bets | | 5% | ~350 bets | | 3% | ~950 bets | | 2% | ~2,100 bets |</p>
<p>A typical professional bettor targeting 3% ROI needs approximately 1,000 bets before they can be reasonably confident their results are not pure luck.</p>
<h2>Why Most Bettors Misread Their Results</h2>
<p>After 200 bets with 4% ROI, many bettors conclude they have "proven" their edge. Statistically, 200 bets at 4% ROI is consistent with both genuine 4% edge AND zero edge with lucky variance. The confidence interval is too wide to conclude anything.</p>
<h2>The CLV Shortcut</h2>
<p>Closing Line Value (CLV) requires smaller samples to detect genuine edge than results-based ROI. This is because CLV directly measures market position (did you beat the closing price?) rather than outcomes (did the selection win?). 200 bets of consistently positive CLV is meaningful evidence; 200 bets of positive ROI is not.</p>
<h2>The Practical Approach</h2>
<ul>
<li>Track CLV from bet 1 (meaningful early signal)</li>
<li>Track ROI but do not interpret it until 500+ bets</li>
<li>Do not change strategy based on short-sample results (either direction)</li>
</ul>

<h2>Real-Data Benchmark: Sample Size, Variance &amp; Walk-Forward Stability</h2>
<p>Short-term win rates fool almost every bettor. Even on a fair 50/50 proposition (<code>2.00</code> odds), the 95% confidence interval around your observed win rate shrinks only with the <em>square root</em> of sample size (<code>&radic;n</code>):</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Settled Bets (n)</th><th>95% Win-Rate Confidence Band (at True 50%)</th><th>95% ROI Noise Band at 2.00 Odds</th><th>Statistical Verdict</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>25 bets</td><td>30.4% – 69.6%</td><td>&minus;39.2% to +39.2%</td><td>Pure noise — impossible to judge skill</td></tr>
    <tr><td>100 bets</td><td>40.2% – 59.8%</td><td>&minus;19.6% to +19.6%</td><td>Early directional signal only</td></tr>
    <tr><td>500 bets</td><td>45.6% – 54.4%</td><td>&minus;8.8% to +8.8%</td><td>Meaningful performance evaluation</td></tr>
    <tr><td>1,000 bets</td><td>46.9% – 53.1%</td><td>&minus;6.2% to +6.2%</td><td>Reliable statistical proof of edge</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Out-of-Sample Proof (PunterStat v2.0.0):</strong> Across 3 strict walk-forward seasons (never peeking into future matches), PunterStat''s model held steady Brier scores of <code>0.60460</code> (2022/23), <code>0.60983</code> (2023/24), and <code>0.60887</code> (2024/25+) — proving that true statistical validation requires out-of-sample testing over thousands of events, never cherry-picked hot streaks.</p>

<h2>Key Takeaway</h2>
<p><strong>How Much Data Is Enough?:</strong> Demand large out-of-sample evidence, respect regression to the mean, and calibrate probabilities against objective scoring rules like Brier score and CLV.</p>'
