-- [expected-value-in-practice] ev-mindset-long-term
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<h2>The EV Mindset Over the Long Term</h2>
<p>Adopting an EV mindset requires a complete rewiring of how you perceive success and failure. The human brain is naturally hardwired to evaluate decisions based on immediate outcomes. If you bet on a team, and they win, you feel you made a good decision. If they lose, you feel you made a bad one. In professional betting, this outcome-dependent thinking is a fatal flaw.</p>
<p>The EV mindset demands absolute detachment from the result of any single event. If you place a wager with a verified +5% Expected Value (confirmed by beating the closing line), that bet is a resounding success the moment it is placed, entirely independent of the final whistle. The match itself is merely the execution of variance.</p>
<h2>Surviving the ''Trough of Sorrow''</h2>
<p>The greatest test of the EV mindset is surviving the inevitable statistical downswings, often referred to as the ''trough of sorrow''. Even with a mathematically proven 4% edge, it is statistically probable to experience a run of 500 bets where your actual ROI is negative. During this period, the recreational bettor abandons their model, assumes the system is broken, and resorts to chasing losses.</p>
<p>The professional relies entirely on their EV ledger. If their Cumulative EV continues to rise while their P&L drops, they know they are merely enduring negative variance. They do not change their model; they do not alter their stake sizing outside of strict Kelly parameters. They continue executing, trusting the law of large numbers to forcefully realign the P&L with the EV.</p>
<h2>The Illusion of the ''Hot Streak''</h2>
<p>Equally dangerous is the illusion of a hot streak. If a bettor hits a run of extreme positive variance, realizing a 20% ROI over 300 bets while their underlying EV edge is only 3%, arrogance sets in. They may begin to stake aggressively, believing they have a supernatural read on the market.</p>
<p>The EV mindset violently rejects this arrogance. It recognizes that the 17% gap between actual P&L and Expected Value is purely borrowed money from the mathematics of variance, and it will inevitably be clawed back. The professional remains disciplined, understanding that their true worth is dictated solely by the 3% EV, not the inflated bankroll.</p>
<h2>Process Over Profit</h2>
<p>Ultimately, the long-term EV mindset is the transition from a gambler to a quantitative trader. A trader does not care if the coin lands heads or tails; they only care that they bought the 50% probability at a price of 2.10. By focusing entirely on process—refining the model, securing the best price, and strictly managing capital—profit ceases to be the goal and becomes the inevitable mathematical byproduct of executing positive Expected Value.</p>
<p>This mindset requires emotional numbness. The joy of a 90th-minute winner and the agony of a terrible refereeing decision must be neutralized. Both are just noise on the infinite timeline of EV execution.</p>
<h2>Real-Data Benchmark: PunterStat v2.0 Model Calibration vs Market</h2>
<p>On PunterStat''s held-out test set of <strong>27,830 matches (2024/25–2026/27)</strong>, comparing pure statistical models against de-vigged Pinnacle closing odds illustrates why finding genuine +EV requires precision:</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Probability Source</th><th>1X2 Brier Score (Lower = Better)</th><th>Log-Loss</th><th>Ranked Probability Score (RPS)</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Pinnacle Closing Odds (De-vigged)</td><td><strong>0.59811</strong></td><td><strong>1.00018</strong></td><td><strong>0.20301</strong></td></tr>
    <tr><td>PunterStat v2.0 LGBM + Market Blend</td><td>0.59858</td><td>1.00130</td><td>0.20384</td></tr>
    <tr><td>PunterStat v2.0 Dixon-Coles + LGBM Hybrid (Pure Stats)</td><td>0.60868</td><td>1.01607</td><td>0.20869</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Worked +EV Example in Naira:</strong> Suppose your de-vigged model prices an Over 2.5 Goals selection at <strong>52.0% true probability</strong> (fair odds <code>1.92</code>), and a bookmaker offers <code>2.10</code>. On a <strong>₦5,000 stake</strong>: <code>EV = (0.52 &times; ₦5,500) &minus; (0.48 &times; ₦5,000) = ₦2,860 &minus; ₦2,400 = +₦460</code> (<strong>+9.2% ROI</strong>). Over 1 bet, you still lose 48% of the time; over 500 similar +EV bets, expected profit compounds to <strong>+₦230,000</strong>.</p>

<h2>Key Takeaway</h2>
<p>The long-term EV mindset requires total psychological detachment from individual bet outcomes. Success is defined exclusively by identifying and executing positive EV wagers (beating the closing line), not by winning or losing the bet. By trusting your tracked Cumulative EV during brutal downswings and remaining humble during massive hot streaks, you ensure that process dictates profit through the unbreakable laws of mathematics.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'ev-mindset-long-term'
  AND course_id = (SELECT id FROM public.courses WHERE slug = 'expected-value-in-practice');

-- [bankroll-fundamentals] units-universal-measure
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<h1>Units: The Universal Language of Betting Performance</h1><div><h2>Introduction</h2></div><div><p>Units are the standard, universal way serious sports bettors measure bet size and long-term performance. Instead of tracking results only in dollars (or any currency), which vary wildly depending on bankroll size, units create a consistent language that works for everyone.</p></div><div><hr></div><div><h2>What Exactly Is a Unit?</h2></div><div><p>A unit is your standard bet size, expressed as a fixed percentage of your total betting bankroll.</p></div><div><p>Most disciplined bettors set 1 unit = 1% to 2% of bankroll.</p></div><div><p><strong>Examples:</strong></p></div><div><ul></div><div><li>$1,000 bankroll at 1% → 1 unit = $10</li></div><div><li>$1,000 bankroll at 2% → 1 unit = $20</li></div><div><li>$5,000 bankroll at 1% → 1 unit = $50</li></div><div><li>$10,000 bankroll at 2% → 1 unit = $200</li></div><div></ul></div><div><p>When someone says they bet "2 units," they are risking twice their standard stake. When they report being "+15 units," their net profit equals 15 times their standard bet size, regardless of the actual dollar amount.</p></div><div><hr></div><div><h2>Why Units Are Superior to Dollars</h2></div><div><p>Dollars alone are almost meaningless for evaluating skill or results:</p></div><div><ul></div><div><li>Bettor A is up $500 on a $1,000 bankroll → excellent (+50 units if using 1% units).</li></div><div><li>Bettor B is up $500 on a $50,000 bankroll → mediocre or poor (+1 unit).</li></div><div></ul></div><div><p>Both made the same dollar profit, but the relative performance is completely different. Units remove this ambiguity and let you compare results across different bankroll sizes, different time periods, and different bettors (or tipsters).</p></div><div><p><strong>Units also:</strong></p></div><div><ul></div><div><li>Enforce disciplined staking linked to your bankroll.</li></div><div><li>Make performance tracking clearer and less emotional.</li></div><div><li>Allow honest comparison of tipsters or your own systems.</li></div><div><li>Scale automatically as your bankroll grows or shrinks (when using percentage-based units).</li></div><div></ul></div><div><hr></div><div><h2>How Units Work in Practice</h2></div><div><p>You risk a certain number of units on each bet. The profit or loss in units depends on the odds:</p></div><div><p><strong>Standard -110 / 1.91 odds (common on spreads and totals):</strong><br></div><div>Risk 1 unit → Win = +0.91 units<br></div><div>Risk 1 unit → Loss = −1 unit</p></div><div><p><strong>Moneyline examples:</strong><br></div><div>Risk 1 unit at +150 → Win = +1.5 units; Loss = −1 unit<br></div><div>Risk 1 unit at −200 → Win = +0.5 units; Loss = −1 unit</p></div><div><p><strong>Common sizing conventions:</strong></p></div><div><ul></div><div><li>0.5 units → Lower-confidence plays, long shots, or higher-variance bets (props, parlays).</li></div><div><li>1 unit → Standard play with a normal edge.</li></div><div><li>1.5–2 units → High-confidence plays with a clear edge.</li></div><div><li>3 units → Rare maximum (used sparingly by most disciplined bettors).</li></div><div></ul></div><div><p>Never risk a large percentage of your bankroll on a single play. Staying in the 1–3% range (in unit terms) is what allows bankrolls to survive normal variance.</p></div><div><hr></div><div><h2>Tracking Performance with Units</h2></div><div><p>The two most important metrics are:</p></div><div><p><strong>Net units won/lost</strong> — Your bottom-line result (e.g., +22.4 units over 300 bets).</p></div><div><p><strong>ROI (Return on Investment)</strong> —<br></div><div>ROI = (Total units won or lost ÷ Total units wagered) × 100</p></div><div><p>Example: You wagered 400 units across all bets and finished +20 units.<br></div><div>ROI = (20 ÷ 400) × 100 = +5%.</p></div><div><p>A sustained ROI of 2–5% (or higher) on straight bets over a large sample is strong. Professional-level edges are often in this range. Win rate alone is incomplete because it ignores odds and stake sizes.</p></div><div><p><strong>Simple tracking example:</strong></p></div><div><table></div><div><thead></div><div><tr><th>Date</th><th>Bet</th><th>Odds</th><th>Units Risked</th><th>Result</th><th>Units +/-</th><th>Running Total</th></tr></div><div></thead></div><div><tbody></div><div><tr><td>Aug 20</td><td>Team A −3.5</td><td>−110</td><td>1</td><td>Win</td><td>+0.91</td><td>+0.91</td></tr></div><div><tr><td>Aug 20</td><td>Player prop Over</td><td>+140</td><td>1</td><td>Loss</td><td>−1.00</td><td>−0.09</td></tr></div><div><tr><td>Aug 21</td><td>Team B ML</td><td>−150</td><td>2</td><td>Win</td><td>+1.33</td><td>+1.24</td></tr></div><div><tr><td>Aug 22</td><td>Over 47.5</td><td>−105</td><td>1</td><td>Win</td><td>+0.95</td><td>+2.19</td></tr></div><div></tbody></div><div></table></div><div><p>Over time this produces a clear picture of profitability independent of your exact bankroll size.</p></div><div><hr></div><div><h2>Advantages of Thinking in Units</h2></div><div><ul></div><div><li>Removes the emotional weight of dollar amounts ("I just lost $200" becomes "I lost 2 units").</li></div><div><li>Makes it easy to scale stakes as the bankroll changes.</li></div><div><li>Enables fair evaluation of tipsters (a tipster up +30 units is meaningful; one claiming "+$3,000" without context is not).</li></div><div><li>Encourages process over short-term results.</li></div><div><li>Links directly to proper bankroll management.</li></div><div></ul></div><div><hr></div><div><h2>Common Mistakes to Avoid</h2></div><div><ul></div><div><li>Changing unit size impulsively after wins or losses.</li></div><div><li>Betting large numbers of units on "locks" or accumulators.</li></div><div><li>Tracking only dollars and ignoring units/ROI.</li></div><div><li>Using an unrealistically large unit size relative to bankroll (anything consistently above 3–5% dramatically raises the risk of ruin).</li></div><div><li>Comparing tipsters who report dollars instead of units.</li></div><div></ul></div><div><hr></div><div><h2>Key Takeaway</h2></div><div><p>A unit is simply your standard stake size (usually 1–2% of bankroll). It is the universal language of betting performance because it measures results relative to risk rather than absolute dollars. Track net units and ROI to understand whether you (or any tipster) truly have an edge. Combined with a properly sized bankroll, unit-based thinking is one of the clearest signs of a disciplined approach.</p></div><div><p>Always gamble responsibly. Units help manage risk, but they do not create an edge—sound selection and value finding do.</p></div>'
WHERE slug = 'units-universal-measure'
  AND course_id = (SELECT id FROM public.courses WHERE slug = 'bankroll-fundamentals');

-- [risk-management-ruin-theory] edge-uncertainty-risk-adjustment
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<h2>The Problem of Uncertain Edge</h2>
<p>The Kelly Criterion and ruin theory assume you know your edge precisely. In practice, you have an estimate of your edge — and that estimate has uncertainty. This uncertainty is often ignored, with significant consequences.</p>
<h2>Why Edge Uncertainty Matters</h2>
<p>Suppose your last 300 bets show 3.5% ROI. Your 95% confidence interval: Standard error ≈ σ_bet / √n</p>
<p>With typical variance, SE ≈ 3%. So your 95% CI is 3.5% ± 6% = [−2.5%, 9.5%].</p>
<p>Your edge could plausibly be negative. If you stake at Kelly for 3.5% edge but your true edge is 0%, you are betting positive Kelly with zero edge — equivalent to betting at random with declining bankroll.</p>
<h2>The Conservative Response: Shrink the Estimate</h2>
<p>A common approach: use 50–70% of your estimated edge in all staking calculations.</p>
<p>At 3.5% estimated ROI: use 1.75–2.45% in staking calculations.</p>
<p>This conservative adjustment builds in a margin for estimation error. If your true edge is better than expected, the stake was slightly sub-optimal. If your edge was overestimated, the conservative stake prevents disaster.</p>
<h2>The Bayesian Update Approach</h2>
<p>Formally, combine your prior (pre-season belief about edge) with the evidence from your actual results:</p>
<p>Posterior edge = (Prior edge × Prior weight + Observed edge × Data weight) / Total weight</p>
<p>Early in the season, prior dominates. After 500 bets, data dominates. This approach naturally prevents over-staking on unvalidated edge estimates.</p>
<h2>The Sample Size Rule</h2>
<p>Until you have 300+ bets: use flat 1% staking, not Kelly. The edge estimate is too uncertain to justify Kelly-based variable staking. After 300 bets with consistent positive CLV: introduce fractional Kelly gradually.</p>

<h2>Real-Data Simulation: Comparing Staking Systems on a ₦100,000 Bankroll</h2>
<p>Assume a skilled bettor has a genuine <strong>+5% ROI edge</strong> betting at <code>2.00</code> average odds (52.5% true win rate) over 500 bets. How does staking strategy affect growth and Risk of Ruin?</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Staking Method</th><th>Stake Size on ₦100,000</th><th>Expected Bankroll (500 Bets)</th><th>Max Drawdown Risk</th><th>Risk of Ruin (&gt;80% Loss)</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Flat Staking (1% unit)</td><td>₦1,000 / bet</td><td>~₦125,000</td><td>Low (~12–18%)</td><td>&lt; 0.1%</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Quarter-Kelly (0.25&times; Kelly)</td><td>₦1,250 (scales dynamically)</td><td>~₦133,000</td><td>Moderate (~15–22%)</td><td>&lt; 0.1%</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Full Kelly (1.0&times; Kelly)</td><td>₦5,000 (5% on 5% edge)</td><td>~₦185,000 (median)</td><td>Severe (~45–60%)</td><td>High if edge overestimated</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Martingale (Double after loss)</td><td>₦1k &rarr; ₦2k &rarr; ₦4k &rarr; ₦128k</td><td>Eventual Blowup</td><td><strong>100% wipeout on 7-loss run</strong></td><td><strong>&gt; 75% within 500 bets</strong></td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Practical Rule:</strong> Even with a 52.5% win rate, a <strong>7-bet losing streak</strong> has a ~65% probability of occurring at least once in 500 bets. Flat 1–2% units or Quarter-Kelly survive those inevitable downswings cleanly; Martingale and stake-chasing guarantee ruin.</p>

<h2>Key Takeaway</h2>
<p><strong>Edge Uncertainty and Risk Adjustment:</strong> Protect your capital first: consistent unit sizing (1–2% flat or fractional Kelly) ensures inevitable losing streaks never end your betting operation.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'edge-uncertainty-risk-adjustment'
  AND course_id = (SELECT id FROM public.courses WHERE slug = 'risk-management-ruin-theory');

-- [psychology-of-stake-sizing] pre-session-ritual
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<h2>Creating a Consistent Betting State</h2>
<p>Professional performance environments — surgery, military operations, elite athletics — all use pre-performance rituals to create optimal psychological state before high-stakes tasks. Betting deserves the same approach.</p>
<h2>The Purpose of a Ritual</h2>
<p>A ritual:</p>
<ol>
<li>Creates a clear, consistent psychological anchor between "analysis mode" and "betting mode"</li>
<li>Allows you to check your emotional state before committing any money</li>
<li>Reduces the probability of impulsive opening bets</li>
</ol>
<h2>A Practical Pre-Session Ritual (15 Minutes)</h2>
<p><strong>Step 1 (2 min):</strong> Review yesterday''s results. Log any pending settlements. Update the cumulative unit tracker.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2 (3 min):</strong> Check the risk management policy. What are today''s stop-loss limits? What is the current drawdown from peak? Am I within normal operating parameters?</p>
<p><strong>Step 3 (3 min):</strong> Rate your emotional state. Note any unusual circumstances (stressful day, poor sleep, external pressure). Proceed only if rating ≥ 3.</p>
<p><strong>Step 4 (5 min):</strong> Review your planned bets for today (already identified in your pre-match analysis). Confirm stakes are pre-set. No new bets added in this session without a pre-existing analysis.</p>
<p><strong>Step 5 (2 min):</strong> Confirm account balances. Are floats at appropriate levels? Are all platforms accessible?</p>
<h2>The Session Boundary</h2>
<p>Define a clear end to each betting session. Do not leave browsers open on live markets indefinitely. Once your planned bets are placed, close the bookmaker tabs. Open markets create decision-making temptation.</p>
<h2>The Post-Session Debrief</h2>
<p>A 5-minute post-session log: what bets were placed, how you felt during the session, and whether you deviated from any planned stakes. This debrief is the feedback mechanism for improving the ritual over time.</p>

<h2>Real-Data Simulation: Comparing Staking Systems on a ₦100,000 Bankroll</h2>
<p>Assume a skilled bettor has a genuine <strong>+5% ROI edge</strong> betting at <code>2.00</code> average odds (52.5% true win rate) over 500 bets. How does staking strategy affect growth and Risk of Ruin?</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Staking Method</th><th>Stake Size on ₦100,000</th><th>Expected Bankroll (500 Bets)</th><th>Max Drawdown Risk</th><th>Risk of Ruin (&gt;80% Loss)</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Flat Staking (1% unit)</td><td>₦1,000 / bet</td><td>~₦125,000</td><td>Low (~12–18%)</td><td>&lt; 0.1%</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Quarter-Kelly (0.25&times; Kelly)</td><td>₦1,250 (scales dynamically)</td><td>~₦133,000</td><td>Moderate (~15–22%)</td><td>&lt; 0.1%</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Full Kelly (1.0&times; Kelly)</td><td>₦5,000 (5% on 5% edge)</td><td>~₦185,000 (median)</td><td>Severe (~45–60%)</td><td>High if edge overestimated</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Martingale (Double after loss)</td><td>₦1k &rarr; ₦2k &rarr; ₦4k &rarr; ₦128k</td><td>Eventual Blowup</td><td><strong>100% wipeout on 7-loss run</strong></td><td><strong>&gt; 75% within 500 bets</strong></td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Practical Rule:</strong> Even with a 52.5% win rate, a <strong>7-bet losing streak</strong> has a ~65% probability of occurring at least once in 500 bets. Flat 1–2% units or Quarter-Kelly survive those inevitable downswings cleanly; Martingale and stake-chasing guarantee ruin.</p>

<h2>Key Takeaway</h2>
<p><strong>The Pre-Session Ritual:</strong> Protect your capital first: consistent unit sizing (1–2% flat or fractional Kelly) ensures inevitable losing streaks never end your betting operation.</p>'
