-- [sample-size-and-variance] confidence-intervals-betting
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<h2>What a Confidence Interval Tells You</h2>
<p>A 95% confidence interval for your ROI gives the range within which your true ROI almost certainly falls, given your observed results. It quantifies how uncertain your performance estimate is.</p>
<h2>The Formula</h2>
<p>For n bets, observed ROI r, and standard error SE:</p>
<p>SE = σ / √n</p>
<p>95% CI = r ± 1.96 × SE</p>
<p>Where σ is the standard deviation of your per-bet returns.</p>
<h2>A Worked Example</h2>
<p>After 300 bets: observed ROI = +3.5%, σ = 1.0 unit per bet (typical even-money betting)</p>
<p>SE = 1.0 / √300 = 0.0577 units = 5.77% (per unit staked)</p>
<p>95% CI = 3.5% ± 1.96 × 5.77% = 3.5% ± 11.3% = [−7.8%, +14.8%]</p>
<p>Interpretation: "My true ROI is between −7.8% and +14.8% with 95% confidence." The confidence interval includes zero — so at 300 bets, you cannot rule out zero edge.</p>
<h2>Using Confidence Intervals Operationally</h2>
<p>Run a confidence interval calculation at every 100-bet milestone. Track how the interval narrows as the sample grows. Only begin scaling stakes when the 95% CI lower bound is above 0% (the entire interval is positive).</p>
<p>This is the statistical test that distinguishes validated edge from lucky variance.</p>
<h2>The Upper Bound Information</h2>
<p>The upper bound of the CI is also informative: "My true ROI is probably not higher than X%." This constrains the maximum expected return and prevents overconfident scaling based on hot streaks.</p>

<h2>Real-Data Benchmark: Sample Size, Variance &amp; Walk-Forward Stability</h2>
<p>Short-term win rates fool almost every bettor. Even on a fair 50/50 proposition (<code>2.00</code> odds), the 95% confidence interval around your observed win rate shrinks only with the <em>square root</em> of sample size (<code>&radic;n</code>):</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Settled Bets (n)</th><th>95% Win-Rate Confidence Band (at True 50%)</th><th>95% ROI Noise Band at 2.00 Odds</th><th>Statistical Verdict</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>25 bets</td><td>30.4% – 69.6%</td><td>&minus;39.2% to +39.2%</td><td>Pure noise — impossible to judge skill</td></tr>
    <tr><td>100 bets</td><td>40.2% – 59.8%</td><td>&minus;19.6% to +19.6%</td><td>Early directional signal only</td></tr>
    <tr><td>500 bets</td><td>45.6% – 54.4%</td><td>&minus;8.8% to +8.8%</td><td>Meaningful performance evaluation</td></tr>
    <tr><td>1,000 bets</td><td>46.9% – 53.1%</td><td>&minus;6.2% to +6.2%</td><td>Reliable statistical proof of edge</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Out-of-Sample Proof (PunterStat v2.0.0):</strong> Across 3 strict walk-forward seasons (never peeking into future matches), PunterStat''s model held steady Brier scores of <code>0.60460</code> (2022/23), <code>0.60983</code> (2023/24), and <code>0.60887</code> (2024/25+) — proving that true statistical validation requires out-of-sample testing over thousands of events, never cherry-picked hot streaks.</p>

<h2>Key Takeaway</h2>
<p><strong>Confidence Intervals for Betting Performance:</strong> Demand large out-of-sample evidence, respect regression to the mean, and calibrate probabilities against objective scoring rules like Brier score and CLV.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'confidence-intervals-betting'
  AND course_id = (SELECT id FROM public.courses WHERE slug = 'sample-size-and-variance');

-- [exchange-betting-lay-markets] liquidity-price-discovery-market-depth
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<h2><h1>Liquidity, Price Discovery, and Market Depth</h1><p>On betting exchanges, three concepts determine whether you can execute a trade efficiently, at what price, and in what size. These are <strong>liquidity</strong>, <strong>price discovery</strong>, and <strong>market depth</strong>. Understanding them is essential for anyone moving beyond basic fixed-odds betting into exchange trading or serious value betting.</p><h2>1. Liquidity</h2><p>Liquidity is the amount of money available to be matched at a given price. It measures how easily you can enter or exit a position without causing a significant change in the odds.</p><p>High liquidity means:</p><ul><li>Large stakes can be matched quickly.</li><li>The gap between back and lay prices (the spread) is narrow.</li><li>Your bet is less likely to move the market.</li><li>Partial matches are less frequent.</li></ul><p>Low liquidity means:</p><ul><li>Only small stakes are available at the best prices.</li><li>The spread between back and lay is wider.</li><li>Your own bet may shift the odds.</li><li>You may struggle to exit a position without taking a worse price.</li></ul><h3>Where Liquidity Is Found</h3><ul><li><strong>Major football leagues</strong> — Premier League, La Liga, Serie A, Bundesliga, Champions League.</li><li><strong>Grand Slam tennis</strong> — Wimbledon, US Open, French Open, Australian Open.</li><li><strong>Major horse racing festivals</strong> — Cheltenham, Royal Ascot, Kentucky Derby, Melbourne Cup.</li><li><strong>Popular US sports</strong> — NFL, NBA, MLB, NHL on major exchanges.</li><li><strong>Major golf tournaments</strong> — The Masters, The Open, PGA Tour events.</li></ul><h3>Where Liquidity Is Thin</h3><ul><li>Lower-league football and minor divisions.</li><li>Niche sports (darts, snooker, volleyball, handball).</li><li>Exotic props and player specials.</li><li>Early markets days before an event.</li><li>Secondary exchanges (Smarkets, Matchbook, Betdaq) on anything other than top events.</li></ul><h3>Liquidity and In-Play Trading</h3><p>Liquidity is even more critical in-play. You need to enter and exit quickly as game states change. On major events, in-play liquidity is often excellent. On smaller events, it can evaporate entirely once the action starts.</p><p><strong>Example:</strong><br>Betfair might show ₦500,000 matched pre-match on a Premier League fixture. During the match, an additional ₦2–5 million may be matched. On a third-tier match, total matched volume might be only ₦10,000–₦50,000, and the in-play order book may be nearly empty.</p><h2>2. Price Discovery</h2><p>Price discovery is the process by which the market determines the "correct" odds for an outcome. On exchanges, no single entity sets the price. It emerges from the interaction of thousands of bettors, each acting on their own information, opinion, and bankroll.</p><p>The order book is the mechanism of price discovery:</p><ul><li><strong>Backers</strong> express demand by offering to buy at a certain price.</li><li><strong>Layers</strong> express supply by offering to sell (lay) at a certain price.</li><li>The <strong>midpoint</strong> between the best back and best lay price often represents the market''s consensus at that moment.</li></ul><p>As new information enters the market — team news, injuries, goals, red cards, weather, betting volume — the balance of back and lay orders shifts, and the price moves to reflect the new consensus.</p><h3>Price Discovery vs Sportsbook Odds</h3><p>Sportsbooks set prices based on their own models and risk management. They may shade lines to balance their book or exploit public bias. Exchanges reveal the market''s raw, unfiltered consensus.</p><p>Because exchange prices represent actual matched money, they are often used by sharp bettors and analysts as a benchmark for "true" market probability.</p><h3>Example of Price Discovery</h3><p>Pre-match, a football team is priced at 2.50 on the exchange. Suddenly, team news confirms the star striker is injured. Within seconds:</p><ul><li>Layers see the news and accept existing back orders, causing the back price to drift.</li><li>Backers see the news and either cancel their orders or demand a higher price.</li><li>The price moves from 2.50 to 2.80, then settles around 2.75 as new equilibrium is found.</li></ul><p>The new price of 2.75 reflects the market''s updated probability estimate. This is price discovery in action.</p><h2>3. Market Depth</h2><p>Market depth refers to the quantity of money available at each price level in the order book, both above and below the current best prices. It shows how much size the market can absorb before the price moves.</p><p>A deep market has:</p><ul><li>Large amounts at multiple price points.</li><li>Small price impact from individual large bets.</li><li>Greater stability and less volatility from single orders.</li></ul><p>A shallow market has:</p><ul><li>Small amounts at each price level.</li><li>Large price movements from relatively modest bets.</li><li>Higher volatility and wider spreads.</li></ul><h3>Reading the Order Book</h3><p>A typical exchange order book for a selection might look like this:</p><table><thead><tr><th>Back</th><th>Amount</th><th>Price</th><th>Amount</th><th>Lay</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Yes</td><td>₦1,250</td><td>2.04</td><td>₦800</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td>Yes</td><td>₦2,000</td><td>2.02</td><td>₦1,500</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td>Yes</td><td>₦3,500</td><td>2.00</td><td>₦2,200</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td></td><td></td><td>1.99</td><td>₦1,000</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td></td><td></td><td>1.98</td><td>₦3,000</td><td>No</td></tr></tbody></table><p>In this example, the best back price is 2.04 with ₦1,250 available. The best lay price is 1.99 with ₦1,000 available. The spread is 0.05. If you want to back ₦5,000 immediately, you would consume the ₦1,250 at 2.04, ₦2,000 at 2.02, and part of the ₦3,500 at 2.00. Your average price would be worse than the best displayed price.</p><p>This is why depth matters — especially for larger stakes.</p><h3>Depth and Large Orders</h3><p>If you are placing a ₦10,000 bet on a market with only ₦3,000 available at the best prices, you have two choices:</p><ul><li><strong>Take what is available</strong> and accept a worse average price by walking up or down the order book.</li><li><strong>Place a limit order</strong> at your desired price and wait for more liquidity to arrive.</li></ul><p>The first option ensures execution but costs you value. The second preserves value but risks the bet not matching fully or at all.</p><h2>How These Three Concepts Interact</h2><p>Liquidity, price discovery, and market depth are deeply connected:</p><ul><li><strong>High liquidity</strong> usually means <strong>deep markets</strong> and <strong>efficient price discovery</strong>.</li><li><strong>Low liquidity</strong> leads to <strong>shallow depth</strong>, wider spreads, and slower or less reliable price discovery.</li><li><strong>Deep markets</strong> attract more participants, which further improves liquidity and price efficiency — a virtuous cycle.</li><li><strong>Shallow markets</strong> discourage large traders, which limits liquidity and makes prices more volatile.</li></ul><p><strong>Example:</strong><br>On a Premier League match, there may be ₦50,000–₦100,000 available within 0.05 ticks of the best price. A ₦5,000 bet barely moves the market.<br>On a lower-league match, there may be only ₦200 available at the best price. A ₦500 bet could shift the odds by several ticks.</p><h2>Practical Implications for Bettors</h2><h3>For Back Bettors</h3><ul><li>Check the best back price and the available amount before placing your stake.</li><li>If your stake exceeds the available amount, expect a worse average price.</li><li>Consider splitting large bets into smaller orders or using limit orders to avoid moving the market.</li><li>In thin markets, be patient. Placing a limit order and waiting can get you a better price than taking what is available immediately.</li></ul><h3>For Layers</h3><ul><li>Always check the available lay amount at your desired price.</li><li>In shallow markets, a large lay can move the price against you before it is fully matched.</li><li>Be aware that your liability may be much larger than your potential profit, especially at high odds.</li><li>Lay in liquid markets whenever possible to ensure you can exit if the market moves against you.</li></ul><h3>For Traders</h3><ul><li>Depth is your friend. Deep markets allow quick entry and exit without excessive slippage.</li><li>Watch how order book depth changes before and during events. Sudden shifts in depth can signal incoming information or large money.</li><li>Use the order book to anticipate short-term price movements. If the lay side is thin and the back side is deep, price may shorten as buyers overwhelm sellers.</li><li>In shallow markets, even small trades can move the price, making trading less predictable and more costly.</li></ul><h2>Common Misconceptions</h2><ul><li><strong>"High matched volume equals high current liquidity"</strong> — Matched volume shows what has already traded. Current liquidity is what is available right now. They are related but not the same.</li><li><strong>"The best displayed price is always available in full size"</strong> — It is only available up to the amount shown. Larger orders will walk the book.</li><li><strong>"Exchanges always have better odds"</strong> — On illiquid markets, the exchange may have a wide spread and no money at competitive prices. A sportsbook may actually offer a better price.</li><li><strong>"A deep market is always stable"</strong> — Even deep markets can move violently on major news. Depth reduces but does not eliminate price impact.</li></ul><h2>Summary</h2><p>Liquidity, price discovery, and market depth form the foundation of how betting exchanges function. Liquidity determines whether you can trade at all. Price discovery determines what the market believes. Market depth determines how much you can trade before moving the price.</p><p>Successful exchange users treat these concepts as essential tools. They check the order book before placing orders. They understand the spread and the available depth. They adapt their stake sizes to the market''s capacity. And they recognise that exchanges are not uniformly deep — the same platform can be highly liquid on one event and nearly empty on another.</p><p>The exchange is a market. Like any market, those who understand its structure — and respect its limitations — will consistently make better decisions than those who simply take the first price they see.</p></h2>

<h2>Key Takeaway</h2>
<p><strong>Liquidity, Price Discovery, and Market Depth:</strong> Always convert offered odds into de-vigged fair probability, compare across bookmakers, and measure your decisions against the sharp closing line.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'liquidity-price-discovery-market-depth'
  AND course_id = (SELECT id FROM public.courses WHERE slug = 'exchange-betting-lay-markets');

-- [handicap-and-asian-handicap] handicap-betting-other-sports
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<h2><h1>Handicap Betting in Other Sports</h1><p>Handicap betting is not exclusive to football. Almost every sport with a score or finishing order offers some form of spread or handicap market. The principles are the same: apply a virtual deficit or head start, then settle based on the adjusted result. What differs is the scoring system, the typical margin distribution, and the market mechanics.</p><h2>1. Basketball (NBA, EuroLeague, NCAA)</h2><p>Basketball uses the <strong>point spread</strong> model. The most common lines are half-point spreads to eliminate pushes.</p><ul><li>Los Angeles Lakers –5.5 vs Miami Heat +5.5</li><li>Lakers must win by 6 or more for the favourite to cover</li><li>Heat can lose by 5 or fewer, or win outright, for the underdog to cover</li></ul><p>Whole-number lines like –6.0 are used but push when the margin lands exactly on 6. Pushes in basketball are more common than in football because scoring is higher and margins repeat.</p><p>Quarter-point lines are rare in basketball. The standard is half-point. Some sportsbooks offer "alternate spreads" which allow you to move the line in exchange for adjusted odds.</p><h3>Key features</h3><ul><li>High-scoring games produce wider spreads (typically 3 to 15 points)</li><li>Home-court advantage is typically worth 2.5 to 3.5 points</li><li>Overtime counts toward the final spread result</li><li>Player injuries and load management move lines sharply</li></ul><h2>2. American Football (NFL, College Football)</h2><p>NFL and college football are the original home of the point spread. Lines are expressed in points, typically with half-point increments.</p><ul><li>Kansas City Chiefs –3.5 vs Buffalo Bills +3.5</li><li>Chiefs must win by 4 or more</li><li>Bills can lose by 3 or fewer, or win outright</li></ul><p>Key numbers matter more in football than in any other sport. Because scoring happens in increments of 3 (field goals) and 7 (touchdowns with extra point), certain margins — 3, 7, 10, 14 — occur far more often than others.</p><p>This is why a line of –2.5 is not simply "a bit lower" than –3.5. Moving across the 3 or 7 is disproportionately valuable. Whole-number spreads on key numbers push frequently, which is why half-points around those numbers are aggressively priced.</p><h3>Key features</h3><ul><li>Margins cluster around 3, 7, 10, 14, and 17</li><li>Overtime counts toward the final margin</li><li>Weather (wind, snow) can lower scoring and tighten spreads</li><li>College football has wider talent gaps, producing spreads of 30+ points in mismatches</li></ul><h2>3. Rugby (Union and League)</h2><p>Rugby uses a <strong>points handicap</strong> similar to football spreads. Margins are expressed in points, and both half-point and whole-number lines are used.</p><p>Example: New Zealand –12.5 vs Australia +12.5</p><p>Rugby union has scoring increments of 5 (try) and 2 (conversion) plus 3 (penalty/drop goal), which creates clusters at 7, 10, 12, and multiples thereof. Rugby league has different scoring values (4 for try, 2 for goal, 1 for drop goal), which shifts the common margin distribution.</p><p>Draws are extremely rare in rugby league and uncommon in union, but they do occur. Handicap markets usually use half-points to avoid pushes. Whole-number handicap draws are possible when the adjusted margin lands exactly on the line.</p><h3>Key features</h3><ul><li>Scoring structure creates natural margin clusters</li><li>Handicaps are wider in mismatches (20+ points)</li><li>Red cards and weather conditions materially affect scoring</li><li>In-play handicap movement can be extreme after early tries</li></ul><h2>4. Cricket (Test, ODI, T20)</h2><p>Cricket handicap betting is less standardised than football or basketball. Different bookmakers use different formats.</p><h3>Run line (T20 and ODI)</h3><ul><li>Typically expressed as runs: India –15.5 runs vs Pakistan +15.5 runs</li><li>Favourite must win by 16 runs or more</li><li>Underdog can lose by 15 runs or fewer, or win</li></ul><h3>Wicket handicap (Test and first-class)</h3><ul><li>Expressed in wickets: Australia –3.5 wickets vs England +3.5 wickets</li><li>Based on total wickets taken, not runs</li></ul><h3>Innings handicap (Test)</h3><ul><li>Applied to first-innings lead: Team A –40.5 runs on first innings</li></ul><p>Cricket handicap betting is complicated by weather interruptions, pitch conditions, and the Duckworth-Lewis-Stern method in limited-overs matches. Settlement rules vary by bookmaker, and abandoned matches may void bets depending on the market.</p><h3>Key features</h3><ul><li>Run lines in T20 are typically 10–25 runs</li><li>Test cricket handicaps are less common and less liquid</li><li>In-play handicaps shift dramatically with wickets and run rate</li><li>Always check settlement rules for rain-affected matches</li></ul><h2>5. Tennis</h2><p>Tennis uses a <strong>game handicap</strong> rather than a set handicap in most markets. The favourite gives up a number of games, and the bet settles on total games won, not sets.</p><p>Example: Carlos Alcaraz –3.5 games vs Jannik Sinner +3.5 games</p><ul><li>Alcaraz must win at least 4 more games than Sinner across the match</li><li>Sinner can lose by 3 games or fewer, or win the match</li></ul><p>Set handicaps exist but are less common. Game handicaps are more granular and produce wider odds movement. In Grand Slam matches (best of five sets), game handicaps can be very large — 8.5 games or more — because the total game count is higher.</p><p>Retirements are a major factor in tennis betting. If a player retires mid-match, handicap bets are typically void regardless of the score at the time, but this varies by bookmaker.</p><h3>Key features</h3><ul><li>Game handicaps vary by surface — clay produces longer matches and bigger margins</li><li>Best-of-three vs best-of-five formats change handicap ranges</li><li>Retirements void most handicap bets</li><li>In-play handicap movement reflects momentum shifts and service breaks</li></ul><h2>6. Baseball (MLB, NPB, KBO)</h2><p>Baseball uses the <strong>run line</strong>, which is almost always fixed at –1.5 / +1.5 runs.</p><ul><li>Los Angeles Dodgers –1.5 vs San Francisco Giants +1.5</li><li>Dodgers must win by 2 or more runs</li><li>Giants can lose by 1 run, or win outright</li></ul><p>Because baseball games are typically low-scoring and one-run games are common, the –1.5 run line on the favourite carries much higher odds than the moneyline. Underdogs on the +1.5 run line are popular because they offer a margin of protection.</p><p>Alternate run lines exist (–2.5, +2.5, etc.) but are less efficient and less liquid. The fixed –1.5 structure is the standard.</p><h3>Key features</h3><ul><li>Run line is almost always –1.5 / +1.5</li><li>Starting pitcher is the single most important variable</li><li>National League games (without designated hitter) historically have more one-run outcomes</li><li>Extra innings count toward the run line result</li></ul><h2>7. Ice Hockey (NHL, KHL, SHL)</h2><p>Ice hockey uses a <strong>puck line</strong>, almost always fixed at –1.5 / +1.5 goals.</p><ul><li>Toronto Maple Leafs –1.5 vs Montreal Canadiens +1.5</li><li>Maple Leafs must win by 2 or more goals</li><li>Canadiens can lose by 1, or win outright</li></ul><p>Because hockey is low-scoring and one-goal games are frequent, the –1.5 puck line on the favourite offers inflated odds. Empty-net goals late in games are significant for puck line bets — they often push a one-goal lead to a two-goal lead in the final minute.</p><p>Alternative puck lines (–2.5, +2.5, –0.5, +0.5) are available but less efficient. Overtime and shootout goals count toward puck line settlement.</p><h3>Key features</h3><ul><li>Puck line is almost always –1.5 / +1.5</li><li>Empty-net goals disproportionately affect puck line outcomes</li><li>Overtime and shootouts count</li><li>Goaltender starting confirmation is critical</li></ul><h2>8. Golf</h2><p>Golf handicap betting is less common but exists in two forms: <strong>matchup handicaps</strong> between two players, and <strong>tournament handicaps</strong> applied to the field.</p><p>Player A –1.5 vs Player B +1.5 in a tournament means Player A must finish at least 2 strokes better than Player B. The handicap can be in strokes or finishing position depending on the market.</p><p>Tournament handicap betting applies a stroke adjustment to the entire field, but this is an exotic market with limited liquidity. Matchup handicaps are more accessible but still niche compared to outright or top-10 markets.</p><h3>Key features</h3><ul><li>Handicaps usually expressed in strokes</li><li>Matchup handicap settlement is based on 72-hole total (or shortened event total)</li><li>Withdrawals or missed cuts void most matchup handicap bets</li><li>Weather and course conditions affect margin distributions significantly</li></ul><h2>9. eSports</h2><p>eSports uses both <strong>map handicaps</strong> and <strong>round handicaps</strong> depending on the game.</p><ul><li>Counter-Strike 2: Team A –1.5 maps in a best-of-three</li><li>League of Legends: Team B +5.5 kills in a single map</li><li>Dota 2: Team A –10.5 total kills across a series</li></ul><p>Map handicaps work like set handicaps in tennis — the favourite must win by a certain margin of maps. Round or kill handicaps are more granular and similar to game handicaps.</p><p>eSports handicap markets are growing in liquidity but remain less efficient than traditional sports. Patch changes, roster moves, and map selection can move lines violently. Settlement rules vary by platform and event organiser.</p><h3>Key features</h3><ul><li>Map handicaps for multi-map series</li><li>Kill/round handicaps for single-game markets</li><li>Frequent patch updates shift team strength quickly</li><li>Forfeit or technical issues can void handicap bets depending on the bookmaker</li></ul><h2>Common Principles Across All Handicap Markets</h2><ul><li><strong>Scoring structure determines margin clusters.</strong> In football, 3 and 7 are key numbers. In basketball, 5 to 8 point margins dominate. Know the natural margin distribution for the sport.</li><li><strong>Half-point lines eliminate pushes, whole-point lines invite them.</strong> Some sports (basketball, football) are dominated by half-point spreads. Others (rugby, cricket) use whole points and accept push risk.</li><li><strong>Fixed handicaps vs flexible handicaps.</strong> Baseball and hockey use fixed –1.5 run/puck lines. Basketball and football use flexible spreads that move with the market. Tennis uses flexible game handicaps.</li><li><strong>In-play handicap markets move faster than pre-match.</strong> A single score changes the handicap by more than the points scored because it changes the expected remaining margin.</li><li><strong>Home advantage varies by sport.</strong> It is worth 2.5–3.5 points in basketball, about 2.5 points in NFL, roughly 0.3 goals in football, and is highly variable in rugby and cricket.</li><li><strong>Settlement rules matter.</strong> Overtime counts in basketball, football, baseball, and hockey. Retirements void tennis handicap bets. Rain can void cricket handicap bets. Always check before placing a wager.</li></ul><h2>Handicap Betting Across Sports — Summary Table</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Sport</th><th>Handicap Type</th><th>Standard Unit</th><th>Typical Range</th><th>Common Fixed Line</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Football (soccer)</td><td>Asian Handicap</td><td>Goals</td><td>0 to 2.5</td><td>None (flexible)</td></tr><tr><td>Basketball</td><td>Point spread</td><td>Points</td><td>3 to 15</td><td>None (flexible)</td></tr><tr><td>NFL Football</td><td>Point spread</td><td>Points</td><td>1 to 17</td><td>None (flexible)</td></tr><tr><td>Rugby</td><td>Points handicap</td><td>Points</td><td>5 to 25</td><td>None (flexible)</td></tr><tr><td>Cricket (T20)</td><td>Run line</td><td>Runs</td><td>10 to 25</td><td>None (flexible)</td></tr><tr><td>Tennis</td><td>Game handicap</td><td>Games</td><td>2.5 to 8.5</td><td>None (flexible)</td></tr><tr><td>Baseball</td><td>Run line</td><td>Runs</td><td>1.5</td><td>–1.5 / +1.5 (fixed)</td></tr><tr><td>Ice Hockey</td><td>Puck line</td><td>Goals</td><td>1.5</td><td>–1.5 / +1.5 (fixed)</td></tr><tr><td>Golf</td><td>Matchup handicap</td><td>Strokes</td><td>0.5 to 3.5</td><td>None (flexible)</td></tr><tr><td>eSports</td><td>Map/round handicap</td><td>Maps or kills</td><td>0.5 to 2.5 maps</td><td>None (flexible)</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Key Takeaways</h2><ul><li>The handicap principle is universal — what changes is the scoring unit and the natural margin distribution.</li><li>Basketball and NFL use flexible point spreads dominated by half-point lines.</li><li>Baseball and hockey fix their handicap at –1.5 / +1.5 because the scoring structure makes one-unit margins extremely common.</li><li>Tennis uses game handicaps, which are sensitive to surface, format, and match length.</li><li>Rugby and cricket have natural margin clusters determined by scoring increments.</li><li>eSports handicap markets are growing but less standardised — always verify settlement rules.</li><li>Understanding the sport''s scoring rhythm is the foundation of handicap analysis. The line means nothing without knowing how margins naturally distribute.</li></ul></h2>'
WHERE slug = 'handicap-betting-other-sports'
  AND course_id = (SELECT id FROM public.courses WHERE slug = 'handicap-and-asian-handicap');

-- [risk-management-ruin-theory] scenario-planning-stress-testing
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<h2>Beyond Expected Value</h2>
<p>Bankroll management that only considers expected value and standard variance is incomplete. Rare but real scenarios — model failure, major market shift, simultaneous bad variance across correlated markets — require specific planning.</p>
<h2>The Three Stress Test Scenarios</h2>
<p><strong>Scenario 1: Extended variance (the unlucky run)</strong> Your edge is real but variance delivers a 30-unit losing run. How does your bankroll and staking respond? What is the psychological and financial impact?</p>
<p>Pre-calculation: If you stake 1.5% and experience 30 consecutive losses (each loses 1 unit), total loss = 30 × 1.5% of initial bankroll. At ₦2,000 bankroll: 30 × ₦30 (approximately, given proportional staking) ≈ ₦780 loss. Bankroll drops to ~₦1,220. Remaining operational capacity: 61% of initial. Can you continue? Yes, if stop-loss is set at 50%.</p>
<p><strong>Scenario 2: Edge erosion (the model fails)</strong> Your CLV drops to zero or negative over 6 months. When do you notice? What is your trigger to stop? How much do you lose before stopping?</p>
<p>Pre-calculation: If CLV erodes to 0% from 3%, and you are betting 1.5% per bet with 300 bets remaining before your next review: expected additional loss = 300 × stake × 0% = ₦0 (breakeven). But if edge goes negative (−2%), loss = 300 × stake × 0.02 = meaningful. Monthly reviews prevent this from accumulating.</p>
<p><strong>Scenario 3: Market access restriction cascade</strong> Your top 3 bookmakers restrict your account simultaneously. Your betting capacity drops by 40%. How do you continue operating?</p>
<p>Pre-planning: always maintain 3+ untouched fallback accounts. Never deploy full capacity to a single bookmaker.</p>
<h2>Running the Stress Tests Annually</h2>
<p>Before each season: write out responses to each scenario in your bankroll plan. The written plan is your decision-making guide when the scenario occurs — not an ad hoc response under pressure.</p>

<h2>Real-Data Simulation: Comparing Staking Systems on a ₦100,000 Bankroll</h2>
<p>Assume a skilled bettor has a genuine <strong>+5% ROI edge</strong> betting at <code>2.00</code> average odds (52.5% true win rate) over 500 bets. How does staking strategy affect growth and Risk of Ruin?</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Staking Method</th><th>Stake Size on ₦100,000</th><th>Expected Bankroll (500 Bets)</th><th>Max Drawdown Risk</th><th>Risk of Ruin (&gt;80% Loss)</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Flat Staking (1% unit)</td><td>₦1,000 / bet</td><td>~₦125,000</td><td>Low (~12–18%)</td><td>&lt; 0.1%</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Quarter-Kelly (0.25&times; Kelly)</td><td>₦1,250 (scales dynamically)</td><td>~₦133,000</td><td>Moderate (~15–22%)</td><td>&lt; 0.1%</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Full Kelly (1.0&times; Kelly)</td><td>₦5,000 (5% on 5% edge)</td><td>~₦185,000 (median)</td><td>Severe (~45–60%)</td><td>High if edge overestimated</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Martingale (Double after loss)</td><td>₦1k &rarr; ₦2k &rarr; ₦4k &rarr; ₦128k</td><td>Eventual Blowup</td><td><strong>100% wipeout on 7-loss run</strong></td><td><strong>&gt; 75% within 500 bets</strong></td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Practical Rule:</strong> Even with a 52.5% win rate, a <strong>7-bet losing streak</strong> has a ~65% probability of occurring at least once in 500 bets. Flat 1–2% units or Quarter-Kelly survive those inevitable downswings cleanly; Martingale and stake-chasing guarantee ruin.</p>

<h2>Key Takeaway</h2>
<p><strong>Scenario Planning: Stress Testing Your Bankroll:</strong> Protect your capital first: consistent unit sizing (1–2% flat or fractional Kelly) ensures inevitable losing streaks never end your betting operation.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'scenario-planning-stress-testing'
  AND course_id = (SELECT id FROM public.courses WHERE slug = 'risk-management-ruin-theory');

