-- [advanced-quantitative-methods] event-level-modelling
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<h2>Going Deeper Than Match-Level Statistics</h2>
<p>Most models operate at the match level: aggregate statistics per match (xG total, possession percentage, shots). Event-level modelling uses individual shot or pass events — producing much richer feature sets.</p>
<h2>What Event-Level Data Contains</h2>
<p>Each shot event includes:</p>
<ul>
<li>x/y coordinates on the pitch</li>
<li>Shot type (foot, head, direct free kick)</li>
<li>Assist type (through ball, cross, none)</li>
<li>Situation (open play, set piece, counter-attack)</li>
<li>Whether the goalkeeper was in position</li>
<li>Distance to goal, angle to goal</li>
</ul>
<p>From these raw events, custom features can be constructed:</p>
<ul>
<li>Shot quality distribution (not just total xG, but how it is concentrated)</li>
<li>High-danger chance rate (shots from zones with >25% conversion)</li>
<li>Set piece xG contribution (separate from open play)</li>
<li>Transition speed (time from defensive action to shot)</li>
</ul>
<h2>Building an xG Model From Events</h2>
<p>A custom xG model assigns a probability of scoring to each shot event, based on its characteristics. Fitting this on historical event data (tens of thousands of shots per season) produces highly granular probability estimates.</p>
<p>Advantages over public xG:</p>
<ul>
<li>Tailored to your target leagues and time period</li>
<li>Incorporates features not in public models</li>
<li>Can be updated in near-real-time from live event feeds</li>
</ul>
<h2>The Data Requirement</h2>
<p>Event-level data requires either:</p>
<ul>
<li>Paid commercial data feeds (StatsBomb, Opta, Wyscout)</li>
<li>Open StatsBomb (free for research purposes, limited scope)</li>
<li>Web scraping detailed match logs from sources like Understat (partial event data)</li>
</ul>
<h2>The Analytical Advantage</h2>
<p>Bettors using event-level models often find edge in markets that aggregate-level models miss: player-specific props, half-time markets, and in-play opportunities that depend on granular game-state information.</p>

<h2>Real-Data Benchmark: Sample Size, Variance &amp; Walk-Forward Stability</h2>
<p>Short-term win rates fool almost every bettor. Even on a fair 50/50 proposition (<code>2.00</code> odds), the 95% confidence interval around your observed win rate shrinks only with the <em>square root</em> of sample size (<code>&radic;n</code>):</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Settled Bets (n)</th><th>95% Win-Rate Confidence Band (at True 50%)</th><th>95% ROI Noise Band at 2.00 Odds</th><th>Statistical Verdict</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>25 bets</td><td>30.4% – 69.6%</td><td>&minus;39.2% to +39.2%</td><td>Pure noise — impossible to judge skill</td></tr>
    <tr><td>100 bets</td><td>40.2% – 59.8%</td><td>&minus;19.6% to +19.6%</td><td>Early directional signal only</td></tr>
    <tr><td>500 bets</td><td>45.6% – 54.4%</td><td>&minus;8.8% to +8.8%</td><td>Meaningful performance evaluation</td></tr>
    <tr><td>1,000 bets</td><td>46.9% – 53.1%</td><td>&minus;6.2% to +6.2%</td><td>Reliable statistical proof of edge</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Out-of-Sample Proof (PunterStat v2.0.0):</strong> Across 3 strict walk-forward seasons (never peeking into future matches), PunterStat''s model held steady Brier scores of <code>0.60460</code> (2022/23), <code>0.60983</code> (2023/24), and <code>0.60887</code> (2024/25+) — proving that true statistical validation requires out-of-sample testing over thousands of events, never cherry-picked hot streaks.</p>

<h2>Key Takeaway</h2>
<p><strong>Event-Level Modelling: Beyond Match-Level Data:</strong> Demand large out-of-sample evidence, respect regression to the mean, and calibrate probabilities against objective scoring rules like Brier score and CLV.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'event-level-modelling'
  AND course_id = (SELECT id FROM public.courses WHERE slug = 'advanced-quantitative-methods');

-- [staking-strategies] fractional-kelly-growth-safety
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<h2>The Problem With Full Kelly</h2>
<p>Full Kelly betting produces the maximum long-run growth rate — but the variance is enormous. In the short term (hundreds of bets), you can experience 30–50% drawdowns even with well-calibrated estimates. Most bettors cannot psychologically sustain these drawdowns without abandoning the strategy.</p>
<h2>Fractional Kelly</h2>
<p>Fractional Kelly means betting a fixed fraction of the full Kelly stake. Common fractions:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Quarter Kelly (25%):</strong> Very safe, captures ~75% of Kelly growth rate</li>
<li><strong>Half Kelly (50%):</strong> Moderate, captures ~87.5% of Kelly growth rate</li>
<li><strong>Full Kelly (100%):</strong> Maximum theoretical growth, extreme variance</li>
</ul>
<h2>The Mathematical Trade-Off</h2>
<p>At f fraction of Kelly, the growth rate is proportional to f − f²/2 (relative to full Kelly at 1 − 1/2 = 0.5).</p>
<p>At 50% Kelly: growth rate = 2(0.5) − (0.5)² × (full Kelly growth) ≈ 75% of full Kelly growth, with ~25% of the variance.</p>
<p>At 25% Kelly: growth rate ≈ 44% of full Kelly growth, with ~6% of the variance.</p>
<p>The sharp reduction in variance for modest reduction in expected growth makes fractional Kelly significantly more practical.</p>
<h2>The Professional Standard</h2>
<p>Most professional bettors use 25–33% Kelly. This:</p>
<ul>
<li>Produces meaningful compounding over a season</li>
<li>Limits drawdowns to roughly 15–20% of peak (manageable psychologically)</li>
<li>Accounts for the certainty that probability estimates are not perfectly calibrated</li>
</ul>
<h2>Implementing Fractional Kelly</h2>
<p>Full Kelly calculation: 8.33% (from previous lesson) At 25% Kelly: stake = 0.25 × 8.33% = 2.08% of bankroll At ₦2,000 bankroll: stake = ₦41.60</p>
<p>This is a concrete, mathematically grounded stake — not a guess.</p>

<h2>Real-Data Simulation: Comparing Staking Systems on a ₦100,000 Bankroll</h2>
<p>Assume a skilled bettor has a genuine <strong>+5% ROI edge</strong> betting at <code>2.00</code> average odds (52.5% true win rate) over 500 bets. How does staking strategy affect growth and Risk of Ruin?</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Staking Method</th><th>Stake Size on ₦100,000</th><th>Expected Bankroll (500 Bets)</th><th>Max Drawdown Risk</th><th>Risk of Ruin (&gt;80% Loss)</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Flat Staking (1% unit)</td><td>₦1,000 / bet</td><td>~₦125,000</td><td>Low (~12–18%)</td><td>&lt; 0.1%</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Quarter-Kelly (0.25&times; Kelly)</td><td>₦1,250 (scales dynamically)</td><td>~₦133,000</td><td>Moderate (~15–22%)</td><td>&lt; 0.1%</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Full Kelly (1.0&times; Kelly)</td><td>₦5,000 (5% on 5% edge)</td><td>~₦185,000 (median)</td><td>Severe (~45–60%)</td><td>High if edge overestimated</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Martingale (Double after loss)</td><td>₦1k &rarr; ₦2k &rarr; ₦4k &rarr; ₦128k</td><td>Eventual Blowup</td><td><strong>100% wipeout on 7-loss run</strong></td><td><strong>&gt; 75% within 500 bets</strong></td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Practical Rule:</strong> Even with a 52.5% win rate, a <strong>7-bet losing streak</strong> has a ~65% probability of occurring at least once in 500 bets. Flat 1–2% units or Quarter-Kelly survive those inevitable downswings cleanly; Martingale and stake-chasing guarantee ruin.</p>

<h2>Key Takeaway</h2>
<p><strong>Fractional Kelly: Balancing Growth and Safety:</strong> Protect your capital first: consistent unit sizing (1–2% flat or fractional Kelly) ensures inevitable losing streaks never end your betting operation.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'fractional-kelly-growth-safety'
  AND course_id = (SELECT id FROM public.courses WHERE slug = 'staking-strategies');

-- [building-your-own-lines] validating-model-against-market
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<h2><h1>Validating Your Model Against the Market</h1><p>Building a model is only the beginning. The real question is whether it tells you something the market does not already know. Validation is the process of testing that question rigorously — comparing your model''s outputs to market prices and observed outcomes to determine whether your edge is real or imagined.</p><p>Without validation, you are flying blind. With it, you can separate signal from noise, identify where your model adds value, and refine it over time.</p><h2>1. Why the Market Is the Benchmark</h2><p>The betting market is not perfect, but it is the most information-rich estimate of outcome probabilities available. Sharp bookmakers and exchanges aggregate money from sophisticated participants, incorporate news quickly, and adjust prices in response to new information.</p><p>If your model cannot beat the market consistently, it has no practical betting value — regardless of how sophisticated it is. The market is the standard against which every model must be judged.</p><p>This does not mean the market is always right. It means the market is the default position. Your model must demonstrate that it can improve on that default.</p><h2>2. Types of Validation</h2><p>Validation falls into two broad categories: <strong>backtesting</strong> and <strong>forward testing</strong>.</p><h3>Backtesting</h3><p>Backtesting evaluates how your model would have performed on historical data. You apply the model to past matches, generate probabilities, compare them to historical closing lines, and simulate a betting strategy.</p><p>Advantages:</p><ul><li>Large sample sizes possible</li><li>Fast iteration and parameter testing</li><li>Can evaluate many markets simultaneously</li></ul><p>Risks:</p><ul><li>Overfitting to historical noise</li><li>Look-ahead bias — using information that would not have been available at the time</li><li>Data quality issues in historical datasets</li><li>Regime changes — the sport or market may have changed over time</li></ul><h3>Forward Testing</h3><p>Forward testing, or paper trading, applies the model to live or upcoming matches without betting real money. You record the model''s predictions, the available market prices, and the eventual outcomes.</p><p>Advantages:</p><ul><li>Reflects actual information available at decision time</li><li>No look-ahead or survivorship bias</li><li>Tests real execution, including price availability and timing</li></ul><p>Risks:</p><ul><li>Small samples initially</li><li>Slow feedback loop</li><li>Psychological temptation to adjust the model after a few losses</li></ul><p>The best validation combines both. Backtesting gives you confidence that the model has a basis in historical reality. Forward testing confirms it works in live conditions before you risk capital.</p><h2>3. Key Validation Metrics</h2><p>To validate a model properly, you need objective metrics. Relying on “it feels like it''s working” is not validation.</p><h3>Closing Line Value (CLV)</h3><p>CLV compares the price you could have obtained to the closing line. If your model consistently identifies bets where the price you take is better than the eventual closing price, that suggests your information or analysis is arriving before the market fully adjusts.</p><p>CLV is a process metric, not a profit guarantee. But over a large sample, positive CLV is one of the strongest indicators of a genuine edge.</p><p>Measure it as:</p><p>CLV = (Your odds ÷ Closing odds) − 1</p><p>Positive CLV means you beat the close. Negative CLV means the market moved against you.</p><h3>Brier Score</h3><p>The Brier score measures the accuracy of probabilistic forecasts. For a binary outcome, it is:</p><p>Brier = (Predicted probability − Actual outcome)²</p><p>Lower is better. A perfect forecast scores 0. A forecast that always says 50% scores 0.25.</p><p>Brier scores allow you to compare your model to the market''s implied probabilities directly. If your model has a lower Brier score than the closing line over a large sample, it is genuinely adding information.</p><h3>Log Loss</h3><p>Log loss penalises confident wrong predictions more heavily than Brier. It is especially useful when you are testing whether your model''s confidence levels are justified.</p><p>Log loss = −ln(Predicted probability of the actual outcome)</p><p>Like Brier, lower is better. Compare your log loss to the market''s log loss over the same matches.</p><h3>Calibration</h3><p>Calibration measures whether your predicted probabilities match observed frequencies. If you say an event has a 60% chance, it should happen approximately 60% of the time.</p><p>To assess calibration:</p><ul><li>Bucket your predictions into ranges (50–55%, 55–60%, etc.)</li><li>For each bucket, calculate the observed outcome frequency</li><li>Plot predicted vs. actual</li></ul><p>A well-calibrated model follows the diagonal. Systematic deviations mean your probabilities are biased — too confident or not confident enough.</p><h3>ROI and Yield</h3><p>Return on investment (ROI) measures profit relative to turnover. It is the ultimate test of whether your model creates betting value.</p><p>ROI = (Profit ÷ Total staked) × 100</p><p>A positive ROI over a large sample with low variance and consistent methodology is the gold standard. However, ROI alone can be misleading in small samples. A few lucky wins can create impressive-looking numbers that disappear with more data.</p><p>Always pair ROI with sample size and statistical significance.</p><h2>4. Statistical Significance</h2><p>Random variance is real. A model with no edge can produce positive results over hundreds of bets simply by luck. You must test whether your results could plausibly arise from chance.</p><p>Key concepts:</p><ul><li><strong>Sample size</strong> — more bets mean more reliable conclusions. A few hundred bets is usually not enough.</li><li><strong>Confidence intervals</strong> — express the range around your observed ROI or CLV. Wide intervals mean high uncertainty.</li><li><strong>P-value</strong> — the probability of observing your results if there is no true edge. Low p-values (typically below 0.05) suggest the result is unlikely to be pure noise.</li><li><strong>Standard error</strong> — measures the precision of your estimated edge. Smaller samples mean larger standard errors.</li></ul><p>Be sceptical of small-sample results — including your own. The most common error in betting analysis is declaring victory too early.</p><h2>5. Overfitting and Data Snooping</h2><p>Backtesting can produce impressive results that mean nothing. If you test hundreds of variations and keep the one that performed best historically, you are likely fitting noise.</p><p>Warning signs:</p><ul><li>Performance collapses when parameters are changed slightly</li><li>Exceptional backtest results that seem too good to be true</li><li>Performance concentrated in specific seasons or small subsets</li><li>Many parameters relative to the number of matches</li><li>Repeated tweaking until the backtest looks good</li></ul><p>To protect against overfitting:</p><ul><li>Use simple, robust models with few parameters</li><li>Split data into training and test sets</li><li>Run out-of-sample validation</li><li>Pre-register hypotheses before testing</li><li>Be wary of any model that only works on historical data but not live</li></ul><h2>6. Validating Against Different Market Types</h2><p>Your model may perform differently against different books and market segments.</p><ul><li><strong>Sharp books</strong> — beating Pinnacle closing lines consistently is difficult and meaningful</li><li><strong>Soft books</strong> — softer prices may offer easier edges but lower limits and quicker account restrictions</li><li><strong>Exchanges</strong> — validation must account for commission and market depth</li><li><strong>Early vs. closing lines</strong> — does your edge exist at opening, at closing, or both?</li><li><strong>Major leagues vs. minor leagues</strong> — lower-liquidity markets may be less efficient but have higher variance</li></ul><p>Understand where your edge lives. A model that beats soft books but loses to sharp closing lines may still be valuable — but that is a different strategy than one that beats the sharp market itself.</p><h2>7. Building a Validation Workflow</h2><p>A disciplined validation process looks like this:</p><ol><li><strong>Define the model and its parameters</strong> — write it down, make it reproducible</li><li><strong>Collect historical data</strong> — results, odds, and contextual information</li><li><strong>Run backtests</strong> — generate probabilities and simulate bets</li><li><strong>Compare to market benchmarks</strong> — CLV, Brier, log loss against closing lines</li><li><strong>Test calibration</strong> — does predicted probability match observed frequency?</li><li><strong>Run forward tests</strong> — paper trade for a defined period</li><li><strong>Measure results with significance</strong> — confidence intervals, sample sizes, ROI</li><li><strong>Iterate carefully</strong> — make one change at a time and retest</li><li><strong>Only bet when validation supports it</strong> — no edge, no money</li></ol><p>The process is repetitive and often frustrating. Most models fail validation. That is the honest reality. But the models that survive are worth betting because they have demonstrated something real.</p><h2>Summary</h2><p>Validation is not a one-time check. It is a continuous process of comparing your model to the market and to reality.</p><p>The key components:</p><ul><li><strong>Use the market as your benchmark</strong> — closing lines are the default probability estimate</li><li><strong>Backtest and forward test</strong> — history tells you if it could work; live testing tells you if it does</li><li><strong>Track CLV, Brier score, log loss, calibration, and ROI</strong> — objective metrics over subjective feelings</li><li><strong>Respect variance</strong> — small samples prove nothing</li><li><strong>Guard against overfitting</strong> — simple models survive; over-tuned models collapse</li><li><strong>Know where your edge lives</strong> — market type, timing, and league matter</li></ul><p>A model that passes validation is a tool you can trust. A model that fails is a lesson in humility. Either way, validation is what separates a serious betting operation from a hope-based hobby.</p><p>The market is smart. Your job is to be smarter — and to prove it, not assume it.</p></h2>

<h2>Real-Data Benchmark: Bookmaker Overround &amp; Closing Line Efficiency</h2>
<p>In PunterStat''s historical odds dataset (<strong>157,752 Pinnacle closing lines</strong> across 39 football leagues), market margin varies sharply between sharp exchanges/bookmakers and soft retail sportsbooks:</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Bookmaker / Market Tier</th><th>Typical 1X2 Overround</th><th>Fair 50/50 Price Offered</th><th>Long-Run Drag on ₦100,000 Staked</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Pinnacle / Betting Exchange (Sharp)</td><td>2.0% – 2.8%</td><td>1.95 – 1.96</td><td>&minus;₦2,200 to &minus;₦2,600</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Tier-1 European / Global Retail</td><td>4.5% – 6.0%</td><td>1.88 – 1.91</td><td>&minus;₦4,800 to &minus;₦5,800</td></tr>
    <tr><td>High-Margin Retail / Multi-Market Props</td><td>7.0% – 11.5%</td><td>1.80 – 1.85</td><td>&minus;₦7,000 to &minus;₦10,500</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Worked Example:</strong> On a Premier League 1X2 market priced at Home <code>2.10</code> (47.62%), Draw <code>3.40</code> (29.41%), Away <code>3.60</code> (27.78%), the summed implied probability is <strong>104.81%</strong> — a <strong>4.81% overround</strong>. Dividing each implied probability by <code>1.0481</code> reveals the fair no-vig probabilities: Home <strong>45.43%</strong> (fair odds <code>2.20</code>), Draw <strong>28.06%</strong> (<code>3.56</code>), Away <strong>26.51%</strong> (<code>3.77</code>). Every time you accept <code>2.10</code> on a true <code>2.20</code> outcome, you surrender 4.5% expected value before kick-off.</p>

<h2>Key Takeaway</h2>
<p><strong>Validating Your Model Against the Market:</strong> Always convert offered odds into de-vigged fair probability, compare across bookmakers, and measure your decisions against the sharp closing line.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'validating-model-against-market'
  AND course_id = (SELECT id FROM public.courses WHERE slug = 'building-your-own-lines');

-- [portfolio-bankroll-allocation] portfolio-health-metrics
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<h2>The Portfolio Dashboard</h2>
<p>A portfolio-level bankroll requires portfolio-level monitoring. Individual strategy tracking is necessary but not sufficient — you need a holistic view of the entire operation.</p>
<h2>Key Portfolio Health Metrics</h2>
<p><strong>1. Total portfolio ROI (rolling 90 days)</strong> The primary performance metric. Compare to your long-run target and to the previous quarter.</p>
<p><strong>2. Portfolio Sharpe ratio (rolling 90 days)</strong> ROI / standard deviation of weekly P&L. Measures risk-adjusted performance. Should exceed 1.0 for a well-managed operation.</p>
<p><strong>3. Weighted average CLV across all strategies</strong> The most forward-looking indicator. Negative CLV in aggregate is a warning signal regardless of current P&L.</p>
<p><strong>4. Strategy-level CLV decomposition</strong> Which strategies are producing positive CLV? Which are not? This identifies where to reallocate and where to cut.</p>
<p><strong>5. Correlation heatmap (quarterly)</strong> How correlated are your strategies currently? Has correlation increased (reducing diversification benefit)?</p>
<p><strong>6. Account health distribution</strong> How many accounts are fully open / stake-limited / closed? Is your capacity declining?</p>
<h2>The Early Warning System</h2>
<p>Set thresholds for each metric that trigger automatic review:</p>
<ul>
<li>Portfolio ROI below target for 3 consecutive months → strategy review</li>
<li>Any individual strategy CLV negative for 60 consecutive days → allocation reduction</li>
<li>Total active accounts below 6 → emergency account opening priority</li>
<li>Portfolio Sharpe below 0.5 → risk review</li>
</ul>
<h2>Automated Monitoring</h2>
<p>A Google Sheets dashboard with formulas that automatically calculate all portfolio metrics from the bet log is achievable in a few hours of setup. The investment repays itself immediately in clarity and early warning capability.</p>

<h2>Real-Data Simulation: Comparing Staking Systems on a ₦100,000 Bankroll</h2>
<p>Assume a skilled bettor has a genuine <strong>+5% ROI edge</strong> betting at <code>2.00</code> average odds (52.5% true win rate) over 500 bets. How does staking strategy affect growth and Risk of Ruin?</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Staking Method</th><th>Stake Size on ₦100,000</th><th>Expected Bankroll (500 Bets)</th><th>Max Drawdown Risk</th><th>Risk of Ruin (&gt;80% Loss)</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Flat Staking (1% unit)</td><td>₦1,000 / bet</td><td>~₦125,000</td><td>Low (~12–18%)</td><td>&lt; 0.1%</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Quarter-Kelly (0.25&times; Kelly)</td><td>₦1,250 (scales dynamically)</td><td>~₦133,000</td><td>Moderate (~15–22%)</td><td>&lt; 0.1%</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Full Kelly (1.0&times; Kelly)</td><td>₦5,000 (5% on 5% edge)</td><td>~₦185,000 (median)</td><td>Severe (~45–60%)</td><td>High if edge overestimated</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Martingale (Double after loss)</td><td>₦1k &rarr; ₦2k &rarr; ₦4k &rarr; ₦128k</td><td>Eventual Blowup</td><td><strong>100% wipeout on 7-loss run</strong></td><td><strong>&gt; 75% within 500 bets</strong></td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Practical Rule:</strong> Even with a 52.5% win rate, a <strong>7-bet losing streak</strong> has a ~65% probability of occurring at least once in 500 bets. Flat 1–2% units or Quarter-Kelly survive those inevitable downswings cleanly; Martingale and stake-chasing guarantee ruin.</p>

<h2>Key Takeaway</h2>
<p><strong>Monitoring Portfolio Health Metrics:</strong> Protect your capital first: consistent unit sizing (1–2% flat or fractional Kelly) ensures inevitable losing streaks never end your betting operation.</p>'
