<p><strong>Worked +EV Example in Naira:</strong> Suppose your de-vigged model prices an Over 2.5 Goals selection at <strong>52.0% true probability</strong> (fair odds <code>1.92</code>), and a bookmaker offers <code>2.10</code>. On a <strong>₦5,000 stake</strong>: <code>EV = (0.52 &times; ₦5,500) &minus; (0.48 &times; ₦5,000) = ₦2,860 &minus; ₦2,400 = +₦460</code> (<strong>+9.2% ROI</strong>). Over 1 bet, you still lose 48% of the time; over 500 similar +EV bets, expected profit compounds to <strong>+₦230,000</strong>.</p>

<h2>Key Takeaway</h2>
<p>Market efficiency is heavily dependent on timing. Bettors exploit temporal inefficiencies by attacking low-limit opening lines with proprietary models before sharp syndicates correct the price. As kickoff approaches, bettors secure value by fading the late flood of unsophisticated public money. Finally, by utilizing live xG metrics, bettors can exploit the lag in automated in-play algorithms, capitalizing on shifting tactical momentum before the bookmaker adjusts.</p>'
