-- [bankroll-fundamentals] bankroll-growth-compounding
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<h1>Bankroll Growth: Compounding and Scaling</h1><h2>Introduction</h2><p>Bankroll growth in sports betting is not primarily about winning more bets. It is about protecting capital, applying a consistent edge over a large sample, and letting positive expectancy compound. Scaling (increasing unit size as the bankroll grows) accelerates this process when done correctly and destroys it when done emotionally or prematurely.</p><p>The goal is controlled, sustainable growth rather than rapid swings.</p><hr><h2>The Power of Compounding</h2><p>Compounding works the same way in betting as it does in investing: returns are earned on an ever-larger base.</p><ul><li>A flat-staking bettor who maintains a 4% ROI on a fixed $100 unit size grows slowly and linearly.</li><li>The same edge applied with unit size that scales with the bankroll produces exponential growth over time.</li></ul><p><strong>Example (simplified, ignoring variance):</strong></p><ul><li>Starting bankroll: $5,000</li><li>Unit size: 1% = $50</li><li>Average ROI: 4%</li><li>After 500 bets at constant unit size → roughly +$1,000 (20% growth)</li><li>Same edge with unit size reset periodically to 1% of current bankroll → significantly higher ending bankroll because later bets are larger.</li></ul><p>The difference becomes dramatic over thousands of bets. Compounding rewards discipline and longevity far more than short-term winning streaks.</p><hr><h2>Unit-Based Staking as the Foundation</h2><p>All effective growth systems rest on unit-based staking rather than fixed-dollar bets.</p><ul><li>Define 1 unit as a fixed percentage of the <em>current</em> bankroll (commonly 0.5–2%, most often 1%).</li><li>Recalculate the unit size only at predetermined intervals (e.g., every 50–100 bets, monthly, or after a defined percentage change in bankroll).</li><li>Never adjust unit size mid-session or after a single big win/loss.</li></ul><p>This approach automatically compounds gains and reduces exposure during drawdowns.</p><hr><h2>When and How to Scale</h2><p>Scaling should be systematic, not discretionary.</p><p><strong>Conservative scaling (recommended for most)</strong></p><ul><li>Increase unit size only after the bankroll has grown by a set percentage (e.g., +20% or +25%).</li><li>Or reset unit size on a fixed schedule (monthly or after every 100 bets) based on the new bankroll.</li><li>Keep the percentage risk per bet constant.</li></ul><p><strong>Aggressive scaling</strong></p><ul><li>Increase unit size more frequently or by larger increments.</li><li>Higher growth potential but also higher risk of large drawdowns that force a reset to a smaller base.</li></ul><p><strong>Practical rules of thumb</strong></p><ul><li>Never risk more than 1–2% of the <em>current</em> bankroll on a single bet (straight bets).</li><li>For parlays or higher-variance bets, reduce the percentage further.</li><li>After a significant drawdown (e.g., –20% or more), consider temporarily reducing the unit percentage until the bankroll recovers.</li></ul><hr><h2>The Role of Edge and Volume</h2><p>Compounding only works if you have a genuine positive edge. Without it, scaling simply accelerates losses.</p><p><strong>Key requirements for sustainable growth:</strong></p><ul><li>Positive expected value (ideally confirmed by consistent closing-line value).</li><li>Sufficient volume to let the edge express itself (hundreds of bets, not dozens).</li><li>Strict adherence to staking rules even during losing streaks.</li></ul><p>A 3–5% ROI compounded over a large sample at proper unit size produces excellent long-term results. Chasing higher short-term returns by oversizing units usually ends in large drawdowns.</p><hr><h2>Managing Drawdowns While Scaling</h2><p>Drawdowns are inevitable. How you handle them determines whether compounding continues.</p><ul><li>Expect 20–30%+ drawdowns even with a solid edge.</li><li>Pre-define the maximum drawdown you will tolerate before reducing unit size or pausing.</li><li>Avoid the common mistake of increasing stakes to "win it back." That is the opposite of scaling.</li><li>Keep detailed records so you can distinguish normal variance from a broken process.</li></ul><hr><h2>Practical Implementation Tips</h2><ol><li>Start with a clearly defined bankroll that is completely separate from living expenses.</li><li>Choose a conservative unit percentage (1% is a strong default for most recreational-to-serious bettors).</li><li>Recalculate unit size on a fixed schedule rather than after every bet.</li><li>Track both nominal bankroll and unit bankroll so growth is visible in both dollars and units.</li><li>Review performance monthly: ROI, CLV, and maximum drawdown matter more than recent results.</li><li>If you move up in unit size, treat the new level as permanent until the next scheduled review—do not drop back after a few losses.</li></ol><hr><h2>Common Scaling Mistakes</h2><ul><li>Increasing unit size after a hot streak instead of on a schedule.</li><li>Risking a fixed dollar amount that becomes too large a percentage of a shrinking bankroll.</li><li>Scaling up without confirming the edge still exists.</li><li>Ignoring the emotional impact of larger absolute swings even when the percentage risk stays the same.</li><li>Failing to reduce exposure after a meaningful drawdown.</li></ul><hr><h2>Real-Data Simulation: Comparing Staking Systems on a ₦100,000 Bankroll</h2>
<p>Assume a skilled bettor has a genuine <strong>+5% ROI edge</strong> betting at <code>2.00</code> average odds (52.5% true win rate) over 500 bets. How does staking strategy affect growth and Risk of Ruin?</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Staking Method</th><th>Stake Size on ₦100,000</th><th>Expected Bankroll (500 Bets)</th><th>Max Drawdown Risk</th><th>Risk of Ruin (&gt;80% Loss)</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Flat Staking (1% unit)</td><td>₦1,000 / bet</td><td>~₦125,000</td><td>Low (~12–18%)</td><td>&lt; 0.1%</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Quarter-Kelly (0.25&times; Kelly)</td><td>₦1,250 (scales dynamically)</td><td>~₦133,000</td><td>Moderate (~15–22%)</td><td>&lt; 0.1%</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Full Kelly (1.0&times; Kelly)</td><td>₦5,000 (5% on 5% edge)</td><td>~₦185,000 (median)</td><td>Severe (~45–60%)</td><td>High if edge overestimated</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Martingale (Double after loss)</td><td>₦1k &rarr; ₦2k &rarr; ₦4k &rarr; ₦128k</td><td>Eventual Blowup</td><td><strong>100% wipeout on 7-loss run</strong></td><td><strong>&gt; 75% within 500 bets</strong></td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Practical Rule:</strong> Even with a 52.5% win rate, a <strong>7-bet losing streak</strong> has a ~65% probability of occurring at least once in 500 bets. Flat 1–2% units or Quarter-Kelly survive those inevitable downswings cleanly; Martingale and stake-chasing guarantee ruin.</p>

<h2>Key Takeaway</h2><p>Sustainable bankroll growth comes from three elements working together: a real edge, disciplined unit-based staking, and systematic scaling that lets compounding do the heavy lifting. The bettors who grow their bankrolls over years are rarely the ones who take the biggest swings; they are the ones who protect the base, let winners compound, and refuse to abandon the process during inevitable losing periods.</p><p>Scale only what you can emotionally and financially tolerate at the new level. The math of compounding is powerful, but only if you stay in the game long enough for it to work.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'bankroll-growth-compounding'
  AND course_id = (SELECT id FROM public.courses WHERE slug = 'bankroll-fundamentals');

-- [staking-strategies] dangers-martingale-chasing
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<h2>The Martingale Explained</h2>
<p>The Martingale system doubles your stake after every loss. The idea: eventually you win, and the win covers all previous losses plus a small profit.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> Bet 1 unit. Lose. Bet 2 units. Lose. Bet 4. Lose. Bet 8. Win. Net result: +1 unit after 4 bets.</p>
<p>This sounds appealing. It fails catastrophically.</p>
<h2>Why Martingale Destroys Bankrolls</h2>
<p><strong>The sequence risk:</strong> A losing run of 10 bets requires a stake of 2^10 = 1,024 units on the 11th bet. Starting from 1 unit, a 10-loss run requires staking 1,023 total units to reach this point. At a ₦10 unit: you have staked ₦10,230 to win back ₦10.</p>
<p><strong>Losing runs are normal:</strong> Even with a 50% win rate, a 10-loss run has P = (0.5)^10 = 0.1% probability. At 1,000 bets, expected occurrences: 1. At 10,000 bets: 10 occurrences.</p>
<p><strong>No edge requirement:</strong> Martingale does not address whether your selections have positive EV. It is purely a staking system that assumes infinite bankroll and no stake limits.</p>
<h2>The Fibonacci and D''Alembert Fallacies</h2>
<p>Both are variations of progression betting — increasing stakes after losses. All progression systems share the same fatal flaw: they cannot convert negative EV selections into positive EV outcomes. They just change the loss distribution (many small wins, occasional catastrophic loss).</p>
<h2>The Casino''s Perspective</h2>
<p>Casinos welcome Martingale players. The system is emotionally compelling but mathematically equivalent to flat betting with the same number of units staked in expectation — minus the occasional ruin event.</p>
<h2>The Rule</h2>
<p>Never use any staking system that increases stakes after losses without a mathematical basis (like Kelly) connecting the stake increase to a genuine edge calculation.</p>

<h2>Real-Data Simulation: Comparing Staking Systems on a ₦100,000 Bankroll</h2>
<p>Assume a skilled bettor has a genuine <strong>+5% ROI edge</strong> betting at <code>2.00</code> average odds (52.5% true win rate) over 500 bets. How does staking strategy affect growth and Risk of Ruin?</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Staking Method</th><th>Stake Size on ₦100,000</th><th>Expected Bankroll (500 Bets)</th><th>Max Drawdown Risk</th><th>Risk of Ruin (&gt;80% Loss)</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Flat Staking (1% unit)</td><td>₦1,000 / bet</td><td>~₦125,000</td><td>Low (~12–18%)</td><td>&lt; 0.1%</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Quarter-Kelly (0.25&times; Kelly)</td><td>₦1,250 (scales dynamically)</td><td>~₦133,000</td><td>Moderate (~15–22%)</td><td>&lt; 0.1%</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Full Kelly (1.0&times; Kelly)</td><td>₦5,000 (5% on 5% edge)</td><td>~₦185,000 (median)</td><td>Severe (~45–60%)</td><td>High if edge overestimated</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Martingale (Double after loss)</td><td>₦1k &rarr; ₦2k &rarr; ₦4k &rarr; ₦128k</td><td>Eventual Blowup</td><td><strong>100% wipeout on 7-loss run</strong></td><td><strong>&gt; 75% within 500 bets</strong></td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Practical Rule:</strong> Even with a 52.5% win rate, a <strong>7-bet losing streak</strong> has a ~65% probability of occurring at least once in 500 bets. Flat 1–2% units or Quarter-Kelly survive those inevitable downswings cleanly; Martingale and stake-chasing guarantee ruin.</p>

<h2>Key Takeaway</h2>
<p><strong>The Dangers of Martingale and Chasing Systems:</strong> Protect your capital first: consistent unit sizing (1–2% flat or fractional Kelly) ensures inevitable losing streaks never end your betting operation.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'dangers-martingale-chasing'
  AND course_id = (SELECT id FROM public.courses WHERE slug = 'staking-strategies');

-- [risk-management-ruin-theory] ruin-recovery-rebuilding
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<h2>When the Worst Happens</h2>
<p>Despite best practices, catastrophic losses occur. A catastrophic loss is a drawdown large enough to require fundamental reassessment — typically 50%+ of the original bankroll.</p>
<h2>Causes of Catastrophic Loss</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Edge failure:</strong> The edge was less real than estimated, and the loss ran longer than expected before the stop-loss triggered</li>
<li><strong>Discipline failure:</strong> Stop-loss rules were violated, leading to revenge betting and accelerating losses</li>
<li><strong>External event:</strong> A sudden change in market access, rule change, or personal circumstance forced premature liquidation</li>
</ol>
<h2>The Post-Catastrophe Assessment</h2>
<p>Before rebuilding, conduct an honest post-mortem:</p>
<ul>
<li>Was the loss within the modelled variance range? (If yes: variance, not failure. Rebuild.)</li>
<li>Was there a genuine model or process error? (Identify and fix before rebuilding.)</li>
<li>Were stop-loss rules violated? (Address the psychological discipline issue first.)</li>
</ul>
<p>Rebuilding before fixing the root cause will produce the same result.</p>
<h2>The Conservative Rebuild Protocol</h2>
<p>Start fresh with a smaller bankroll and conservative staking:</p>
<ul>
<li>New bankroll: 30–50% of original peak (from savings, not emergency funds)</li>
<li>Staking: flat 0.75% per bet for the first 3 months (lower than normal)</li>
<li>Bet selection: only your most validated market type (your best-performing historical market)</li>
<li>No variable staking until new 200-bet CLV shows positive results</li>
</ul>
<h2>The Psychological Challenge</h2>
<p>Rebuilding after catastrophic loss requires confronting the exact situations that caused the loss — and responding differently. This is one of the most psychologically demanding challenges in betting. External support (accountability partner, peer group) is often necessary.</p>

<h2>Real-Data Simulation: Comparing Staking Systems on a ₦100,000 Bankroll</h2>
<p>Assume a skilled bettor has a genuine <strong>+5% ROI edge</strong> betting at <code>2.00</code> average odds (52.5% true win rate) over 500 bets. How does staking strategy affect growth and Risk of Ruin?</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Staking Method</th><th>Stake Size on ₦100,000</th><th>Expected Bankroll (500 Bets)</th><th>Max Drawdown Risk</th><th>Risk of Ruin (&gt;80% Loss)</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Flat Staking (1% unit)</td><td>₦1,000 / bet</td><td>~₦125,000</td><td>Low (~12–18%)</td><td>&lt; 0.1%</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Quarter-Kelly (0.25&times; Kelly)</td><td>₦1,250 (scales dynamically)</td><td>~₦133,000</td><td>Moderate (~15–22%)</td><td>&lt; 0.1%</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Full Kelly (1.0&times; Kelly)</td><td>₦5,000 (5% on 5% edge)</td><td>~₦185,000 (median)</td><td>Severe (~45–60%)</td><td>High if edge overestimated</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Martingale (Double after loss)</td><td>₦1k &rarr; ₦2k &rarr; ₦4k &rarr; ₦128k</td><td>Eventual Blowup</td><td><strong>100% wipeout on 7-loss run</strong></td><td><strong>&gt; 75% within 500 bets</strong></td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Practical Rule:</strong> Even with a 52.5% win rate, a <strong>7-bet losing streak</strong> has a ~65% probability of occurring at least once in 500 bets. Flat 1–2% units or Quarter-Kelly survive those inevitable downswings cleanly; Martingale and stake-chasing guarantee ruin.</p>

<h2>Key Takeaway</h2>
<p><strong>Ruin Recovery: Rebuilding After Catastrophic Loss:</strong> Protect your capital first: consistent unit sizing (1–2% flat or fractional Kelly) ensures inevitable losing streaks never end your betting operation.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'ruin-recovery-rebuilding'
  AND course_id = (SELECT id FROM public.courses WHERE slug = 'risk-management-ruin-theory');

-- [psychology-of-stake-sizing] psychology-big-winners-downswings
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<h2>The Post-Win Problem</h2>
<p>A large winning bet — particularly at long odds — creates a psychological distortion almost as damaging as a large loss. The euphoria of a big win leads to:</p>
<ul>
<li>Overconfidence in future selections ("I have a good feel right now")</li>
<li>Inflated stakes on subsequent bets ("I can afford to bet more because I just won")</li>
<li>Reduced analytical rigor ("I don''t need to analyse as carefully — I''m running hot")</li>
</ul>
<p>All three responses are irrational and empirically harmful. A winning bet tells you nothing about your edge on the next bet.</p>
<h2>Implementing a Post-Win Protocol</h2>
<p>After any single bet win > 5× your standard unit:</p>
<ol>
<li>Do not place another bet for 24 hours</li>
<li>Review your next 5 planned bets with extra scrutiny (challenge every assumption)</li>
<li>Confirm stakes are at normal level, not inflated</li>
</ol>
<p>This protocol is uncomfortable. It feels like leaving money on the table. It also prevents euphoria-driven over-staking that often follows big wins.</p>
<h2>The Downswing Identity Threat</h2>
<p>Extended losing runs threaten self-concept. A bettor who defines themselves as "good at this" finds a downswing cognitively threatening — it suggests they may not be as good as they thought. The response to an identity threat is often defensiveness: minimising the significance of the loss, attributing it entirely to bad luck, or doubling down to prove the approach works.</p>
<h2>The Identity Shift</h2>
<p>Replace "I am a good bettor" with "I run a good process." Process identity is resilient to losing runs — the process is intact even when results are negative. When results are negative, a process-oriented bettor investigates the process, not the self.</p>
<h2>The Equanimity Target</h2>
<p>The mental state that produces the best long-run staking decisions is equanimity — calm acceptance of both wins and losses as variance around the mean, without emotional significance attached to either.</p>

<h2>Real-Data Simulation: Comparing Staking Systems on a ₦100,000 Bankroll</h2>
<p>Assume a skilled bettor has a genuine <strong>+5% ROI edge</strong> betting at <code>2.00</code> average odds (52.5% true win rate) over 500 bets. How does staking strategy affect growth and Risk of Ruin?</p>
