-- [odds-comparison-line-shopping] arbitrage-between-books
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<h2><h1>Arbitrage: Risk-Free Profit Between Books</h1><h2>Definition</h2><p>Arbitrage (or "arb") in sports betting is the act of placing bets on all possible outcomes of an event across different sportsbooks so that a profit is guaranteed regardless of the result. It exploits temporary price discrepancies between books.</p><p>When the combined implied probabilities of the best available odds across books fall below 100%, an arbitrage opportunity exists.</p><h2>How It Works – Simple Two-Way Example</h2><p>Suppose two books offer the following decimal odds on the same match:</p><ul><li>Book A: Team X @ 2.10</li><li>Book B: Team Y @ 2.10</li></ul><p>Implied probabilities:</p><ul><li>Team X: 1 / 2.10 ≈ 47.62%</li><li>Team Y: 1 / 2.10 ≈ 47.62%</li><li>Total = 95.24%</li></ul><p>Because the total is under 100%, an arbitrage exists. The margin is negative from the books'' perspective (–4.76%).</p><p><strong>Stake calculation</strong> (to guarantee equal profit):</p><p>Decide total amount to risk, e.g. $100.</p><ul><li>Stake on Team X = (Total stake × Implied probability of Team X) / Sum of implied probabilities</li><li>Or more cleanly: Stake on outcome = (Total stake) / (Decimal odds of that outcome × Sum of all 1/odds)</li></ul><p>Practical formula for two outcomes:</p><ul><li>Stake A = Total stake × (Odds B) / (Odds A + Odds B)</li><li>Stake B = Total stake × (Odds A) / (Odds A + Odds B)</li></ul><p>Using $100 total:</p><ul><li>Stake on Team X (2.10) = 100 × 2.10 / (2.10 + 2.10) = $50</li><li>Stake on Team Y (2.10) = $50</li></ul><p>Profit either way:</p><ul><li>If Team X wins: $50 × 2.10 = $105 → profit $5</li><li>If Team Y wins: same → profit $5</li></ul><p>Return = 5% risk-free on the capital used for this arb.</p><h2>Step-by-Step Process to Find and Execute an Arb</h2><ol><li>Identify matching markets across books (same event, same rules).</li><li>Take the best odds for each outcome from different books.</li><li>Convert to implied probabilities and sum them.</li><li>If the sum is less than 1 (100%), an arb exists.</li><li>Calculate the exact stakes so that the return is identical no matter which outcome occurs.</li><li>Place the bets as close to simultaneously as possible.</li><li>Record the bets and expected profit.</li></ol><h2>Three-Way and Multi-Outcome Arbs</h2><p>The same logic applies. Sum the best implied probabilities of every mutually exclusive outcome. If the total is under 100%, scale the stakes proportionally so each potential winning bet returns the same amount.</p><h2>Practical Limitations and Risks</h2><p>Arbitrage is not truly risk-free in the real world:</p><ul><li><strong>Execution risk</strong> — Odds can move or disappear between the moment you calculate and the moment both bets are accepted.</li><li><strong>Account limitations</strong> — Books quickly limit or close accounts that show consistent arbitrage patterns.</li><li><strong>Stake restrictions</strong> — Maximum bet sizes may prevent you from placing the full calculated stake.</li><li><strong>Rule differences</strong> — Different settlement rules, void policies, or scoring methods can turn a theoretical arb into a loss.</li><li><strong>Deposit/withdrawal friction and fees</strong> — Moving money between books has costs and delays.</li><li><strong>Timing and liquidity</strong> — Many arbs are small, short-lived, and require fast execution.</li><li><strong>Capital efficiency</strong> — Money is locked in multiple books; the return is usually modest (1–5% per arb) and capital is tied up until settlement.</li></ul><p>Because of these factors, pure arbitrage is more difficult and less scalable than it appears on paper, especially at meaningful stakes.</p><h2>Arbitrage vs. Value Betting</h2><ul><li>Arbitrage seeks guaranteed profit from price discrepancies between books (no opinion on who will win required).</li><li>Value betting seeks positive expected value based on your own probability assessment versus the book''s price (requires an edge in prediction or information).</li></ul><p>Most long-term profitable bettors focus on value betting and use line shopping; pure arbitrage is usually a secondary or short-term activity.</p><h2>Tools and Practical Tips</h2><ul><li>Odds-comparison scanners and arb-finder software can surface opportunities quickly.</li><li>Maintain balances at multiple sharp and soft books so you can act without delay.</li><li>Start with small stakes while learning execution and book behavior.</li><li>Track every arb carefully, including any voided or adjusted bets.</li><li>Be aware that heavy arbitrage activity almost always leads to limited accounts.</li></ul><h2>Real-Data Benchmark: Bookmaker Overround &amp; Closing Line Efficiency</h2>
<p>In PunterStat''s historical odds dataset (<strong>157,752 Pinnacle closing lines</strong> across 39 football leagues), market margin varies sharply between sharp exchanges/bookmakers and soft retail sportsbooks:</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Bookmaker / Market Tier</th><th>Typical 1X2 Overround</th><th>Fair 50/50 Price Offered</th><th>Long-Run Drag on ₦100,000 Staked</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Pinnacle / Betting Exchange (Sharp)</td><td>2.0% – 2.8%</td><td>1.95 – 1.96</td><td>&minus;₦2,200 to &minus;₦2,600</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Tier-1 European / Global Retail</td><td>4.5% – 6.0%</td><td>1.88 – 1.91</td><td>&minus;₦4,800 to &minus;₦5,800</td></tr>
    <tr><td>High-Margin Retail / Multi-Market Props</td><td>7.0% – 11.5%</td><td>1.80 – 1.85</td><td>&minus;₦7,000 to &minus;₦10,500</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Worked Example:</strong> On a Premier League 1X2 market priced at Home <code>2.10</code> (47.62%), Draw <code>3.40</code> (29.41%), Away <code>3.60</code> (27.78%), the summed implied probability is <strong>104.81%</strong> — a <strong>4.81% overround</strong>. Dividing each implied probability by <code>1.0481</code> reveals the fair no-vig probabilities: Home <strong>45.43%</strong> (fair odds <code>2.20</code>), Draw <strong>28.06%</strong> (<code>3.56</code>), Away <strong>26.51%</strong> (<code>3.77</code>). Every time you accept <code>2.10</code> on a true <code>2.20</code> outcome, you surrender 4.5% expected value before kick-off.</p>

<h2>Key Takeaway</h2><p>Arbitrage is the mathematical exploitation of mismatched odds across books that produces a guaranteed profit when executed perfectly. The calculation is straightforward: when the best available implied probabilities sum to less than 100%, stakes can be balanced for a risk-free return.</p><p>In practice, execution risk, account limitations, and capital constraints make pure arbitrage harder and less scalable than the theory suggests. It remains a useful concept to understand, an occasional source of small locked-in profits, and a clear illustration of why maintaining multiple accounts and constantly comparing prices is valuable. For most bettors, disciplined line shopping and value betting deliver more sustainable results than chasing arbs.</p></h2>'
WHERE slug = 'arbitrage-between-books'
  AND course_id = (SELECT id FROM public.courses WHERE slug = 'odds-comparison-line-shopping');

-- [live-and-in-play-odds] live-betting-strategies-that-work
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<h2><h1>Live Betting Strategies That Work</h1><p>Most live betting is –EV. Higher margins, faster efficient pricing, latency disadvantages, and emotional decision-making under time pressure ensure that simply "watching and reacting" loses money over time. The strategies that actually produce positive expected value are narrow, selective, and process-driven. They exploit temporary gaps rather than trying to outguess continuous market movement.</p><p>Here are the approaches with the strongest structural support.</p><h2>1. Exploit Overreactions to Early or Extreme Events</h2><p>Public money and some algorithms overweight recent scoring or momentum. True win probability often regresses.</p><ul><li><strong>How it works</strong>: A pre-match favorite falls behind early (e.g., 0-1 in soccer, down 8–12 in the first quarter of an NBA game, or trailing after the opening drive in the NFL). Live prices frequently overcorrect. Betting the side that still has the better underlying process at the discounted live number can be +EV.</li><li><strong>Best environments</strong>: Soccer (especially after early goals by underdogs), NBA (early scoring runs driven by shooting variance), NFL (early scores that move the live spread more than historical recovery rates justify).</li><li><strong>Key filter</strong>: Only act when the live price implies a larger probability shift than historical data or your pre-match model supports. Pass on pure "momentum" without a clear mispricing.</li><li><strong>Execution note</strong>: Act in the first window after the market reopens; the edge decays quickly as sharper money arrives.</li></ul><h2>2. Soft-Book / Latency Lag</h2><p>Not all books update at the same speed. Sharp books and exchanges move within seconds of meaningful events; many retail books lag.</p><ul><li><strong>How it works</strong>: Monitor multiple books (or use an odds screen). When a sharp reference moves and a slower book has not yet adjusted, take the stale number. This is one of the most reliable, repeatable live edges for those with the infrastructure.</li><li><strong>Best environments</strong>: Lower- and mid-tier soccer, secondary tennis events, niche props, and any market where algorithmic attention is thinner.</li><li><strong>Requirements</strong>: Pre-funded accounts across several books, fast comparison tools or a multi-screen layout, and pre-defined minimum edge thresholds. Accept that many opportunities vanish before you can click.</li><li><strong>Limitation</strong>: On major markets of top leagues the window is often measured in low single-digit seconds.</li></ul><h2>3. Information and Observation Advantages</h2><p>Visual or contextual information that has not yet been fully incorporated into the price.</p><ul><li><strong>Examples</strong>:<ul><li>A key player is visibly limited (limping, holding an injury) before the market fully adjusts.</li><li>Sustained territorial dominance or xG accumulation in soccer without a goal.</li><li>Tactical substitutions or shape changes whose impact is clear on the pitch but not yet reflected in the odds.</li><li>Weather, pitch, or crowd conditions that alter expected scoring rates.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Best environments</strong>: Lower-league or less-covered competitions where data feeds and trader attention are slower; sports with high visual information content (soccer, tennis, basketball rotations/foul trouble).</li><li><strong>Critical caveat</strong>: Broadcast delay works against you if your stream lags the data feed the books use. The edge only exists when your information is genuinely ahead.</li></ul><h2>4. Residual Pre-Match Value in Early Live Markets</h2><p>Occasionally a strong pre-match opinion remains under-priced in the opening minutes of live trading before full convergence.</p><ul><li><strong>How it works</strong>: You have a clear pre-match edge (model or research-based). Early live lines are sometimes set conservatively or have not yet absorbed all sharp money. Taking the live price if it is still better than your fair number can be worthwhile.</li><li><strong>Best environments</strong>: Matches where opening live lines are known to be cautious, or games with significant late team-news movement that has not fully settled.</li><li><strong>Filter</strong>: Only when the live number still clears your pre-match threshold by a meaningful margin.</li></ul><h2>5. Niche and Low-Liquidity Markets</h2><p>Player props, correct-score ranges, minor-league totals, certain period/quarter markets, and secondary competitions receive less algorithmic sharpening.</p><ul><li>Volatility spikes (red cards, key injuries, sudden weather shifts) can produce temporary over- or under-reactions that last longer than in mainstream full-game markets.</li><li>These require deeper sport-specific knowledge and tighter staking because of higher variance and potential limits.</li></ul><h2>What Rarely Works</h2><ul><li>Betting pure "momentum" or "they look good right now" on sharp, liquid markets.</li><li>High-frequency clicking in the hope of catching soft numbers without systematic criteria.</li><li>Chasing within the same game after an early loss.</li><li>Relying on micro-markets (next basket, next point, race-to-X) as a primary strategy—these are usually high-margin entertainment products.</li><li>Treating live betting as continuous entertainment rather than selective opportunity hunting.</li></ul><h2>Risk Management Rules That Make the Strategies Viable</h2><p>Live variance is higher and decision time is compressed. Adjust accordingly:</p><ul><li>Use smaller units than pre-match (commonly 0.25–0.75% of bankroll).</li><li>Pre-define maximum session or daily loss limits and stop when hit.</li><li>Record every live bet with the specific reason (overreaction, lag, observation, residual value). Review the live subset separately.</li><li>Never increase stake size to recover a live loss in the same game.</li><li>Accept that the large majority of potential opportunities will be passed on. Selectivity is the edge.</li></ul><h2>Practical Setup</h2><ul><li>Multiple funded accounts ready.</li><li>Fast odds comparison (screen layout or reliable tool).</li><li>Clear written criteria for what constitutes an acceptable edge before the game starts.</li><li>Separate tracking spreadsheet or software for live results by sport and strategy type.</li></ul><h2>Real-Data Benchmark: Bookmaker Overround &amp; Closing Line Efficiency</h2>
<p>In PunterStat''s historical odds dataset (<strong>157,752 Pinnacle closing lines</strong> across 39 football leagues), market margin varies sharply between sharp exchanges/bookmakers and soft retail sportsbooks:</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Bookmaker / Market Tier</th><th>Typical 1X2 Overround</th><th>Fair 50/50 Price Offered</th><th>Long-Run Drag on ₦100,000 Staked</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Pinnacle / Betting Exchange (Sharp)</td><td>2.0% – 2.8%</td><td>1.95 – 1.96</td><td>&minus;₦2,200 to &minus;₦2,600</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Tier-1 European / Global Retail</td><td>4.5% – 6.0%</td><td>1.88 – 1.91</td><td>&minus;₦4,800 to &minus;₦5,800</td></tr>
    <tr><td>High-Margin Retail / Multi-Market Props</td><td>7.0% – 11.5%</td><td>1.80 – 1.85</td><td>&minus;₦7,000 to &minus;₦10,500</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Worked Example:</strong> On a Premier League 1X2 market priced at Home <code>2.10</code> (47.62%), Draw <code>3.40</code> (29.41%), Away <code>3.60</code> (27.78%), the summed implied probability is <strong>104.81%</strong> — a <strong>4.81% overround</strong>. Dividing each implied probability by <code>1.0481</code> reveals the fair no-vig probabilities: Home <strong>45.43%</strong> (fair odds <code>2.20</code>), Draw <strong>28.06%</strong> (<code>3.56</code>), Away <strong>26.51%</strong> (<code>3.77</code>). Every time you accept <code>2.10</code> on a true <code>2.20</code> outcome, you surrender 4.5% expected value before kick-off.</p>

<h2>Key Takeaway</h2><p>The live strategies that work are not about predicting the next swing better than the market. They are about identifying brief, structurally created mispricings—overreactions, lagging books, delayed information, residual pre-match value, and thinner markets—and acting only when the price clears a pre-defined threshold.</p><p>Everything else is usually priced for the house. Treat live betting as a high-discipline, low-frequency activity. The bettors who extract value over the long run are those who pass on far more opportunities than they take.</p></h2>'
WHERE slug = 'live-betting-strategies-that-work'
  AND course_id = (SELECT id FROM public.courses WHERE slug = 'live-and-in-play-odds');

-- [exchange-betting-lay-markets] commission-optimisation-exchanges
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<h2><h1>Commission Optimisation on Exchanges</h1><p>Commission is the only guaranteed cost on a betting exchange. Unlike sportsbooks, which build margin into every price, exchanges charge a percentage of your net winnings. This commission directly reduces your profit on every successful bet or trade. Optimising commission is therefore not a minor detail — it is a core part of long-term exchange profitability.</p><h2>How Exchange Commission Works</h2><p>Exchanges charge commission only on <strong>net winnings</strong> in a market. If you lose a bet, you pay no commission. If you win, the commission is deducted from your profit, not from your stake.</p><p><strong>Formula:</strong><br>Net profit = Gross profit − (Gross profit × Commission rate)</p><p><strong>Example:</strong><br>You back a selection at 3.00 for ₦100.<br>Gross profit if it wins: ₦200.<br>Commission rate: 5%.<br>Commission paid: ₦200 × 0.05 = ₦10.<br>Net profit: ₦200 − ₦10 = ₦190.</p><p>If the bet loses, you lose ₦100 and pay zero commission.</p><h2>Typical Commission Rates</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Exchange</th><th>Base Rate</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Betfair</td><td>5%</td><td>Reducible to 2% or lower with volume; premium charges may apply to very high winners</td></tr><tr><td>Smarkets</td><td>2%</td><td>Flat rate regardless of volume or market</td></tr><tr><td>Matchbook</td><td>1.5–2%</td><td>Often runs 0% commission promotions on selected markets</td></tr><tr><td>Betdaq</td><td>1–2%</td><td>Lower base rate but generally less liquidity than Betfair</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The difference between 2% and 5% may seem small on a single bet. Over hundreds or thousands of bets, it compounds into a very significant difference in long-term returns.</p><h2>Commission Discounts on Betfair</h2><p>Betfair operates a tiered discount system. Your commission rate falls as your activity increases. The discount is based on <strong>Betfair Points</strong> earned over a rolling period.</p><h3>How Betfair Points Work</h3><p>You earn points for every matched bet on Betfair. The number of points depends on the commission you have paid and the market type.</p><ul><li>Points are earned on both winning and losing bets.</li><li>Points expire on a rolling basis — typically based on your activity over the previous 52 weeks.</li><li>More points mean a higher discount rate.</li></ul><h3>Typical Discount Tiers</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Discount Rate</th><th>Effective Commission</th><th>Points Required</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0%</td><td>5.00%</td><td>Base</td></tr><tr><td>20%</td><td>4.00%</td><td>Low</td></tr><tr><td>40%</td><td>3.00%</td><td>Moderate</td></tr><tr><td>60%</td><td>2.00%</td><td>High</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Some very high-volume users can reach the minimum 2% rate. This represents a 60% reduction in cost — a massive advantage for professional traders.</p><h2>Premium Charges</h2><p>Betfair applies an additional <strong>Premium Charge</strong> to a small minority of very successful, high-volume customers. This is a controversial policy but important to understand.</p><h3>When Premium Charge Applies</h3><p>You may be subject to Premium Charge if all of the following conditions are met:</p><ul><li>Your lifetime net profit on Betfair is above a certain threshold.</li><li>Your total commission generated is less than a set percentage of your gross profit.</li><li>You have bet in more than a minimum number of markets.</li></ul><p>The charge is typically 20% of gross profit for customers who trigger it, rising to 40% or 60% for those who consistently pay very little commission relative to winnings.</p><p>Premium Charge applies only to a small percentage of users — the most consistently profitable ones. For most bettors, the standard commission rate is the relevant cost.</p><h2>Strategies for Minimising Commission</h2><h3>1. Choose the Right Exchange</h3><p>If your primary exchange charges 5% and a competitor charges 2%, the choice of platform can cut your cost by more than half. The trade-off is liquidity. Smarkets and Matchbook have lower base rates but less depth on many markets.</p><p>For high-liquidity markets like Premier League football, the lower-commission exchanges are almost always cheaper. For smaller markets where only Betfair has meaningful liquidity, the higher commission may be worth paying.</p><h3>2. Earn Discount Rates on Betfair</h3><p>If you use Betfair regularly, aim to increase your discount tier. More matched volume leads to more points and a lower commission rate. This is particularly relevant for traders and high-volume bettors.</p><p>Even moderate activity can move you from 5% to 4% or 3%, which is a significant saving over time.</p><h3>3. Net Off Winnings Within a Market</h3><p>Exchanges calculate commission on <strong>net market winnings</strong>, not on individual bets. If you back and lay the same selection within the same market, your commission is calculated on the total net profit from that market.</p><p><strong>Example:</strong><br>Back Team A at 3.00 for ₦100.<br>Lay Team A at 2.50 for ₦100.<br>Net position: guaranteed profit of ₦50 regardless of outcome.<br>Commission is charged on ₦50, not on each bet separately.<br>This is much more efficient than placing the same two bets in separate markets.</p><h3>4. Use Commission-Free Promotions</h3><p>Some exchanges periodically offer <strong>0% commission</strong> on selected markets. Matchbook and Betdaq run these promotions regularly. Taking advantage of these windows can significantly reduce costs, especially for high-turnover strategies.</p><h3>5. Avoid Unnecessary Trading</h3><p>Every trade generates commission. If you enter and exit positions too frequently, commission can erode your edge. Before making a trade, ask:</p><ul><li>Is the expected profit large enough to justify the commission cost?</li><li>Would a single back or lay position achieve the same objective with less turnover?</li><li>Am I trading for value or for entertainment?</li></ul><h3>6. Consider Liquidity Before Commission</h3><p>A lower commission rate is worthless if there is no liquidity to match your bets. Before choosing an exchange based solely on commission, check the available depth on the markets you actually use.</p><p>A 5% commission on a deeply liquid market may be better than 2% on a market where you cannot get matched at competitive prices.</p><h3>7. Track Effective Commission</h3><p>Serious exchange users track their effective commission rate over time. This is calculated as:</p><p><strong>Effective commission = Total commission paid ÷ Total gross profit</strong></p><p>If your effective rate is rising, you may be overtrading, using the wrong exchange, or missing available discounts.</p><h2>Worked Example: Same Strategy, Different Exchanges</h2><p>Assume you place 500 bets per year with an average stake of ₦100. Your average winning odds are 2.20. Your strike rate is 50%. Your gross profit before commission is ₦6,000 per year.</p><p><strong>At 5% commission (Betfair base rate):</strong><br>Commission = ₦6,000 × 0.05 = ₦300.<br>Net profit = ₦5,700.</p><p><strong>At 2% commission (Smarkets flat rate):</strong><br>Commission = ₦6,000 × 0.02 = ₦120.<br>Net profit = ₦5,880.</p><p><strong>At 0% commission (promotional period):</strong><br>Commission = ₦0.<br>Net profit = ₦6,000.</p><p>The difference between 5% and 2% is ₦180 per year on this modest volume. For a trader doing 10x this volume, the difference is ₦1,800 — substantial enough to justify switching platforms or earning a discount.</p><h2>Common Mistakes</h2><ul><li><strong>Ignoring commission entirely</strong> — Treating gross odds as real odds. Every winning bet is reduced by commission.</li><li><strong>Choosing an exchange for low commission but ignoring liquidity</strong> — A cheap platform with no money available is useless.</li><li><strong>Overtrading on thin margins</strong> — Frequent small profits can be entirely consumed by commission.</li><li><strong>Not tracking effective commission</strong> — You cannot optimise what you do not measure.</li><li><strong>Misunderstanding Premium Charge</strong> — High winners on Betfair may face additional charges that change the cost calculation completely.</li><li><strong>Assuming all markets have the same commission</strong> — Some exchanges charge different rates for different sports or market types.</li></ul><h2>Summary</h2><p>Commission is the silent cost of exchange betting. It does not appear in the odds, but it reduces every winning bet and every profitable trade. Optimising commission involves:</p><ul><li>Choosing the right exchange for your volume and markets.</li><li>Earning discount rates where available.</li><li>Netting off winnings within markets to avoid paying commission on offsetting bets.</li><li>Using commission-free promotions strategically.</li><li>Avoiding excessive trading that generates commission without meaningful added value.</li><li>Tracking your effective commission rate as a key performance indicator.</li></ul><p>The best exchange traders treat commission like a cost of doing business. They know their rate, they plan around it, and they make sure every trade clears the commission hurdle before it is placed. In the long run, a 1–2% saving on commission compounds into a massive difference in profitability.</p></h2>

<h2>Key Takeaway</h2>
<p><strong>Commission Optimisation on Exchanges:</strong> Always convert offered odds into de-vigged fair probability, compare across bookmakers, and measure your decisions against the sharp closing line.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'commission-optimisation-exchanges'
  AND course_id = (SELECT id FROM public.courses WHERE slug = 'exchange-betting-lay-markets');

-- [building-your-own-lines] pricing-non-standard-markets
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<h2><h1>Pricing Non-Standard Markets</h1><p>Most modelling effort focuses on mainstream markets: 1X2, over/under 2.5 goals, Asian handicaps, and point spreads. These markets are liquid, heavily traded, and relatively efficient. Edges exist but are harder to find and smaller in magnitude.</p><p>Non-standard markets — also called props, derivatives, or exotics — offer a different opportunity. They are less efficient because bookmakers spend less time and fewer resources pricing them, and sharp money is less concentrated. But they also require a different approach to pricing. You cannot simply reuse your standard match model and expect it to work.</p><p>Pricing non-standard markets means understanding the underlying event, building a probability estimate from first principles or from related markets, and comparing it to the available price. The skills are transferable, but the execution is distinct.</p><h2>1. What Counts as a Non-Standard Market?</h2><p>Non-standard markets vary by sport, but in football they include:</p><ul><li><strong>Both teams to score (BTTS)</strong> — yes/no</li><li><strong>Team totals</strong> — over/under goals for one specific team</li><li><strong>Exact score</strong> — a specific final scoreline</li><li><strong>Correct score group</strong> — e.g., home win by 1 goal, draw, away win by 2+</li><li><strong>Half-time/full-time</strong> — result at half-time combined with full-time result</li><li><strong>First goalscorer / anytime goalscorer</strong> — player-level markets</li><li><strong>Corners, cards, and fouls</strong> — match event totals and handicaps</li><li><strong>Shots and shots on target</strong> — player and team shot props</li><li><strong>Time of first goal</strong> — before/after a specific minute</li><li><strong>Win to nil</strong> — team wins without conceding</li><li><strong>Both halves over/under</strong> — scoring in each half individually</li></ul><p>These markets share a common characteristic: the underlying probability is often derived from the same match model but requires additional assumptions, dependencies, or data.</p><h2>2. Why Non-Standard Markets Are Less Efficient</h2><p>Several structural factors make props and derivatives more exploitable than main markets.</p><ul><li><strong>Lower liquidity</strong> — fewer sharp participants, less money to correct mispricings</li><li><strong>Simpler pricing models</strong> — bookmakers often use generic formulas or copy other books</li><li><strong>Less public scrutiny</strong> — most bettors focus on main lines, so errors persist longer</li><li><strong>Higher margins</strong> — bookmakers protect themselves with larger overrounds, but this can create mispriced selections within the market</li><li><strong>Slower updates</strong> — team news and market moves are incorporated into main lines first; props lag behind</li></ul><p>However, lower efficiency does not guarantee easy money. The higher bookmaker margin must be overcome, and your model must still beat the price. Non-standard markets reward sharper analysis because the baseline is softer, not because the betting is effortless.</p><h2>3. Pricing Approaches for Non-Standard Markets</h2><p>How you price a non-standard market depends on the type of event and the data available. Several approaches work in practice.</p><h3>Approach 1 — Derive from Your Main Match Model</h3><p>If you already have a goal-based model that produces expected goals for each team, many derivative markets follow directly.</p><ul><li><strong>BTTS</strong> — probability that both teams score at least one goal, calculated from the joint goal distribution</li><li><strong>Team totals</strong> — marginal distribution of goals for one team</li><li><strong>Exact score</strong> — product of individual goal probabilities with low-scoring adjustment</li><li><strong>Win to nil</strong> — probability that one team scores at least once and the other scores zero</li><li><strong>Half-time/full-time</strong> — requires a model for first-half and second-half scoring rates</li></ul><p>This approach is efficient because it reuses existing infrastructure. The limitation is that your main model may not capture dependencies that matter for derivatives — scoring rates change with game state, and first-half dynamics differ from full-match dynamics.</p><h3>Approach 2 — Build a Separate Event Model</h3><p>Some non-standard markets are not well described by goals alone. Corners, cards, and shots have their own dynamics and require their own expected rates.</p><ul><li><strong>Corners</strong> — model expected corners as a function of team style, possession, territory, and match context</li><li><strong>Cards</strong> — model expected booking points based on referee tendencies, rivalry intensity, and tactical aggression</li><li><strong>Shots and shots on target</strong> — model shot volume and accuracy from attacking and defensive profiles</li></ul><p>These event models can follow the same Poisson framework as goals but with different baseline rates and predictors. The key is finding data that is reliable and consistently available.</p><h3>Approach 3 — Derive from Related Market Prices</h3><p>Sometimes the best information comes from other markets rather than from your own raw model.</p><ul><li><strong>Use the main market as a prior</strong> — if you trust the closing 1X2 and totals lines, derive derivative probabilities from them</li><li><strong>Compare across books</strong> — inconsistencies between books in related markets can reveal mispricings</li><li><strong>Use exchange prices</strong> — more liquid derivatives on exchanges can provide better baselines for correlation-based props</li></ul><p>This approach is especially useful when you lack a full statistical model for a niche market. Start from the market''s implied probabilities, adjust for factors you believe are mispriced, and look for value relative to the adjusted number.</p><h3>Approach 4 — Direct Empirical Pricing</h3><p>For markets with sufficient historical data, you can price props directly from observed frequencies rather than from a parametric model.</p><ul><li><strong>First goalscorer</strong> — use historical scoring rates, minutes played, and starting lineup probability</li><li><strong>Time of first goal</strong> — fit a distribution to historical time-to-first-goal data</li><li><strong>Correct score</strong> — use historical scoreline frequencies adjusted for team strength</li></ul><p>Empirical pricing is transparent and easy to update. The risk is sample size — rare events like exact scores or first goalscorer require large datasets to produce stable estimates.</p><h2>4. Key Challenges in Non-Standard Market Pricing</h2><p>Pricing these markets is harder than it appears. Several challenges recur.</p><h3>Dependencies and Correlations</h3><p>Many props are correlated with each other and with the match result. A team that wins 2–0 has a much higher probability of winning to nil than a team that wins 2–1. First goalscorer and correct score are linked. Ignoring correlations leads to inconsistent pricing.</p><p>Solution: build dependencies into the model structure. Use joint distributions where possible, or apply correlation adjustments based on historical data.</p><h3>Game State Effects</h3><p>Teams change behaviour depending on the score. A team that takes an early lead may defend deeper, reducing its own shot volume but increasing its opponent''s. A team chasing the game takes more risks, leading to more goals at both ends.</p><p>Static models that assume constant scoring rates miss these dynamics. For in-play props and second-half markets, game state adjustments are essential.</p><h3>Team News and Lineups</h3><p>Player-level markets depend heavily on who starts. A first goalscorer price changes dramatically if the starting striker is rested. Shot props shift if a key playmaker is absent.</p><p>Non-standard markets often lag main markets in incorporating team news. That lag creates opportunity — but only if you can act before the price corrects.</p><h3>Bookmaker Margin Structure</h3><p>Exotic markets carry higher margins. A market with 110% overround requires a larger edge to overcome than one with 102%. Always calculate the implied probability and adjust for the specific margin structure of the market you are betting.</p><p>Do not compare raw prices across markets with different margin profiles without normalising.</p><h3>Low Liquidity and Limits</h3><p>Many non-standard markets have low maximum stakes. Even if you find a genuine edge, you may not be able to bet enough to make it meaningful. This is a practical constraint that must be factored into your strategy.</p><p>Low liquidity also means prices move quickly when smart money arrives. The window for exploitation may be short.</p><h2>5. Building a Non-Standard Market Pricing System</h2><p>If you want to price props systematically, follow a structured workflow.</p><p><strong>Step 1 — Identify target markets</strong></p><p>Focus on markets where you have data, understanding, and access to competitive prices. BTTS, team totals, and select player props are good starting points.</p><p><strong>Step 2 — Build the base model</strong></p><p>Use expected goals or a similar foundation. Ensure your base model is well-calibrated for main markets before extending it.</p><p><strong>Step 3 — Extend to derivatives</strong></p><p>Calculate derivative probabilities from the base model. For markets where the base model does not apply directly, build separate event models or use empirical baselines.</p><p><strong>Step 4 — Apply context adjustments</strong></p><p>Adjust for lineups, motivation, weather, and game state where relevant. Document every adjustment.</p><p><strong>Step 5 — Compare to available odds</strong></p><p>Convert your probabilities to fair odds. Compare against multiple books and exchanges. Account for margin differences.</p><p><strong>Step 6 — Track and validate</strong></p><p>Track CLV, calibration, and ROI separately for each market type. Non-standard markets may validate differently from your main markets. Some will fail. Some will surprise you.</p><p><strong>Step 7 — Iterate</strong></p><p>Refine the models that show promise. Drop or overhaul the ones that do not. Keep the process transparent and reproducible.</p><h2>6. Practical Considerations</h2><ul><li><strong>Start with markets where your main model already has a signal</strong> — BTTS and team totals are natural extensions of goal-based ratings</li><li><strong>Be selective</strong> — a few well-priced props are better than many poorly priced ones</li><li><strong>Respect the limits</strong> — low maximum stakes mean you need multiple books and accounts to scale</li><li><strong>Watch for correlated errors</strong> — if your main model is wrong, your derivatives will be wrong too</li><li><strong>Use sharp books as a reference</strong> — their derivative prices are better than soft books, even if still imperfect</li><li><strong>Paper trade first</strong> — validate non-standard market pricing before risking real money</li></ul><h2>Summary</h2><p>Non-standard markets offer real opportunity because they are priced less efficiently than main lines. But that opportunity is only accessible if you can price them accurately.</p><p>The core principles:</p><ul><li><strong>Start from a sound base model</strong> — expected goals or a similar foundation</li><li><strong>Derive derivatives where possible</strong> — many props follow directly from goal distributions</li><li><strong>Build separate event models where needed</strong> — corners, cards, shots, and player markets require their own data</li><li><strong>Account for dependencies and game state</strong> — independent assumptions will misprice correlated markets</li><li><strong>Adjust for context</strong> — lineups, rest, motivation, and weather matter as much in props as in main markets</li><li><strong>Validate each market type separately</strong> — what works for BTTS may not work for cards</li><li><strong>Respect margins and limits</strong> — the overround is higher and the maximum stake is lower</li></ul><p>Pricing non-standard markets is not a shortcut. It is a specialised skill built on the same foundation as main-market modelling: probability, discipline, and constant comparison with the market. The difference is that the market is softer, the edges are wider, and the competition is less fierce. For the modeller willing to do the work, that is an attractive combination.</p></h2>

<h2>Real-Data Benchmark: Bookmaker Overround &amp; Closing Line Efficiency</h2>
<p>In PunterStat''s historical odds dataset (<strong>157,752 Pinnacle closing lines</strong> across 39 football leagues), market margin varies sharply between sharp exchanges/bookmakers and soft retail sportsbooks:</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Bookmaker / Market Tier</th><th>Typical 1X2 Overround</th><th>Fair 50/50 Price Offered</th><th>Long-Run Drag on ₦100,000 Staked</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Pinnacle / Betting Exchange (Sharp)</td><td>2.0% – 2.8%</td><td>1.95 – 1.96</td><td>&minus;₦2,200 to &minus;₦2,600</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Tier-1 European / Global Retail</td><td>4.5% – 6.0%</td><td>1.88 – 1.91</td><td>&minus;₦4,800 to &minus;₦5,800</td></tr>
    <tr><td>High-Margin Retail / Multi-Market Props</td><td>7.0% – 11.5%</td><td>1.80 – 1.85</td><td>&minus;₦7,000 to &minus;₦10,500</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Worked Example:</strong> On a Premier League 1X2 market priced at Home <code>2.10</code> (47.62%), Draw <code>3.40</code> (29.41%), Away <code>3.60</code> (27.78%), the summed implied probability is <strong>104.81%</strong> — a <strong>4.81% overround</strong>. Dividing each implied probability by <code>1.0481</code> reveals the fair no-vig probabilities: Home <strong>45.43%</strong> (fair odds <code>2.20</code>), Draw <strong>28.06%</strong> (<code>3.56</code>), Away <strong>26.51%</strong> (<code>3.77</code>). Every time you accept <code>2.10</code> on a true <code>2.20</code> outcome, you surrender 4.5% expected value before kick-off.</p>

<h2>Key Takeaway</h2>
<p><strong>Pricing Non-Standard Markets:</strong> Always convert offered odds into de-vigged fair probability, compare across bookmakers, and measure your decisions against the sharp closing line.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'pricing-non-standard-markets'
  AND course_id = (SELECT id FROM public.courses WHERE slug = 'building-your-own-lines');

-- [cognitive-biases-in-betting] availability-heuristic-betting
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<h2>When Vivid Memories Distort Probability</h2>
<p>The availability heuristic is the tendency to estimate the probability of an event based on how easily examples come to mind. Events that are vivid, recent, or emotionally charged are mentally "available" and therefore perceived as more probable than they actually are.</p>
<h2>Betting Examples</h2>
<p><strong>The memorable upset:</strong> Brazil lost 7-1 to Germany in the 2014 World Cup. This vivid, shocking result is mentally available to everyone who saw it. Bettors who experienced this event may systematically overestimate the probability of historic upsets when Brazil plays strong opposition — even 10 years later.</p>
<p><strong>The spectacular goal:</strong> A striker scores a spectacular 35-yard volley in a broadcast match. This goal is memorable and mentally available. His anytime scorer price is affected by public memory of spectacular goals disproportionately to his underlying xG rate.</p>
<p><strong>The dramatic late goal:</strong> You personally watched a match where a 94th-minute goal overturned a winning position. This experience makes late goals feel more probable in future matches you watch than the statistical base rate warrants.</p>
<h2>Why Availability Distorts Markets</h2>
<p>Public betting markets are influenced by what bettors think about — not just what the statistics show. The availability heuristic systematically inflates prices on memorable outcomes (high-profile upsets, famous strikers, dramatic comebacks) and deflates prices on statistically probable but unremarkable outcomes (the quiet 2-0 win by the deserving favourite).</p>
<h2>The Systematic Correction</h2>
<p>Keep a probability calibration log: for each category of bet (e.g. "favourite wins by 2+ goals"), track your estimated probability vs actual frequency. If your estimated frequency is consistently above actual frequency for a category that includes vivid counterexamples, availability bias is likely at work.</p>
<p>The correction is mechanical: return to base rates and model outputs. The vivid exceptions are exceptions — their vivid quality does not make them typical.</p>

<h2>Real-Data Pattern: How Loss-Chasing ("Tilt") Destroys Long-Run ROI</h2>
<p>Behavioral analysis across tracked bet journals (and PunterStat''s Empirical Bayes <strong>Tilt Risk Model</strong>) reveals a stark divergence between disciplined flat-stake sessions and post-loss chasing sessions:</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Behavioral State</th><th>Typical Stake vs Median</th><th>Average Leg Count</th><th>Typical Realized ROI</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Pre-planned disciplined bet (rested)</td><td>1.0&times; median stake</td><td>1 – 2 legs</td><td>Near fair (&minus;3% to +4%)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Post-loss bet within 2 hours of a 2+ loss streak</td><td><strong>2.2&times; – 3.5&times; median stake</strong></td><td>3 – 6 legs (chasing payout)</td><td><strong>&minus;24% to &minus;42%</strong></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Late-night / unfamiliar-league impulse bet</td><td>1.5&times; – 2.5&times; median stake</td><td>4+ legs</td><td><strong>&minus;30%+</strong></td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Actionable Defense:</strong> Pre-commit your maximum daily stake (e.g. 3% of bankroll) and enforce a mandatory <strong>24-hour cooling-off rule</strong> after 3 consecutive losses. Log every slip in PunterStat''s <strong>Bet Tracker</strong> so your Report Card exposes tilt patterns objectively before they drain your bankroll.</p>

<h2>Key Takeaway</h2>
<p><strong>Availability Heuristic and Memorable Events:</strong> Separate decision quality from single-bet outcomes, enforce pre-committed stake limits, and rely on logged journal data rather than gut instinct.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'availability-heuristic-betting'
  AND course_id = (SELECT id FROM public.courses WHERE slug = 'cognitive-biases-in-betting');

-- [discipline-and-record-keeping] structuring-your-betting-day
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<h2>Time as a Competitive Resource</h2>
<p>Disciplined bettors treat time as a finite, precious resource. The hours available for analysis, bet monitoring, and review must be allocated deliberately to the activities that produce the most value.</p>
<h2>The High-Value Activities</h2>
<p><strong>Model runs and analysis (highest value):</strong> Running your selection model, comparing output to available market prices, identifying value bets. This is where edge is created.</p>
<p><strong>Record-keeping and CLV review (high value):</strong> Logging bets, recording closing prices, updating the performance dashboard. This is where learning occurs.</p>
<p><strong>Account management (medium value):</strong> Balance monitoring, float management, withdrawal processing. Operationally necessary but creates no edge.</p>
<p><strong>Watching events (variable value):</strong> Watching a match you have bet on creates emotional investment but rarely produces actionable insight. Watching for live betting analysis is high-value; watching to "support" your selection is zero-value and potentially harmful.</p>
<h2>A Structured Betting Day Schedule</h2>
<p><strong>Morning (30–45 min):</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Check overnight results and update the bet log</li>
<li>Log any closing prices from overnight events</li>
<li>Review the day''s fixture list for your target leagues</li>
<li>Run the model on today''s qualifying fixtures</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Afternoon (20–30 min):</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Compare model output to opened market prices</li>
<li>Identify value selections, calculate stakes</li>
<li>Place bets for evening fixtures</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Evening (20–30 min):</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Monitor for significant line movement on placed bets (not anxious watching — analytical)</li>
<li>Check confirmed lineups and adjust if needed</li>
<li>After events: log results</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Weekly (1–2 hours):</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Full performance dashboard review</li>
<li>Closing price record for all weekly bets (CLV calculation)</li>
<li>Account balance reconciliation</li>
</ul>
<h2>The Time-Limited Bettor</h2>
<p>For part-time bettors with 5–10 hours per week: allocate 50% to analysis, 30% to review, 20% to operations. Quality of analysis matters more than volume of bets.</p>

