-- [sample-size-and-variance] poisson-distribution-sports
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<h2>Why Poisson Fits Rare Events</h2>
<p>The Poisson distribution models the probability of a given number of events occurring in a fixed interval, when events are rare, independent, and random. Football goals fit these criteria surprisingly well.</p>
<h2>The Poisson Formula</h2>
<p>P(X = k) = (e^(−λ) × λ^k) / k!</p>
<p>Where:</p>
<ul>
<li>λ = expected number of goals (e.g. 1.5 for a team in a match)</li>
<li>k = the number of goals you want the probability for</li>
<li>e ≈ 2.718</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> Team A expected goals = 1.5. What is the probability they score exactly 2?</p>
<p>P(X = 2) = (e^(−1.5) × 1.5²) / 2! = (0.2231 × 2.25) / 2 = 0.2510 = 25.1%</p>
<h2>Building a Full Goals Distribution</h2>
<p>For a match where Team A expects 1.3 goals and Team B expects 1.1 goals:</p>
<p>Compute P(A scores 0, 1, 2, 3, 4+) using Poisson with λ = 1.3 Compute P(B scores 0, 1, 2, 3, 4+) using Poisson with λ = 1.1</p>
<p>For every score combination (A=0, B=0), (A=0, B=1), ..., multiply the independent probabilities: P(A=1, B=0) = P(A scores 1) × P(B scores 0)</p>
<p>Sum all score combinations where A > B for home win probability. Sum all A < B for away win. Sum all A = B for draw.</p>
<h2>The Limitations of Poisson</h2>
<ul>
<li>Poisson assumes goals are independent — but a goal changes match dynamics (teams often park the bus or push forward), which violates the independence assumption</li>
<li>Dixon-Coles correction adjusts for the underestimation of 0-0 and 1-0/0-1 scorelines</li>
<li>For extended coverage, use bivariate Poisson which captures goal correlation between teams</li>
</ul>

<h2>Real-Data Benchmark: Sample Size, Variance &amp; Walk-Forward Stability</h2>
<p>Short-term win rates fool almost every bettor. Even on a fair 50/50 proposition (<code>2.00</code> odds), the 95% confidence interval around your observed win rate shrinks only with the <em>square root</em> of sample size (<code>&radic;n</code>):</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Settled Bets (n)</th><th>95% Win-Rate Confidence Band (at True 50%)</th><th>95% ROI Noise Band at 2.00 Odds</th><th>Statistical Verdict</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>25 bets</td><td>30.4% – 69.6%</td><td>&minus;39.2% to +39.2%</td><td>Pure noise — impossible to judge skill</td></tr>
    <tr><td>100 bets</td><td>40.2% – 59.8%</td><td>&minus;19.6% to +19.6%</td><td>Early directional signal only</td></tr>
    <tr><td>500 bets</td><td>45.6% – 54.4%</td><td>&minus;8.8% to +8.8%</td><td>Meaningful performance evaluation</td></tr>
    <tr><td>1,000 bets</td><td>46.9% – 53.1%</td><td>&minus;6.2% to +6.2%</td><td>Reliable statistical proof of edge</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Out-of-Sample Proof (PunterStat v2.0.0):</strong> Across 3 strict walk-forward seasons (never peeking into future matches), PunterStat''s model held steady Brier scores of <code>0.60460</code> (2022/23), <code>0.60983</code> (2023/24), and <code>0.60887</code> (2024/25+) — proving that true statistical validation requires out-of-sample testing over thousands of events, never cherry-picked hot streaks.</p>

<h2>Key Takeaway</h2>
<p><strong>The Poisson Distribution in Sports Modelling:</strong> Demand large out-of-sample evidence, respect regression to the mean, and calibrate probabilities against objective scoring rules like Brier score and CLV.</p>'
