-- [mental-models-betting-uncertainty] uncertainty-vs-risk-knightian
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<h2>Two Types of the Unknown</h2>
<p>Economist Frank Knight distinguished between risk (unknown outcome, but knowable probability) and uncertainty (unknown outcome, unknowable probability). This distinction has profound implications for betting.</p>
<p><strong>Risk:</strong> The probability of a fair coin landing heads is exactly 50%. The outcome is unknown but the probability is mathematically certain. Most standard betting scenarios are primarily risk — we can estimate probabilities from historical data.</p>
<p><strong>Uncertainty (ambiguity):</strong> The probability that a specific economic policy will cause a recession in 3 years is genuinely unknown — not just unknown to you but fundamentally unknowable. There is no historical distribution to reference.</p>
<h2>Where Betting Is Risk vs Uncertainty</h2>
<p><strong>Risk-dominated situations (probability estimable):</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Match winner in a league with 10 seasons of data at the same competitive level</li>
<li>Total goals in a well-studied competition</li>
<li>Player performance in a stable team context</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Uncertainty-dominated situations (probability less estimable):</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Match outcome for a newly promoted team in their first season in the higher division</li>
<li>Tournament winner after major squad rebuilding</li>
<li>Player performance after a serious injury return</li>
</ul>
<h2>The Practical Implication</h2>
<p>In risk situations: quantitative models are reliable. Bet with appropriate Kelly fraction.</p>
<p>In uncertainty situations: quantitative models are less reliable. Reduce stake size below what Kelly would suggest (because the Kelly calculation assumes accurate probability estimates). Apply wider confidence intervals.</p>
<h2>Recognising the Uncertainty Premium</h2>
<p>In uncertainty situations, bookmakers must include an uncertainty premium in the margin — they know their model is less accurate. This means both your estimate and the bookmaker''s estimate may be far from the true probability. Caution is warranted.</p>

<h2>Real-Data Pattern: How Loss-Chasing ("Tilt") Destroys Long-Run ROI</h2>
<p>Behavioral analysis across tracked bet journals (and PunterStat''s Empirical Bayes <strong>Tilt Risk Model</strong>) reveals a stark divergence between disciplined flat-stake sessions and post-loss chasing sessions:</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Behavioral State</th><th>Typical Stake vs Median</th><th>Average Leg Count</th><th>Typical Realized ROI</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Pre-planned disciplined bet (rested)</td><td>1.0&times; median stake</td><td>1 – 2 legs</td><td>Near fair (&minus;3% to +4%)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Post-loss bet within 2 hours of a 2+ loss streak</td><td><strong>2.2&times; – 3.5&times; median stake</strong></td><td>3 – 6 legs (chasing payout)</td><td><strong>&minus;24% to &minus;42%</strong></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Late-night / unfamiliar-league impulse bet</td><td>1.5&times; – 2.5&times; median stake</td><td>4+ legs</td><td><strong>&minus;30%+</strong></td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Actionable Defense:</strong> Pre-commit your maximum daily stake (e.g. 3% of bankroll) and enforce a mandatory <strong>24-hour cooling-off rule</strong> after 3 consecutive losses. Log every slip in PunterStat''s <strong>Bet Tracker</strong> so your Report Card exposes tilt patterns objectively before they drain your bankroll.</p>

<h2>Key Takeaway</h2>
<p><strong>Uncertainty vs Risk: The Knightian Distinction:</strong> Separate decision quality from single-bet outcomes, enforce pre-committed stake limits, and rely on logged journal data rather than gut instinct.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'uncertainty-vs-risk-knightian'
  AND course_id = (SELECT id FROM public.courses WHERE slug = 'mental-models-betting-uncertainty');

-- [handicap-and-asian-handicap] modelling-ah-probability-distributions
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<h2>Understanding Modelling AH Probability Distributions</h2>
<p>Mastering <strong>Modelling AH Probability Distributions</strong> is an essential step for any bettor transitioning from recreational play to professional, data-driven investing. In the modern sports betting ecosystem, markets are highly efficient, meaning that an edge can only be found by thoroughly understanding the specific mechanics of the bet type. A common mistake made by recreational punters is failing to track closing line value (CLV). If your bets consistently beat the closing odds at sharp bookmakers, you hold a tangible edge, regardless of short-term variance. This principle is heavily applicable here, where pricing efficiency can fluctuate wildly before kickoff.</p>
<p>When we look at the historical data, particularly drawing from the extensive FDCO CSV databases covering top European leagues since 1993/94, clear patterns emerge. Market makers price these outcomes based on established baselines. Your objective is to identify when those baselines fail to account for unique situational variables.</p>
<h2>Core Mechanics and Mathematical Realities</h2>
<p>The fundamental structure of this market relies on converting true statistical probability into implied odds, with the bookmaker''s margin baked in. Asian Handicap markets operate on a 2-2.5% margin at Pinnacle, compared to 5-8% at soft books, making them highly efficient. Understanding this structural reality changes how you should approach your staking methodology.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Implied Probability:</strong> The true odds minus the operator''s vigorish.</li>
<li><strong>Market Efficiency:</strong> How quickly sharp money moves the line to its true price.</li>
<li><strong>Variance Modeling:</strong> Understanding the expected downswings inherent in the bet type.</li>
</ul>
<p>Understanding variance is critical. Even with a proven edge, drawdowns are inevitable. Proper bankroll management, typically a fractional Kelly Criterion approach, ensures that you can weather these downswings without risking ruin. This is particularly vital when operating in markets with inherently higher volatility.</p>
<h2>Strategic Execution in the Market</h2>
<p>Executing a profitable strategy requires a deep understanding of market timing. Prices are dynamic. Modeling AH probability distributions often utilizes a modified Poisson approach adjusted for zero-inflation in lower scoring leagues. If you are operating at soft books, you face higher margins but slower line adjustments. Conversely, sharp exchanges offer better prices but hyper-efficient lines.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Market Scenario</th><th>Typical Overround</th><th>Bettor Edge Required</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Early Sharp Line (e.g. Pinnacle)</td><td>2.0% - 2.5%</td><td>High structural knowledge</td></tr>
<tr><td>Recreational Bookmaker</td><td>5.0% - 8.0%</td><td>Exploiting slow adjustments</td></tr>
<tr><td>Betting Exchange</td><td>4.5% - 5.0% (Comm)</td><td>Market making / Arbitrage</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Another key consideration is price origination. Rather than simply evaluating whether the offered odds ''look good'', you must build a model that outputs a true price. If your model outputs 2.00 (50%) and the market offers 2.20 (45.4%), you execute the bet. A common mistake made by recreational punters is failing to track closing line value (CLV). If your bets consistently beat the closing odds at sharp bookmakers, you hold a tangible edge, regardless of short-term variance. This principle is heavily applicable here, where pricing efficiency can fluctuate wildly before kickoff.</p>
<h2>Advanced Considerations and Pitfalls</h2>
<p>One of the most significant pitfalls for bettors is confirmation bias—finding a narrative and cherry-picking stats to fit it. The bookmaker''s algorithm does not care about narratives; it strictly processes liability and historical data. The +0.5 Asian Handicap is mathematically identical to a Double Chance (Win/Draw) market, often presenting arbitrage if misaligned.</p>
<p>Furthermore, bankroll management cannot be overstated. Operating in this market without a strict fractional Kelly staking plan guarantees ruin over an infinite timeline. To approach this market systematically, bettors must divorce themselves from the entertainment value of the sport and focus strictly on the underlying math. The primary objective is identifying discrepancies between the bookmaker''s implied probability and the true probability of the event. Over a large sample size, these small mathematical advantages compound into significant ROI.</p>
<h2>Key Takeaway</h2>
<p><strong>Never ignore the math underpinning Modelling AH Probability Distributions. Modeling AH probability distributions often utilizes a modified Poisson approach adjusted for zero-inflation in lower scoring leagues. Every decimal point of value secured against the closing line contributes directly to long-term yield.</strong></p>'
WHERE slug = 'modelling-ah-probability-distributions'
  AND course_id = (SELECT id FROM public.courses WHERE slug = 'handicap-and-asian-handicap');

-- [odds-comparison-line-shopping] selective-aggression-bet-sizing
UPDATE public.lessons
SET content = '<h2><h1>Selective Aggression: When to Bet Bigger</h1><h2>Introduction</h2><p>Most bankroll systems recommend a fixed unit size (commonly 0.5–2% of bankroll) for every bet. That baseline is correct for the majority of wagers. Selective aggression is the disciplined exception: deliberately increasing stake size on a small subset of bets that meet stricter criteria.</p><p>Done correctly, it improves long-term growth without abandoning risk control. Done emotionally or too frequently, it simply amplifies variance and drawdowns.</p><h2>The Core Principle</h2><p>Bet bigger only when the combination of edge and confidence is meaningfully higher than your normal threshold. The increase should be pre-defined, limited in frequency, and still expressed in units of the current bankroll—not in arbitrary dollar amounts.</p><p>Selective aggression is not "feeling good about a game." It is a rules-based decision to allocate more capital to higher-quality opportunities.</p><h2>When Bigger Bets Are Justified</h2><p>Increase stake size only when several of the following conditions are present:</p><ol><li><strong>Clear, quantifiable edge</strong><br>Your estimated probability meaningfully exceeds the implied probability of the best available odds (after accounting for the overround). Larger edges support larger stakes.</li><li><strong>Strong closing-line value expectation</strong><br>You have reason to believe the current price is soft relative to where the market is likely to close. Consistent positive CLV is one of the strongest justifications for aggression.</li><li><strong>High information quality</strong><br>You possess a specific, well-supported edge (injury news, market inefficiency, model discrepancy, or situational factor) that is not yet fully reflected in the price.</li><li><strong>Lower relative variance (optional but helpful)</strong><br>Straight bets at moderate odds are generally safer candidates for larger stakes than high-odds props, parlays, or highly volatile markets.</li><li><strong>Process confirmation</strong><br>Recent tracking shows your overall approach is profitable and your CLV remains positive. Aggression during a cold process is usually counterproductive.</li></ol><p>If only one or two of these factors are present, stay at standard unit size.</p><h2>How Much Bigger?</h2><p>Keep the increase modest and capped.</p><p>Common frameworks:</p><ul><li>Standard bet: 1 unit</li><li>Elevated bet: 1.5–2 units</li><li>Maximum bet (rare): 2.5–3 units</li></ul><p>Never exceed a hard ceiling (e.g., 3% of current bankroll) regardless of perceived edge. The goal is controlled asymmetry, not all-in behavior.</p><p>Define the tiers in advance and write them down. Emotional "this is the one" decisions should not override the pre-set limits.</p><h2>Practical Implementation Rules</h2><ul><li>Decide the stake size <strong>before</strong> looking at the result of recent bets.</li><li>Record the reason for every elevated stake in your tracker (edge estimate, CLV expectation, specific information).</li><li>Limit the percentage of total bets that receive elevated stakes (many disciplined bettors keep this under 10–20%).</li><li>Apply the same stop-loss and daily risk limits to the overall portfolio—larger individual bets still count fully against those caps.</li><li>Review elevated bets as a separate sample periodically. If they underperform your standard bets on a risk-adjusted basis, tighten the criteria.</li></ul><h2>What Selective Aggression Is Not</h2><ul><li>Increasing stakes after a losing streak to "get even."</li><li>Betting bigger because a team is popular or the narrative is compelling.</li><li>Raising unit size across the board after a winning run.</li><li>Using larger stakes on long-shot props or multi-leg parlays as a form of aggression.</li><li>Ignoring bankroll percentage limits because "the edge is huge."</li></ul><p>These behaviors are simply poor bankroll management dressed up as confidence.</p><h2>Relationship to Bankroll Growth and Recovery</h2><p>During normal growth phases, selective aggression can modestly accelerate compounding when true high-edge spots appear.</p><p>During drawdowns, most bettors should reduce or eliminate elevated stakes until process metrics and bankroll stability improve. Aggression is a tool for exploiting advantage, not for forcing recovery.</p><h2>Real-Data Benchmark: Bookmaker Overround &amp; Closing Line Efficiency</h2>
<p>In PunterStat''s historical odds dataset (<strong>157,752 Pinnacle closing lines</strong> across 39 football leagues), market margin varies sharply between sharp exchanges/bookmakers and soft retail sportsbooks:</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Bookmaker / Market Tier</th><th>Typical 1X2 Overround</th><th>Fair 50/50 Price Offered</th><th>Long-Run Drag on ₦100,000 Staked</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Pinnacle / Betting Exchange (Sharp)</td><td>2.0% – 2.8%</td><td>1.95 – 1.96</td><td>&minus;₦2,200 to &minus;₦2,600</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Tier-1 European / Global Retail</td><td>4.5% – 6.0%</td><td>1.88 – 1.91</td><td>&minus;₦4,800 to &minus;₦5,800</td></tr>
    <tr><td>High-Margin Retail / Multi-Market Props</td><td>7.0% – 11.5%</td><td>1.80 – 1.85</td><td>&minus;₦7,000 to &minus;₦10,500</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Worked Example:</strong> On a Premier League 1X2 market priced at Home <code>2.10</code> (47.62%), Draw <code>3.40</code> (29.41%), Away <code>3.60</code> (27.78%), the summed implied probability is <strong>104.81%</strong> — a <strong>4.81% overround</strong>. Dividing each implied probability by <code>1.0481</code> reveals the fair no-vig probabilities: Home <strong>45.43%</strong> (fair odds <code>2.20</code>), Draw <strong>28.06%</strong> (<code>3.56</code>), Away <strong>26.51%</strong> (<code>3.77</code>). Every time you accept <code>2.10</code> on a true <code>2.20</code> outcome, you surrender 4.5% expected value before kick-off.</p>

<h2>Key Takeaway</h2><p>Selective aggression means betting bigger only on a small minority of opportunities that clear a higher bar for edge, price quality, and information strength. The increase should be modest, pre-defined, and still expressed as a percentage of current bankroll.</p><p>Used sparingly and systematically, it improves capital efficiency. Used frequently or emotionally, it increases the chance of large drawdowns. The discipline lies in the selectivity: most bets stay at standard size so that the occasional larger bet remains meaningful and controlled.</p></h2>'
WHERE slug = 'selective-aggression-bet-sizing'
  AND course_id = (SELECT id FROM public.courses WHERE slug = 'odds-comparison-line-shopping');

-- [outright-and-futures-betting] in-season-outright-updates
